WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:04.373
- Good afternoon.

00:00:04.373 --> 00:00:07.383
If I can start the public board.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.800
Let me first of all welcome
members of the public

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:15.273
who joined us to observe
the session today.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:17.310
And also let me welcome

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:20.400
a number of new non-executive directors,

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.680
for whom this is their
first board meeting.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:24.750
So Baroness Mary Watkins,

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:27.000
Professor Sir Simon Wessely

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.490
and Professor Sir Mark Walport,

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:30.323
who are, as I say,

00:00:30.323 --> 00:00:32.370
joining their first
private board this morning

00:00:32.370 --> 00:00:33.360
for the NHS England

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.320
and this first public
board this afternoon.

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:39.780
And we're delighted
that they have joined us

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:41.070
as board members.

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:44.880
Baroness Watkins is an
international expert in nursing

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.770
and in healthcare delivery.

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:49.590
She qualified as a general
nurse and a mental health nurse,

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:53.160
and has worked in community
inpatient mental health

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:54.960
and acute settings.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.400
She's a visiting professor

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.950
in the Florence Nightingale
Faculty of Nursing,

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:02.850
Midwifery and Palliative
Care, King's College London

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:05.430
and is president of the
Florence Nightingale Foundation.

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:09.480
And we are hugely pleased
that she has joined us.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:11.160
Professor Sir Simon Wessely is

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.320
Regius Professor of Psychiatry

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:15.060
at King's College London,

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:17.010
and has worked as a clinical psychiatrist

00:01:17.010 --> 00:01:19.230
specialising in general
hospital psychiatry

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:21.390
for the past 35 years.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:22.740
Held a number of leadership roles

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:24.870
and was president of the
Royal College of Psychiatrists

00:01:24.870 --> 00:01:27.060
prior to being the
first psychiatrist to be

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:30.003
elected president of the
Royal Society of Medicine.

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:32.130
Professor Wessely has served on

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:34.140
numerous national governmental committees

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:35.820
connected with military health,

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:37.230
emergency response,

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:40.200
population health and mental health.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.720
And Simon, we're delighted
that you've joined us.

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.610
And then Professor Sir Mark Walport

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:47.460
is Honorary Distinguished
Professor of Medicine

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:49.320
at Imperial College London

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.990
and Chair of Imperial
College Health Partners.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:54.150
His previous roles include as

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:57.450
Chief Executive of UK
Research and Innovation,

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.970
as government Chief Scientific Advisor,

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:01.980
as head of Government Office for Science

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:04.380
and as a director of the Wellcome Trust.

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:07.479
And this appointment also
builds on the NHS's role

00:02:07.479 --> 00:02:10.200
as a key partner of universities

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:12.210
and also the life sciences industry,

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:15.000
which clearly deliver vital
research and innovation.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:15.833
And Mark, again,

00:02:15.833 --> 00:02:18.183
we're delighted that
you have also joined us.

00:02:20.010 --> 00:02:23.733
If I turn to the agenda of today,

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:26.520
we are going to have an update on

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:29.910
the "Urgent and Emergency
Care Recovery Plan,"

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:31.680
which was published on Monday,

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.740
and had been worked on by NHS England

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:36.540
in collaboration with government

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:39.090
and is a clear blueprint
for recovering urgent

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:41.520
and emergency care services,

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.560
helping to reduce both waiting times

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.540
and to improve patient experience.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:48.600
And we'll go through that
in more detail later on.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:50.100
That is a prompt for me, actually,

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:51.600
to thank one of our colleagues

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.690
for whom this is her last board meeting,

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:55.770
Pauline Philip.

00:02:55.770 --> 00:02:58.590
And I'd just like to
pay a tribute to Pauline

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:00.120
for all the work that she has done,

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:04.320
I think since about 2014, but a long time.

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:07.350
So member of this board,

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:09.300
and you have provided, Pauline,

00:03:09.300 --> 00:03:10.770
an outstanding service, actually,

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:12.480
and thank you very much for the passion

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:15.810
with which you have led so much, actually,

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:18.810
of what NHS England has achieved.

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:19.890
If we're doing farewells,

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:21.870
and it's slightly out of order,

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:24.180
I'd also like to thank Mark Cubbon,

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:27.374
whom also this is, Mark,
your last board meeting.

00:03:27.374 --> 00:03:30.930
And, again, I've known
you for 10 months or so.

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:35.930
I am not amazed, actually,
it's the wrong word.

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:37.479
I compliment,

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:40.935
there's not a negative word coming, Mark.

00:03:40.935 --> 00:03:44.550
I think you are hugely impressive

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:47.040
in the way you lead
the executive functions

00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:49.980
and perhaps best exemplified,
in my experience,

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:51.180
of the work you've done leading, actually,

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:54.168
on the merger of a number of our entities

00:03:54.168 --> 00:03:58.200
as we create the new NHS England.

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:00.420
And thank you very much
for all that you have done.

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:01.980
And as we said in the private board,

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:05.193
I think Manchester's gain
is NHS England's loss, but.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:11.490
Not on the agenda today, but
I do want to reflect on it.

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:13.420
The long-term workforce plan

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:17.640
is in the very final stages of development

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:19.170
and it will underpin

00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:21.990
all of the transformation work that we do.

00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:24.940
We absolutely recognise
that a sustainable workforce

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:31.080
is required for us to be able
to deliver on our objectives.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:33.247
And, actually, many of the problems we see

00:04:33.247 --> 00:04:34.620
and we wrestle with as NHS England

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:37.290
tend to have a problem in terms of

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:39.960
do we have the right staff or
sufficient numbers of staff

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:40.793
in the right place.

00:04:40.793 --> 00:04:45.360
And so I think an ambitious
and sensibly bold plan

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:48.210
for a long-term workforce development

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:52.440
over the next five, 10
and 15 years is essential.

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:53.273
And as I say,

00:04:53.273 --> 00:04:55.743
that is now in the very
final stages of preparation.

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:02.154
We are going to hear in David
Sloman's update, I think,

00:05:02.154 --> 00:05:05.610
about, frankly, levels
of unprecedented demand

00:05:05.610 --> 00:05:07.920
that the NHS has been dealing with

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:12.180
over the past months and past weeks.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:13.800
And as I reflect on that, actually,

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:16.470
what's interesting is how
much of that demand comes from

00:05:16.470 --> 00:05:19.650
exogenous factors as opposed
to endogenous factors.

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:21.420
Either the endogenous factors from where

00:05:21.420 --> 00:05:24.690
parts of the system don't
operate as fully as we might like

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:26.817
and causes pressure
elsewhere in the system.

00:05:26.817 --> 00:05:29.610
And so working out and thinking hard about

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:33.480
exogenous sources of demand
versus endogenous, I think,

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:35.583
is an important reflection.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:40.110
The "Urgent Emergency Care
Plan" published on Monday,

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:42.480
the team is now hard at work

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:46.661
already starting on a
primary care recovery plan,

00:05:46.661 --> 00:05:49.500
and clearly working on
that with government.

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:52.560
Again, that will be a
very major piece of work

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:56.970
where NHS England is right at
the centre of that thinking.

00:05:56.970 --> 00:05:59.640
And if I look just very
quickly at the agenda,

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:01.050
in addition to an update

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:04.470
from Amanda and from Julia and from David,

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:08.250
I think there are four
very important topics.

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:09.083
One I've mentioned,

00:06:09.083 --> 00:06:12.240
which is the "Urgent and
Emergency Care Recovery Plan."

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:14.610
We're then looking at mental
health transformation,

00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:17.910
a really important, important
area for us to focus

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:20.070
and receive report upon.

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:21.210
We've got the delegation of

00:06:21.210 --> 00:06:23.970
specialised services commissioning.

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:24.930
It's an area, actually,

00:06:24.930 --> 00:06:27.510
which is a huge area of great complexity,

00:06:27.510 --> 00:06:29.580
perhaps little recognised or understood

00:06:29.580 --> 00:06:34.140
outside NHS England and
those who commission,

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:36.900
but a really important area.

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:37.733
And then lastly,

00:06:37.733 --> 00:06:40.830
an update on from the
transformation directorate

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:43.380
on Digital and Data and Technology,

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:46.770
and in particular, key work
areas underpinning strategy

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:48.930
over the next three years in that area.

00:06:48.930 --> 00:06:51.153
So all very good topics.

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:54.030
Lastly, at the private board this morning,

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:57.090
we had a very good conversation
which we are continuing,

00:06:57.090 --> 00:06:59.537
which is on strategy work.

00:06:59.537 --> 00:07:00.970
Where's Chris Hopson?

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:03.030
Led by Chris.

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:07.380
And we're going to maintain
clear focus on that.

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:09.480
But one of the areas we did
think about and talk about

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:11.970
was in spite of the very
good steps being taken

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:14.430
in the "Urgent Emergency Care Plan,"

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:17.580
which will make a real difference
as we deliver on those,

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:20.730
there is a question about
capacity and physical capacity,

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:21.960
and doing the work to look at

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:23.850
what would be the physical capacity that,

00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:25.110
frankly, we ought to have,

00:07:25.110 --> 00:07:27.420
looking at international
comparative systems

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:29.460
or that demand, growth or need

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:31.170
outside of what we've done on workforce,

00:07:31.170 --> 00:07:32.790
looking at long-term workforce plan,

00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:33.820
but what's the physical capacity,

00:07:33.820 --> 00:07:36.480
be it in beds or scanners, et cetera,

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:39.446
that we think NHS England should have

00:07:39.446 --> 00:07:44.190
in order to offer really
good patient care?

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:48.600
So let me finish introductory remarks.

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:49.590
I've gone on a little too long.

00:07:49.590 --> 00:07:50.940
Amanda, that will probably compress you.

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:51.963
I do apologise.

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:55.140
Just to the workforce,

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:57.310
it has been an incredibly tough time

00:07:58.230 --> 00:08:00.060
over the last couple of months or so,

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:01.860
as we've gone through winter.

00:08:01.860 --> 00:08:03.390
We'll hear from David, as I said earlier,

00:08:03.390 --> 00:08:04.770
about levels of demand,

00:08:04.770 --> 00:08:06.330
and, actually, also
that some of that demand

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:07.800
is now beginning to ease.

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:10.590
But, you know, we are still
under variable pressure.

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:14.760
But what I'd like to do, and
in particular at this time,

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:15.960
is to take the opportunity

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:19.140
to thank all of our
colleagues on the front line

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:20.640
for their herculean efforts,

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:22.470
and not just those on
the front line, actually,

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:26.040
but all of those who enable
our people on the front line

00:08:26.040 --> 00:08:28.440
to do their jobs to the
best of their ability.

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:30.360
So I'd just like to record my thanks

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:31.830
and thanks to the board,

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:33.600
to the workforce for they've done,

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:36.514
in particular, over the
last couple of months.

00:08:36.514 --> 00:08:40.320
So at that point, Amanda,
can I hand to you?

00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:42.090
Can I just check logistics?

00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:43.290
I've forgotten some logistics.

00:08:43.290 --> 00:08:45.870
Can I just ask any
declarations of interest,

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:50.370
additional conflicts of interest
that anyone has to record?

00:08:50.370 --> 00:08:52.604
No, thank you very much.

00:08:52.604 --> 00:08:55.560
Can I ask if the board
will approve the minutes

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:57.555
of the last public board?

00:08:57.555 --> 00:09:01.170
Happy with the minutes as circulated?

00:09:01.170 --> 00:09:03.570
And then we do have apologies for absence

00:09:03.570 --> 00:09:04.680
for this afternoon, actually,

00:09:04.680 --> 00:09:09.420
from Wol Kolade, from Rakesh
Kapoor, Jeremy Townsend,

00:09:09.420 --> 00:09:12.120
Munir Pirmohamed, Laura Wade-Gery,

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:13.710
and Susan Kilsby was here this morning,

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:15.120
has actually been called away.

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:18.573
And so Susan has also sent apologies.

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:20.273
Amanda?

00:09:20.273 --> 00:09:22.500
- Great, Richard, thank
you very much indeed.

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:24.000
And I wonder if I could just start by

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:25.800
picking up where you left off,

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:29.190
which is just to echo, again,
thanks to all colleagues

00:09:29.190 --> 00:09:31.230
who have worked,

00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:33.800
particularly over the
last month, six weeks

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:36.450
in what has been a period
of unprecedented pressure

00:09:36.450 --> 00:09:39.714
and continue to just do
their absolute best every day

00:09:39.714 --> 00:09:41.097
to look after patients.

00:09:41.097 --> 00:09:43.050
And I think we all feel that very acutely.

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:44.310
And I also extend my thanks

00:09:44.310 --> 00:09:46.560
to all of those who've
been kind enough to host me

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:48.090
as I've been out and about,

00:09:48.090 --> 00:09:51.030
talking to lots of people
over that period to,

00:09:51.030 --> 00:09:52.530
really, I suppose,

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:55.710
partly get a sense of what the things are

00:09:55.710 --> 00:09:57.420
that are really causing
some of the pressures

00:09:57.420 --> 00:09:58.253
and the challenges,

00:09:58.253 --> 00:09:59.340
but also just to check that

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:00.450
the things that we're planning to do,

00:10:00.450 --> 00:10:02.070
which we're going to
talk about in a minute

00:10:02.070 --> 00:10:04.710
in our "Urgent Emergency
Care Plan" in particular,

00:10:04.710 --> 00:10:05.763
you know, are the right things

00:10:05.763 --> 00:10:08.580
that are really making a
difference on the ground,

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:09.494
and they are.

00:10:09.494 --> 00:10:14.494
So you also very kindly said
so beautifully, Richard,

00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:15.960
I think quite appropriately,

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:17.940
a big thanks to Pauline and to Mark

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:19.410
for everything that they've done,

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:23.910
not just over the last year,
but over the last few years.

00:10:23.910 --> 00:10:27.480
Can I also just say, then,
welcome to Sarah Jane,

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:28.560
who has joined us as

00:10:28.560 --> 00:10:31.020
the National Director
of Urgent Emergency Care

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:33.321
and Deputy Chief Operating Officer.

00:10:33.321 --> 00:10:36.510
And also, I think, probably
a thank you to Steve Russell,

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:38.340
who, of course, is taking over from Mark

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:39.960
in managing this portfolio,

00:10:39.960 --> 00:10:44.960
as Mark, you move on
and Steve picks that up.

00:10:45.199 --> 00:10:48.450
And finally, just another welcome,

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:49.920
and a really important one.

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:51.780
We said it yesterday,
but would like set again.

00:10:51.780 --> 00:10:53.430
To all of our colleagues
who've now joined us

00:10:53.430 --> 00:10:55.140
from NHS Digital.

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:58.890
So it was a big moment,

00:10:58.890 --> 00:11:01.200
and I said both on a webinar yesterday

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:05.550
and in a note that we sent out
to colleagues in NHS Digital,

00:11:05.550 --> 00:11:08.010
the reason this matters so much is that

00:11:08.010 --> 00:11:11.220
digital data and
technology already underpin

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:14.430
so much of our personal
and professional lives,

00:11:14.430 --> 00:11:17.730
and the opportunity to
harness the potential

00:11:17.730 --> 00:11:22.730
to bring all of that expertise
and knowledge gained over,

00:11:23.287 --> 00:11:25.047
you know, many years,

00:11:25.047 --> 00:11:27.150
and in the heat of the pandemic as well,

00:11:27.150 --> 00:11:29.220
right into the heart of NHS England,

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:31.530
I think, is the thing that we've
all got very excited about.

00:11:31.530 --> 00:11:35.010
And it's just important to
acknowledge that that, of course,

00:11:35.010 --> 00:11:35.843
isn't the last step,

00:11:35.843 --> 00:11:38.040
because the next step will
be bringing in colleagues

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:39.120
from Health Education England,

00:11:39.120 --> 00:11:42.030
again reinforcing that very
obvious point you made, Richard,

00:11:42.030 --> 00:11:42.863
which is, you know,

00:11:42.863 --> 00:11:46.080
workforce is absolutely
the underpinning thing,

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:47.820
without which we can do nothing.

00:11:47.820 --> 00:11:51.030
So really great to welcome
colleagues from NHS Digital.

00:11:51.030 --> 00:11:52.350
I think we're going to
talk a bit more about

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:53.183
the change programme,

00:11:53.183 --> 00:11:54.793
and we're conscious that change, you know,

00:11:54.793 --> 00:11:57.120
can be and is difficult,

00:11:57.120 --> 00:11:59.520
and we're doing a lot of
change in this organisation

00:11:59.520 --> 00:12:02.520
whilst also trying to do a
whole number of other things

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:04.440
on the delivery side.

00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:07.537
But just, again, thank
everyone for working very hard

00:12:07.537 --> 00:12:10.290
to make that first day
go as smoothly as it did

00:12:10.290 --> 00:12:12.720
and conscious that we now
have a real opportunity

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:15.090
to continue to work together
within this organisation

00:12:15.090 --> 00:12:16.170
and with colleagues in ICBs

00:12:16.170 --> 00:12:18.510
and with colleagues in regions

00:12:18.510 --> 00:12:20.970
to make sure that we really are creating

00:12:20.970 --> 00:12:25.410
the best of a new NHS England
over the coming months.

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:27.642
So second thing I wanted to say, then,

00:12:27.642 --> 00:12:30.870
and again, you've queued it
in, Richard, is just, you know,

00:12:30.870 --> 00:12:35.870
we have had the most
challenging period I have known

00:12:36.510 --> 00:12:37.760
in my 25 years in the NHS

00:12:38.610 --> 00:12:42.390
over the last month, six weeks.

00:12:42.390 --> 00:12:45.330
David, I know, is going to
pick this up in some detail

00:12:45.330 --> 00:12:46.920
when we get onto the operational report,

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:48.030
but it's just worth noting,

00:12:48.030 --> 00:12:51.300
when we met last time,
beginning of December,

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:53.600
we were talking then about the prospect of

00:12:53.600 --> 00:12:56.210
a twindemmic of COVID and flu
happening at the same time.

00:12:56.210 --> 00:12:57.930
And we were talking at that time

00:12:57.930 --> 00:13:00.450
about the potential for industrial action.

00:13:00.450 --> 00:13:04.110
Of course, both of those
things have happened

00:13:04.110 --> 00:13:09.110
and that has clearly had
an impact on services.

00:13:10.260 --> 00:13:11.790
So in relation to industrial action,

00:13:11.790 --> 00:13:13.410
I know, again, David and others,

00:13:13.410 --> 00:13:16.321
Navina will pick this up in more detail,

00:13:16.321 --> 00:13:19.290
but a huge amount of work has gone on

00:13:19.290 --> 00:13:21.870
locally, regionally and nationally

00:13:21.870 --> 00:13:26.340
with a focus on providing
core services safely

00:13:26.340 --> 00:13:28.197
during periods of action.

00:13:28.197 --> 00:13:31.462
And I do want to thank
everybody involved in that.

00:13:31.462 --> 00:13:35.130
Clearly though, there has been an impact,

00:13:35.130 --> 00:13:37.500
particularly on planned care,

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:39.120
and we have seen, you know,

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:41.610
tens of thousands of appointments
needing to be rescheduled

00:13:41.610 --> 00:13:42.900
as a consequence.

00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:45.300
And, of course, we all understand this,

00:13:45.300 --> 00:13:50.300
but the more now that we
begin to see action expand

00:13:50.310 --> 00:13:52.650
and take place over more days

00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:54.930
and take place closer together,

00:13:54.930 --> 00:13:57.150
then that does become more challenging

00:13:57.150 --> 00:14:00.330
to mitigate the impact
of industrial action

00:14:00.330 --> 00:14:02.190
on wider services.

00:14:02.190 --> 00:14:04.518
So I think it's probably fair to say that

00:14:04.518 --> 00:14:06.360
I speak on behalf of everyone

00:14:06.360 --> 00:14:09.780
when I say we would like to see
resolution to these disputes

00:14:09.780 --> 00:14:10.710
as soon as possible.
- Yeah.

00:14:10.710 --> 00:14:12.382
- But of course, it's our job

00:14:12.382 --> 00:14:14.880
to make sure that we are
doing the work to plan,

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:17.250
to support local colleagues to plan

00:14:17.250 --> 00:14:18.690
and to make sure that
we're supporting people

00:14:18.690 --> 00:14:21.180
to keep patients as safe as possible

00:14:21.180 --> 00:14:23.790
through these periods of action.

00:14:23.790 --> 00:14:25.830
Just talking about winter,

00:14:25.830 --> 00:14:27.690
so we talked about the
extraordinary pressure

00:14:27.690 --> 00:14:28.860
that we've seen over the last period.

00:14:28.860 --> 00:14:31.110
David will talk more about it,

00:14:31.110 --> 00:14:32.850
and that leads us on I think very much

00:14:32.850 --> 00:14:35.953
to talking about the emergency
care plan in a moment.

00:14:35.953 --> 00:14:40.953
But I think it is worth
saying that that is,

00:14:42.690 --> 00:14:44.120
well, we'll talk about
the extraordinary numbers

00:14:44.120 --> 00:14:45.420
in a minute,

00:14:45.420 --> 00:14:50.420
but that is a huge kind of
debt of gratitude, I think,

00:14:51.120 --> 00:14:55.620
that we owe to the staff
who have given so much

00:14:55.620 --> 00:14:58.230
in looking after patients
over that period of time,

00:14:58.230 --> 00:15:00.570
but also to those people
who worked so hard

00:15:00.570 --> 00:15:03.570
to put the mitigations in
place in advance of winter.

00:15:03.570 --> 00:15:06.420
So the fact that we had
24/7 control centres,

00:15:06.420 --> 00:15:09.038
that we've had the extra
beds open in the system,

00:15:09.038 --> 00:15:10.571
that we've had such a successful

00:15:10.571 --> 00:15:12.240
COVID vaccination programme,

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:13.770
flu vaccination programme,

00:15:13.770 --> 00:15:15.990
that we've got community
for all services set up,

00:15:15.990 --> 00:15:18.210
that we've got respiratory services,

00:15:18.210 --> 00:15:20.310
respiratory hubs set up,

00:15:20.310 --> 00:15:21.210
that we've been able to do

00:15:21.210 --> 00:15:23.310
that whole combination of things and more,

00:15:23.310 --> 00:15:27.600
has been critical in enabling the NHS

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:30.990
to withstand some of the
pressure that we have been under

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:34.290
without it being even more
challenging for patients

00:15:34.290 --> 00:15:36.090
and even more challenging for staff.

00:15:36.090 --> 00:15:38.400
So without that extensive preparation,

00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:39.360
I think, no doubt,

00:15:39.360 --> 00:15:41.880
that the impact of the
extreme pressure we've seen

00:15:41.880 --> 00:15:44.459
would have been much worse.

00:15:44.459 --> 00:15:47.880
So that said, we're all really clear

00:15:47.880 --> 00:15:50.580
that we absolutely do not want to see

00:15:50.580 --> 00:15:55.580
another winter like this one next year.

00:15:56.007 --> 00:15:58.200
And that is why it is incredibly timely

00:15:58.200 --> 00:16:00.630
that we've published,
jointly with government,

00:16:00.630 --> 00:16:02.190
the delivery plan for recovering

00:16:02.190 --> 00:16:04.100
urgent and emergency care services.

00:16:04.100 --> 00:16:06.150
We can talk about that in more detail,

00:16:06.150 --> 00:16:09.130
but I think the thing I would
just want to reflect on is

00:16:09.130 --> 00:16:10.980
it is really ambitious.

00:16:10.980 --> 00:16:13.770
Actually delivering what
is described in the plan

00:16:13.770 --> 00:16:15.738
would be the longest and, you know,

00:16:15.738 --> 00:16:19.284
one of I think the longest
but also, crucially,

00:16:19.284 --> 00:16:22.127
most sustained improvements
in urgent emergency care

00:16:22.127 --> 00:16:24.300
in the NHS's history.

00:16:24.300 --> 00:16:25.980
But I think the reason we have confidence

00:16:25.980 --> 00:16:27.720
that it can be delivered

00:16:27.720 --> 00:16:30.600
is because almost everything in the plan,

00:16:30.600 --> 00:16:31.950
in fact, I'd probably go so far as to say

00:16:31.950 --> 00:16:35.880
everything in the plan is
happening somewhere in the NHS,

00:16:35.880 --> 00:16:36.840
and happening well.

00:16:36.840 --> 00:16:40.680
So it really is building on
the best of what is out there

00:16:40.680 --> 00:16:42.540
and the evidence that we now have

00:16:42.540 --> 00:16:45.240
of the impact that
those services can have.

00:16:45.240 --> 00:16:46.500
And certainly when I go out,

00:16:46.500 --> 00:16:49.721
whether I'm in Warwickshire
or Medstead or North Tweed

00:16:49.721 --> 00:16:51.120
or, you know, many of the other places,

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:54.790
what I pick up, actually, is
an enormous clinical energy,

00:16:54.790 --> 00:16:57.930
particularly around these new services

00:16:57.930 --> 00:17:00.030
and the impact that they can have on

00:17:00.030 --> 00:17:02.760
not just taking some of the
pressure off bits of the system

00:17:02.760 --> 00:17:03.593
that are really obvious,

00:17:03.593 --> 00:17:05.790
but actually about providing
better care for patients

00:17:05.790 --> 00:17:06.990
in the right places

00:17:06.990 --> 00:17:09.100
in the way that works best for them.

00:17:09.100 --> 00:17:12.240
So we'll talk about that more in a moment.

00:17:12.240 --> 00:17:13.680
Just this theme, though,

00:17:13.680 --> 00:17:15.150
of kind of spreading and backing

00:17:15.150 --> 00:17:16.500
innovation and good practise

00:17:16.500 --> 00:17:19.740
is something that we also picked
up in the planning guidance

00:17:19.740 --> 00:17:24.678
which went out, I have to say
with apologies to colleagues,

00:17:24.678 --> 00:17:26.250
if anyone is watching this,

00:17:26.250 --> 00:17:28.350
who received the planning guidance from us

00:17:28.350 --> 00:17:29.790
just after five o'clock

00:17:29.790 --> 00:17:32.700
on the last working day before Christmas,

00:17:32.700 --> 00:17:36.240
but we did get it out on the deadline,

00:17:36.240 --> 00:17:37.530
and we know that that's important

00:17:37.530 --> 00:17:41.157
because people do want maximum
time to now engage with that

00:17:41.157 --> 00:17:43.890
and make sure that they
are setting up to be

00:17:43.890 --> 00:17:46.140
as ready as possible for the year to come.

00:17:46.140 --> 00:17:49.590
And that means this time we've
been very deliberate about

00:17:49.590 --> 00:17:53.280
setting out fewer, more
focused headline objectives

00:17:53.280 --> 00:17:56.160
with a strong emphasis,
then, on local planning,

00:17:56.160 --> 00:17:57.810
particularly through ICBs-
- Yeah.

00:17:57.810 --> 00:17:59.610
- Finding the right combination of things

00:17:59.610 --> 00:18:01.290
to meet the needs of the local population,

00:18:01.290 --> 00:18:03.240
but based on those headline objectives.

00:18:03.240 --> 00:18:06.000
And we're working really
closely with teams

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:08.760
over the course of these
next few weeks and months

00:18:08.760 --> 00:18:10.920
to make sure that this
process really does land us

00:18:10.920 --> 00:18:12.750
in as strong a place as possible

00:18:12.750 --> 00:18:14.520
as we head into next year.

00:18:14.520 --> 00:18:15.810
And I'm looking forward to

00:18:15.810 --> 00:18:20.790
we're having an in-person event
with ICB leaders next month

00:18:20.790 --> 00:18:22.350
to go through some of that in more detail.

00:18:22.350 --> 00:18:24.100
I'm really looking forward to that.

00:18:24.990 --> 00:18:26.940
You've mentioned it already, Richard,

00:18:26.940 --> 00:18:30.060
but I think just worth saying again,

00:18:30.060 --> 00:18:35.060
we are mid-flight on the development of

00:18:35.490 --> 00:18:37.290
the primary care recovery plan.

00:18:37.290 --> 00:18:41.214
So we had elective this time
last year, UEC last week,

00:18:41.214 --> 00:18:45.240
and primary care will be
the other really important

00:18:45.240 --> 00:18:47.665
kind of pillar of the recovery plan,

00:18:47.665 --> 00:18:49.420
the trio of things-

00:18:49.420 --> 00:18:51.220
- Yeah.
- That we'll be publishing.

00:18:52.081 --> 00:18:55.290
Just as we've had a
theme through, I think,

00:18:55.290 --> 00:18:57.150
all of the plans on workforce,

00:18:57.150 --> 00:18:59.100
we've also had a theme
through all of the plans

00:18:59.100 --> 00:19:00.960
on digital data technology.

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:03.240
We're expecting the same on primary care,

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:06.060
so great that we'll be talking
a bit more about that today.

00:19:06.060 --> 00:19:08.790
So complimentary to all of these plans

00:19:08.790 --> 00:19:11.820
is the work that we're
doing under Tim's leadership

00:19:11.820 --> 00:19:15.540
to increase our ambition on
how people can access services

00:19:15.540 --> 00:19:18.510
and exercise choice through the NHS app

00:19:18.510 --> 00:19:20.651
and also on navigation of services,

00:19:20.651 --> 00:19:22.020
so that would make it as easy as possible

00:19:22.020 --> 00:19:25.500
for people to get to the right
place to meet their needs.

00:19:25.500 --> 00:19:26.580
You've mentioned it as well,

00:19:26.580 --> 00:19:29.775
the other big one, not part
of the recovery plan trio,

00:19:29.775 --> 00:19:33.173
but it's a crucial piece
of the jigsaw for now

00:19:33.173 --> 00:19:34.230
and for the future,

00:19:34.230 --> 00:19:35.280
is the workforce plan.
- Yeah.

00:19:35.280 --> 00:19:38.010
- So we are still, I think, on track

00:19:38.010 --> 00:19:40.830
to publish something in Spring.

00:19:40.830 --> 00:19:42.840
Finally, I think just important
to say that we do have

00:19:42.840 --> 00:19:45.090
the maternity plan also-
- Yeah.

00:19:45.090 --> 00:19:45.930
- In flight.

00:19:45.930 --> 00:19:49.528
And Ruth will be bringing
something in March, I think-

00:19:49.528 --> 00:19:50.790
- Great.
- To try and pull together,

00:19:50.790 --> 00:19:51.623
crucially,

00:19:51.623 --> 00:19:54.210
all of the areas that we've talked about

00:19:54.210 --> 00:19:56.070
many times in this board.

00:19:56.070 --> 00:19:58.560
And, actually, I think it's
worth us perhaps saying,

00:19:58.560 --> 00:20:01.135
just as we're talking about maternity,

00:20:01.135 --> 00:20:03.327
that colleagues will have seen that

00:20:03.327 --> 00:20:08.160
the CQC prosecuted
Nottingham University Trust

00:20:08.160 --> 00:20:11.220
over the death of Winter Andrews.

00:20:11.220 --> 00:20:12.540
And whilst, of course,

00:20:12.540 --> 00:20:15.420
our thoughts are all with Winter's family,

00:20:15.420 --> 00:20:18.000
who didn't receive the care
that they should have done

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:18.900
from the trust,

00:20:18.900 --> 00:20:21.120
it is a reminder of what,

00:20:21.120 --> 00:20:23.340
and, again, thanks to, then,

00:20:23.340 --> 00:20:26.370
Baroness Julia Cumberlege
and Sir Cyril Chantler,

00:20:26.370 --> 00:20:28.680
have said this week in their letter

00:20:28.680 --> 00:20:30.540
telling us that they're standing down from

00:20:30.540 --> 00:20:32.070
the maternity transformation board.

00:20:32.070 --> 00:20:32.903
And I quote,

00:20:32.903 --> 00:20:35.670
"Maternity care has been on
a journey of improvement.

00:20:35.670 --> 00:20:37.290
The journey is not over.

00:20:37.290 --> 00:20:41.040
Much has been achieved, but
much more remains to be done."

00:20:41.040 --> 00:20:42.600
So we're really looking forward, Ruth,

00:20:42.600 --> 00:20:44.340
to seeing that maternity plan,

00:20:44.340 --> 00:20:47.820
which will build on the
learning from East Kent,

00:20:47.820 --> 00:20:49.290
the learning from Shrewsbury and Telford,

00:20:49.290 --> 00:20:52.980
the existing long-term plan
commitments, better births,

00:20:52.980 --> 00:20:55.140
but really recognising the
truth of that statement.

00:20:55.140 --> 00:20:56.160
It's been on a journey,

00:20:56.160 --> 00:20:59.460
much has been achieved, but
much more remains to come.

00:20:59.460 --> 00:21:02.280
So final two things, if I may,

00:21:02.280 --> 00:21:04.893
it's just to say we have,

00:21:05.790 --> 00:21:08.910
inevitably Richard talked
a lot about NHS services-

00:21:08.910 --> 00:21:09.743
- Yeah.

00:21:09.743 --> 00:21:11.040
- When we talk here at the board,

00:21:11.040 --> 00:21:13.290
but I think we just want to acknowledge

00:21:13.290 --> 00:21:15.678
that we do nothing in isolation.

00:21:15.678 --> 00:21:17.910
So when we talk about services,

00:21:17.910 --> 00:21:21.000
we'd just like to take a moment, I think,

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:24.120
to acknowledge how much work
goes on unseen in communities,

00:21:24.120 --> 00:21:26.130
how important the
voluntary sector has been-

00:21:26.130 --> 00:21:27.330
- Yeah.
- Continues to be

00:21:27.330 --> 00:21:30.600
in all of the things that
we are are trying to do.

00:21:30.600 --> 00:21:31.633
And in fact,

00:21:31.633 --> 00:21:34.950
how much of some of the
amazing stuff that the NHS does

00:21:34.950 --> 00:21:36.150
just does go unseen.

00:21:36.150 --> 00:21:37.770
And in that vein, Claire,

00:21:37.770 --> 00:21:39.300
it was a real privilege
to be able to go out

00:21:39.300 --> 00:21:42.030
with some of your colleagues, actually,

00:21:42.030 --> 00:21:45.390
and visiting with a
specialist health visitor

00:21:45.390 --> 00:21:48.930
who looks after people who are homeless.

00:21:48.930 --> 00:21:52.170
And, you know, it was both remarkable

00:21:52.170 --> 00:21:55.110
for showing some of the
extraordinary skills and commitment

00:21:55.110 --> 00:21:56.160
that people have,

00:21:56.160 --> 00:21:58.170
but also just a great
opportunity, I think,

00:21:58.170 --> 00:22:01.380
to shine a light on something
that is so, so important,

00:22:01.380 --> 00:22:02.460
but sometimes doesn't, perhaps,

00:22:02.460 --> 00:22:05.028
get quite to the top of the media story.

00:22:05.028 --> 00:22:08.850
In a similar vein, I know, Ruth,

00:22:08.850 --> 00:22:12.000
that the work that the volunteer
task force has been doing

00:22:12.000 --> 00:22:13.473
is nearing fruition.

00:22:13.473 --> 00:22:16.170
So volunteering has
been a part of the NHS,

00:22:16.170 --> 00:22:20.790
a crucial part since it
first existed 74 years ago.

00:22:20.790 --> 00:22:22.830
I think the last couple
of years, three years,

00:22:22.830 --> 00:22:25.560
if anything have just
shown even more obviously

00:22:25.560 --> 00:22:29.580
how crucial the role of
volunteers is to the NHS,

00:22:29.580 --> 00:22:32.160
especially in some of our
most challenged areas.

00:22:32.160 --> 00:22:35.550
So that's what the volunteering
task force was set up to do,

00:22:35.550 --> 00:22:37.050
to look at where we could go further.

00:22:37.050 --> 00:22:41.100
Ruth has co-chaired that
with Sir Tom Hughes-Hallett,

00:22:41.100 --> 00:22:42.780
and I think I'm right in saying

00:22:42.780 --> 00:22:44.730
that report is going to be coming soon

00:22:44.730 --> 00:22:47.700
and that will be an exciting
opportunity, I think,

00:22:47.700 --> 00:22:49.110
for us to look forward

00:22:49.110 --> 00:22:50.700
as we think about how volunteers

00:22:50.700 --> 00:22:52.890
and part of that wider
network of colleagues

00:22:52.890 --> 00:22:54.120
that we all work with

00:22:54.120 --> 00:22:56.070
can play an even greater
role in the future.

00:22:56.070 --> 00:22:56.903
- Thank you.

00:22:56.903 --> 00:22:58.020
So let me pause.

00:22:58.020 --> 00:22:59.866
Any comments or questions?

00:22:59.866 --> 00:23:01.950
I know, Andrew, one of the
things we've talked about

00:23:01.950 --> 00:23:03.060
in the private board,

00:23:03.060 --> 00:23:05.730
and you've certainly referenced
to me a few times, actually,

00:23:05.730 --> 00:23:08.280
about thinking now about next winter.

00:23:08.280 --> 00:23:09.300
I mean, last year,

00:23:09.300 --> 00:23:11.250
I think we published a winter plan

00:23:11.250 --> 00:23:13.050
earlier than we'd published before,

00:23:13.050 --> 00:23:15.219
but, actually, we're still in winter.

00:23:15.219 --> 00:23:18.060
But, actually, I suppose one
of the questions and thoughts

00:23:18.060 --> 00:23:20.910
is are we learning enough now

00:23:20.910 --> 00:23:22.650
and starting to prep for next winter

00:23:22.650 --> 00:23:26.400
in addition to the urgent
emergency care published plan?

00:23:26.400 --> 00:23:27.470
I don't know if you want to-

00:23:27.470 --> 00:23:30.270
- So, I think it's really
important that we're acting now-

00:23:30.270 --> 00:23:31.103
- Yeah.

00:23:31.103 --> 00:23:33.210
- Around developing our
plans for the coming winter.

00:23:33.210 --> 00:23:38.210
We've not done that before so
early in the previous year.

00:23:39.030 --> 00:23:43.710
So, I think the focus on capacity,

00:23:43.710 --> 00:23:45.000
social care,

00:23:45.000 --> 00:23:49.590
and trying to improve our
arrangements for discharge,

00:23:49.590 --> 00:23:52.500
and it's not just down
to social care capacity,

00:23:52.500 --> 00:23:54.780
it's down to the NHS
getting its act together

00:23:54.780 --> 00:23:57.578
in a more uniform way across the country

00:23:57.578 --> 00:24:00.720
and handling some of the workforce issues

00:24:00.720 --> 00:24:02.340
that we've got ahead of us.

00:24:02.340 --> 00:24:03.930
So those three elements, I think,

00:24:03.930 --> 00:24:05.786
start to come together in the plan.

00:24:05.786 --> 00:24:06.619
- Yeah.

00:24:06.619 --> 00:24:08.790
- And I think we can
make some real progress,

00:24:08.790 --> 00:24:12.240
but all of this has to
be done at ICB level.

00:24:12.240 --> 00:24:14.130
That's the really important thing.

00:24:14.130 --> 00:24:17.940
We can do our bit to support the service

00:24:17.940 --> 00:24:21.000
in terms of sign posting and resourcing,

00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:25.080
but the real flow or improvements in flow

00:24:25.080 --> 00:24:28.020
need to happen on the
ground in local settings.

00:24:28.020 --> 00:24:29.310
And that's the big role.

00:24:29.310 --> 00:24:31.020
That's the one big thing

00:24:31.020 --> 00:24:33.930
that I think Amanda will
be asking ICB colleagues

00:24:33.930 --> 00:24:37.320
to actually focus on and deliver
some tangible improvements.

00:24:37.320 --> 00:24:39.540
- The meeting, is it late
February they're coming together?

00:24:39.540 --> 00:24:41.930
- Yeah, so I think this is
a great cue in, actually,

00:24:41.930 --> 00:24:43.530
to the next part of the conversation,

00:24:43.530 --> 00:24:44.610
because you are absolutely right,

00:24:44.610 --> 00:24:47.700
a plan is only as good as the delivery.

00:24:47.700 --> 00:24:48.750
So we now have two things.

00:24:48.750 --> 00:24:49.830
We have the planning guidance,

00:24:49.830 --> 00:24:53.347
which is that opportunity
for ICBs to absolutely say,

00:24:53.347 --> 00:24:55.860
"Right, this is how we are
going to make sense of it here,"

00:24:55.860 --> 00:24:57.994
and we have the the UEC plan,

00:24:57.994 --> 00:25:00.600
which is very clearly what
the evidence is saying

00:25:00.600 --> 00:25:03.210
are the really big things
that can make a difference.

00:25:03.210 --> 00:25:05.250
So delivery of those two things

00:25:05.250 --> 00:25:07.440
is absolutely the focus now.

00:25:07.440 --> 00:25:08.670
- Before we just,

00:25:08.670 --> 00:25:11.698
and then, Mark, you want to
ask a question make up on to a-

00:25:11.698 --> 00:25:13.470
- Well, I want to pick
up theme of resilience,

00:25:13.470 --> 00:25:15.390
'cause I mean if there's one
thing that's come out of COVID,

00:25:15.390 --> 00:25:17.280
and, actually, what's happening
in Ukraine at the moment,

00:25:17.280 --> 00:25:19.650
is the sort of challenge to resilience

00:25:19.650 --> 00:25:22.110
and issues of antibiotic
supply, for example,

00:25:22.110 --> 00:25:24.371
around streptococcal infection.

00:25:24.371 --> 00:25:26.070
And so it just seems to me that,

00:25:26.070 --> 00:25:28.320
as sorry if it's a comment
more than a question,

00:25:28.320 --> 00:25:29.807
that resilience really is
going to have to be built

00:25:29.807 --> 00:25:31.350
in the plans going forward.

00:25:31.350 --> 00:25:33.840
And we're seeing just how stretched

00:25:33.840 --> 00:25:34.767
the system is at the moment,

00:25:34.767 --> 00:25:36.783
and how narrow the resilience is.

00:25:37.772 --> 00:25:38.730
- Agreed.

00:25:38.730 --> 00:25:40.770
I think that's one of the reasons, partly,

00:25:40.770 --> 00:25:43.530
I made the point earlier
about Chris doing take away,

00:25:43.530 --> 00:25:46.620
doing a piece of work just
thinking about physical capacity.

00:25:46.620 --> 00:25:49.380
So rather aligned with
long-term workforce plan,

00:25:49.380 --> 00:25:50.337
how do we see that?

00:25:50.337 --> 00:25:51.450
And in the private board,

00:25:51.450 --> 00:25:54.210
we had that very good exchange
and conversation around,

00:25:54.210 --> 00:25:56.010
actually, capacity in the system,

00:25:56.010 --> 00:25:59.100
the way it dealt with
unprecedented levels of demand.

00:25:59.100 --> 00:26:01.080
But also there was an
analogy across, if you like,

00:26:01.080 --> 00:26:04.050
to climate change flooding about surges.

00:26:04.050 --> 00:26:05.940
How do we handle surges?

00:26:05.940 --> 00:26:07.350
And actually, we'll hear from David,

00:26:07.350 --> 00:26:08.640
I think probably now, actually,

00:26:08.640 --> 00:26:10.830
about the surge, I think, in demand

00:26:10.830 --> 00:26:13.740
that we certainly saw over the
past four, five weeks or so.

00:26:13.740 --> 00:26:14.573
David?

00:26:14.573 --> 00:26:15.547
- Thank you.

00:26:15.547 --> 00:26:16.380
Thank you.

00:26:16.380 --> 00:26:17.213
Thank you, Amanda.

00:26:17.213 --> 00:26:19.050
So I will focus my report
this month on two areas.

00:26:19.050 --> 00:26:20.610
One is performance in December,

00:26:20.610 --> 00:26:22.863
and secondly, industrial reaction.

00:26:23.827 --> 00:26:25.860
I always say industrial reaction.

00:26:25.860 --> 00:26:26.693
Industrial action.

00:26:26.693 --> 00:26:29.340
I did that on a webinar
yesterday, actually.

00:26:29.340 --> 00:26:31.800
So as has been described,

00:26:31.800 --> 00:26:35.430
December performance
was really challenged by

00:26:35.430 --> 00:26:39.120
a particular and unprecedented
constellation of challenges

00:26:39.120 --> 00:26:41.820
which affected both the
demand and the supply side

00:26:41.820 --> 00:26:43.320
across the NHS

00:26:43.320 --> 00:26:47.070
So on the demand, COVID-19
peaked on the 29th of December

00:26:47.070 --> 00:26:49.798
with 9,553 inpatients.

00:26:49.798 --> 00:26:51.510
And on the same day,

00:26:51.510 --> 00:26:55.890
influenza peaked with 6,172 inpatients.

00:26:55.890 --> 00:26:59.024
There was an early winter
wave of group Strep A,

00:26:59.024 --> 00:27:04.024
with 37,068 notifications of scarlet fever

00:27:04.080 --> 00:27:05.310
from September to January,

00:27:05.310 --> 00:27:07.650
which is the highest
observed since the 1950s.

00:27:07.650 --> 00:27:09.060
These are really, you know,

00:27:09.060 --> 00:27:12.000
very, very unusual
pressures in the system.

00:27:12.000 --> 00:27:14.760
Overall, the group Strep A cases peaked

00:27:14.760 --> 00:27:16.800
on the week of the ninth
to the 16th of December,

00:27:16.800 --> 00:27:20.160
with 9,886 cases.

00:27:20.160 --> 00:27:22.620
We also had a very particular
cold snap of weather

00:27:22.620 --> 00:27:24.510
from the seventh to the 18th of December,

00:27:24.510 --> 00:27:26.730
which increased respiratory disease

00:27:26.730 --> 00:27:29.820
and demand for 111 was the highest ever.

00:27:29.820 --> 00:27:31.020
During the month of December,

00:27:31.020 --> 00:27:33.990
we received 2,920,000 calls,

00:27:33.990 --> 00:27:36.664
averaging 94,000 calls a day,

00:27:36.664 --> 00:27:40.560
with an eye watering peak of 171,000 calls

00:27:40.560 --> 00:27:41.940
on December the 27th.

00:27:41.940 --> 00:27:45.540
These are genuinely
unprecedented levels of demand.

00:27:45.540 --> 00:27:48.270
Ambulance services answered
over 1 million calls.

00:27:48.270 --> 00:27:52.638
This peaked at 43,000 calls
on the 19th of December.

00:27:52.638 --> 00:27:57.420
A and E attendances were
2,283,000 attendances,

00:27:57.420 --> 00:28:00.930
which is an increase of 21%
on the previous December.

00:28:00.930 --> 00:28:04.080
And there were 517,000
emergency admissions,

00:28:04.080 --> 00:28:07.920
which is 3.7% higher than
the previous December.

00:28:07.920 --> 00:28:10.165
So real increase in demand.

00:28:10.165 --> 00:28:13.650
On the supply side, we
had 98,000 GNA beds open

00:28:13.650 --> 00:28:16.590
with an occupancy of 94.2%,

00:28:16.590 --> 00:28:18.213
so we were running hot.

00:28:18.213 --> 00:28:21.540
We also saw, and I will
come on to talk more about

00:28:21.540 --> 00:28:24.750
unprecedented industrial
action being taken place

00:28:24.750 --> 00:28:26.940
by both within the nurses
and the ambulance services.

00:28:26.940 --> 00:28:29.698
Again putting real, real
pressure on the system.

00:28:29.698 --> 00:28:31.110
And of course,

00:28:31.110 --> 00:28:33.960
we did have the two four-day
bank holiday weekends

00:28:33.960 --> 00:28:35.010
over that winter period,

00:28:35.010 --> 00:28:35.970
which again,

00:28:35.970 --> 00:28:39.451
putting further pressure on the
supply side of the equation.

00:28:39.451 --> 00:28:40.347
As such,

00:28:40.347 --> 00:28:44.250
the performance particularly
in urgent care, during December

00:28:44.250 --> 00:28:46.020
was really, really challenged.

00:28:46.020 --> 00:28:48.240
I mean 111 call handling,

00:28:48.240 --> 00:28:51.720
abandonment rate was 48.9%
on the 12th of December,

00:28:51.720 --> 00:28:53.430
which is as low as it's been.

00:28:53.430 --> 00:28:54.960
On Cat-2 response times,

00:28:54.960 --> 00:28:57.570
the peak was over 132 minutes,

00:28:57.570 --> 00:28:59.550
and on the 2nd of January,

00:28:59.550 --> 00:29:03.270
we lost almost 8,000 hours
of ambulance handover hours

00:29:03.270 --> 00:29:05.160
on that particular day.

00:29:05.160 --> 00:29:07.770
Now that meant December
was really challenging,

00:29:07.770 --> 00:29:10.590
but we have now moved into January,

00:29:10.590 --> 00:29:12.840
where we've seen reductions in demand.

00:29:12.840 --> 00:29:14.550
So the COVID numbers are down,

00:29:14.550 --> 00:29:17.640
now down to 5,750 inpatients,

00:29:17.640 --> 00:29:20.490
compared to the nine and a
half thousand in December.

00:29:20.490 --> 00:29:22.920
There's been a 77% reduction

00:29:22.920 --> 00:29:25.170
in the number of people in beds with flu

00:29:25.170 --> 00:29:28.065
and similar reductions
in the group A strep.

00:29:28.065 --> 00:29:32.430
111 call volumes are back
to about 52,000 a day

00:29:32.430 --> 00:29:35.580
compared to that peak of 171,000,

00:29:35.580 --> 00:29:39.120
and 999 volumes are again
down to 20,000 a day

00:29:39.120 --> 00:29:41.790
as opposed to that peak of 32,000.

00:29:41.790 --> 00:29:44.040
We still have, also on the supply side,

00:29:44.040 --> 00:29:46.290
opened to further almost 2,000 beds,

00:29:46.290 --> 00:29:48.960
but occupancy is still running at 94%.

00:29:48.960 --> 00:29:51.210
So we are still running hot.

00:29:51.210 --> 00:29:52.043
But again,

00:29:52.043 --> 00:29:53.880
as a consequence of that reductions in

00:29:53.880 --> 00:29:55.890
those increases in capacity,

00:29:55.890 --> 00:29:57.780
we have seen performance improve,

00:29:57.780 --> 00:30:00.480
and although it might not be
where we would want it to be,

00:30:00.480 --> 00:30:03.360
we are certainly in a much
better and improving place

00:30:03.360 --> 00:30:05.460
as we go into this calendar year.

00:30:05.460 --> 00:30:07.740
And I'm sure we'll hear from
Sarah Jane Marshall later,

00:30:07.740 --> 00:30:09.990
we'll talk about how our
plans are being developed

00:30:09.990 --> 00:30:12.450
to sustain this level of infrastructure

00:30:12.450 --> 00:30:15.330
to meet demand going forward.

00:30:15.330 --> 00:30:16.830
The one other point I would make,

00:30:16.830 --> 00:30:18.600
as a consequence of the industrial action,

00:30:18.600 --> 00:30:19.800
which we're about to come onto,

00:30:19.800 --> 00:30:23.370
we have seen some rescheduling
of elective activity,

00:30:23.370 --> 00:30:25.860
about 30,000 inpatient, outpatients,

00:30:25.860 --> 00:30:28.230
in both December and January,

00:30:28.230 --> 00:30:31.080
which has had an impact on
some of the pace of improvement

00:30:31.080 --> 00:30:32.790
around the 78 week wait challenge.

00:30:32.790 --> 00:30:36.360
But we are still working
hard on making real progress

00:30:36.360 --> 00:30:38.710
to virtually eliminating the 78 week wait

00:30:39.667 --> 00:30:42.385
by the end of this financial year.

00:30:42.385 --> 00:30:43.950
So I'll pause there, Richard,

00:30:43.950 --> 00:30:46.170
before I move on to industrial action,

00:30:46.170 --> 00:30:48.900
in case there are any questions
of clarification or content

00:30:48.900 --> 00:30:50.250
people would wish to raise.

00:30:50.250 --> 00:30:51.083
- Any?

00:30:51.083 --> 00:30:51.916
Any, Andrew?

00:30:51.916 --> 00:30:56.070
- Can I just make a point on
the elective target, David?

00:30:56.070 --> 00:30:58.470
So we've got target of 78 weeks

00:30:58.470 --> 00:31:00.660
to meet by the 31st of March,

00:31:00.660 --> 00:31:03.570
and we're now into early Feb,

00:31:03.570 --> 00:31:06.690
so we'd expect providers
to have booked in-

00:31:06.690 --> 00:31:07.523
- Yep.

00:31:07.523 --> 00:31:11.670
- Most of their patients
during the next few days or so.

00:31:11.670 --> 00:31:14.400
So have you got any data back on that?

00:31:14.400 --> 00:31:17.130
Because that'll be one
of the success factors

00:31:17.130 --> 00:31:19.044
in terms of, you know,
are we going to get there?

00:31:19.044 --> 00:31:19.877
- Yeah.

00:31:19.877 --> 00:31:20.710
- So I will use this an opportunity

00:31:20.710 --> 00:31:23.280
to ring in my colleague, Pauline,
in her last board meeting

00:31:23.280 --> 00:31:24.840
to talk about the 78 wait weeks.

00:31:24.840 --> 00:31:26.155
Pauline?

00:31:26.155 --> 00:31:30.020
- So, Andrew, we've done an
exercise right across all trusts

00:31:30.020 --> 00:31:33.150
asking them to look at their bookings

00:31:33.150 --> 00:31:35.430
for the rest of the financial year,

00:31:35.430 --> 00:31:38.130
for inpatients and also outpatients.

00:31:38.130 --> 00:31:41.280
We asked them to do returns
for the end of January.

00:31:41.280 --> 00:31:44.850
So we're just receiving that
information at the moment,

00:31:44.850 --> 00:31:49.140
and clearly any organisation
that has not booked a patient

00:31:49.140 --> 00:31:51.000
who needs to come in for admission,

00:31:51.000 --> 00:31:52.130
we will have a dialogue with

00:31:52.130 --> 00:31:54.750
in the same way as any outpatient

00:31:54.750 --> 00:31:57.690
who hasn't got their
next appointment booked.

00:31:57.690 --> 00:31:59.340
So within a number of days,

00:31:59.340 --> 00:32:01.841
we will have that data available.

00:32:01.841 --> 00:32:04.710
- Are we still seeing
the same broad pattern

00:32:04.710 --> 00:32:08.730
if we take the 7.2, 7.4
million on the waiting list?

00:32:08.730 --> 00:32:09.563
- [Pauline] Yeah.

00:32:09.563 --> 00:32:13.300
- That what I carry in my
head is about 3% of that list

00:32:14.250 --> 00:32:15.630
need an operation,

00:32:15.630 --> 00:32:18.763
surgical procedure
including an overnight stay,

00:32:18.763 --> 00:32:23.280
and about 18 to 20% require
some form of surgical procedure.

00:32:23.280 --> 00:32:25.285
But 80% are at-
- [Pauline] 80%, yes.

00:32:25.285 --> 00:32:27.854
- Is that pattern still
holding in the list?

00:32:27.854 --> 00:32:31.260
- So about 80% of the patients involved

00:32:31.260 --> 00:32:33.450
are basically outpatients, et cetera.

00:32:33.450 --> 00:32:36.390
And that's why it's really
important to book the next event

00:32:36.390 --> 00:32:38.070
from an outpatient perspective.

00:32:38.070 --> 00:32:41.700
And you're right, approximately
with the other numbers,

00:32:41.700 --> 00:32:43.500
but it's the same picture.

00:32:43.500 --> 00:32:44.333
- [Richard] Right.

00:32:44.333 --> 00:32:46.860
- And, you know, clearly we've
been through this exercise

00:32:46.860 --> 00:32:49.740
for patients who were waiting two years

00:32:49.740 --> 00:32:52.020
and we've got to a place now where

00:32:52.020 --> 00:32:53.340
the only remaining patients

00:32:53.340 --> 00:32:56.255
are patients who are choosing
to wait for care, et cetera.

00:32:56.255 --> 00:32:59.390
And our ambition is to
get as close as possible

00:32:59.390 --> 00:33:02.657
to the same place for
78 weeks, ie. 18 months,

00:33:02.657 --> 00:33:04.020
by the end of March.

00:33:04.020 --> 00:33:04.853
- Very good, thank you.

00:33:04.853 --> 00:33:05.904
Thank you, Pauline.

00:33:05.904 --> 00:33:06.990
- [Andrew] Richard, can I
just raise another point?

00:33:06.990 --> 00:33:08.010
- Please, yep.

00:33:08.010 --> 00:33:10.020
- So on ambulance handovers,

00:33:10.020 --> 00:33:12.870
I think that was the big
concern of the public

00:33:12.870 --> 00:33:15.660
over the last few weeks,

00:33:15.660 --> 00:33:16.590
and it'd be good, David,

00:33:16.590 --> 00:33:20.160
if you've got some data to share on

00:33:20.160 --> 00:33:23.973
what's happened more recently
during the month of January.

00:33:25.290 --> 00:33:26.250
- Yes.

00:33:26.250 --> 00:33:30.360
And I am looking in my notes
here as you ask that question.

00:33:30.360 --> 00:33:33.513
Ambulance handovers performance,

00:33:34.380 --> 00:33:35.730
Cat-2, we've seen improvement.

00:33:35.730 --> 00:33:38.640
They are now down to, over
the course of January,

00:33:38.640 --> 00:33:40.020
just over 25 minutes

00:33:40.020 --> 00:33:42.840
compared to an average of 95
minutes the previous month.

00:33:42.840 --> 00:33:44.040
- [Andrew] That's a big step change.

00:33:44.040 --> 00:33:45.470
- Yeah, that is absolutely
heading in the right direction.

00:33:45.470 --> 00:33:47.130
So that's the Cat-2 performance.

00:33:47.130 --> 00:33:47.963
- [Richard] Okay.

00:33:47.963 --> 00:33:49.920
- Ambulance handovers improved.

00:33:49.920 --> 00:33:53.460
The hours lost have reduced
from 7,900 in December

00:33:53.460 --> 00:33:56.220
down to 1,475 in January.

00:33:56.220 --> 00:33:58.062
So again, things moving
in the right direction.

00:33:58.062 --> 00:34:02.293
- Because I'm prompted by the
word ambulances from Andrew,

00:34:04.170 --> 00:34:06.210
can I just ask again,

00:34:06.210 --> 00:34:08.883
whilst under significant,
really severe pressure,

00:34:10.532 --> 00:34:13.200
did we see any differences
in conveyancing rates?

00:34:13.200 --> 00:34:16.020
Because I think about half the callouts

00:34:16.020 --> 00:34:18.600
actually result in conveyancing.

00:34:18.600 --> 00:34:20.100
I don't know if that changed at all.

00:34:20.100 --> 00:34:21.148
- [David] I don't have-

00:34:21.148 --> 00:34:22.650
- and then elderly falls.

00:34:22.650 --> 00:34:24.990
- I don't have the detailed
data in front of me, Richard.

00:34:24.990 --> 00:34:25.823
- [Richard] No, no.

00:34:25.823 --> 00:34:26.656
- What we did see,

00:34:26.656 --> 00:34:29.400
we did see a general reduction
in conveyancing rates.

00:34:29.400 --> 00:34:31.470
The other thing that we
did by the end of December,

00:34:31.470 --> 00:34:34.710
we established across
each ICS across the UK,

00:34:34.710 --> 00:34:37.217
is an eight 'til eight, seven
day a week full service.

00:34:37.217 --> 00:34:38.050
- [Richard] Very good.

00:34:38.050 --> 00:34:39.660
- So it is too early at the moment

00:34:39.660 --> 00:34:41.940
to really quantify the
impact that has had,

00:34:41.940 --> 00:34:43.440
but I think that will turn out to be

00:34:43.440 --> 00:34:45.660
one of the most important
interventions that we've made

00:34:45.660 --> 00:34:47.430
as part of this year's winter plan.

00:34:47.430 --> 00:34:49.200
And having that in place sustainably

00:34:49.200 --> 00:34:52.103
will really make material
benefits at the time.

00:34:52.103 --> 00:34:54.472
- I mean, I do think
that it'll be really good

00:34:54.472 --> 00:34:56.250
in subsequent boards

00:34:56.250 --> 00:34:59.100
to get reporting on how
the elderly falls service

00:34:59.100 --> 00:35:01.290
has actually, hopefully, helped the system

00:35:01.290 --> 00:35:03.970
and certainly helped to
get service to patients

00:35:04.830 --> 00:35:08.760
or elderly people who have
fallen much, much more quickly.

00:35:08.760 --> 00:35:09.593
Mark you-

00:35:09.593 --> 00:35:10.512
- I had a question specifically

00:35:10.512 --> 00:35:11.345
on the elderly falls, actually,

00:35:11.345 --> 00:35:13.890
which is a lot of people
fall at night, actually,

00:35:13.890 --> 00:35:16.350
and that's when if you end up
on the floor for a long time,

00:35:16.350 --> 00:35:18.417
you are likely to end up in hospital.

00:35:18.417 --> 00:35:19.250
- [Richard] Then you have to be conveyed.

00:35:19.250 --> 00:35:20.289
- Yeah, so I mean,

00:35:20.289 --> 00:35:24.870
it says as a minimum 8:00 AM to 8:00 PM,

00:35:24.870 --> 00:35:27.490
but I mean, ideally, it
would be a 24/7 service.

00:35:27.490 --> 00:35:29.440
- So I think again, what we need to do,

00:35:29.440 --> 00:35:32.400
we've got the service up and
running, let's track the data,

00:35:32.400 --> 00:35:33.363
let's see what we need then to do

00:35:33.363 --> 00:35:35.040
as a consequence overnight.

00:35:35.040 --> 00:35:37.620
- So just before you then go and pick up

00:35:37.620 --> 00:35:39.150
on industrial action-
- [David] Yeah.

00:35:39.150 --> 00:35:39.983
- I mean, I would say,

00:35:39.983 --> 00:35:43.863
and I want to be careful
how I do describe it,

00:35:45.300 --> 00:35:46.307
but you're quite right, you know?

00:35:46.307 --> 00:35:51.307
We are running hot in capacity
terms with 98,000 beds,

00:35:52.511 --> 00:35:54.840
over 94% operated.

00:35:54.840 --> 00:35:58.260
We then have, as you say
on the 29th of December,

00:35:58.260 --> 00:36:00.750
two highest levels of flu and COVID.

00:36:00.750 --> 00:36:02.340
And then we have Strep A coming,

00:36:02.340 --> 00:36:05.670
and Strep A has the
consequence of, quite clearly,

00:36:05.670 --> 00:36:08.460
the public respond out of
anxiety for their children.

00:36:08.460 --> 00:36:09.810
In particular it causes volume,

00:36:09.810 --> 00:36:11.760
which I suspect a lot of the 111,

00:36:11.760 --> 00:36:14.181
remarkable 170,000 calls,

00:36:14.181 --> 00:36:18.870
are driven by people wholly
understandably anxious.

00:36:18.870 --> 00:36:20.610
And we have two bank holidays.

00:36:20.610 --> 00:36:22.930
So you have two four days two,

00:36:22.930 --> 00:36:24.360
you have a 10 day period where

00:36:24.360 --> 00:36:27.240
I would call that an endogenous
problem within the system

00:36:27.240 --> 00:36:31.170
about how we manage demand,
as opposed to exogenous.

00:36:31.170 --> 00:36:33.900
But it does point to
Andrew's question about,

00:36:33.900 --> 00:36:38.900
so how do we really think
about resilience against surge

00:36:39.071 --> 00:36:42.660
as opposed to just purely capacity?

00:36:42.660 --> 00:36:44.850
And how do we model that as
we look forward potentially

00:36:44.850 --> 00:36:45.720
for the next winter,

00:36:45.720 --> 00:36:48.492
without knowing what will
come at us next winter?

00:36:48.492 --> 00:36:49.470
You know, if we start,

00:36:49.470 --> 00:36:53.220
if we run at a level hot
before we get surges,

00:36:53.220 --> 00:36:56.095
then, actually, the flood waters
overwhelm us for a period.

00:36:56.095 --> 00:36:59.520
And so I think we have
to think hard about that.

00:36:59.520 --> 00:37:00.690
David, do you want to
do industrial action?

00:37:00.690 --> 00:37:03.270
- Yes, so I will reflect briefly, Richard,

00:37:03.270 --> 00:37:05.160
on the industrial action
we've seen to date,

00:37:05.160 --> 00:37:06.450
but probably more importantly,

00:37:06.450 --> 00:37:08.550
I will look forward with
both Navina and Ruth

00:37:08.550 --> 00:37:12.240
to the industrial action
that we have ahead of us.

00:37:12.240 --> 00:37:14.070
Again, you will have seen in my report

00:37:14.070 --> 00:37:16.050
the industrial action took place

00:37:16.050 --> 00:37:17.700
in both December and January

00:37:17.700 --> 00:37:19.440
within both Royal College of Nurses,

00:37:19.440 --> 00:37:20.940
ambulance staff and trade unions,

00:37:20.940 --> 00:37:22.140
and most recently,

00:37:22.140 --> 00:37:24.540
the Chartered Society of Physiotherapists.

00:37:24.540 --> 00:37:28.350
So we have set up a full incident machine

00:37:28.350 --> 00:37:29.880
to oversee the industrial action,

00:37:29.880 --> 00:37:32.134
which Navina is the SRO of.

00:37:32.134 --> 00:37:34.530
We've worked extremely
well with colleagues

00:37:34.530 --> 00:37:35.670
right across the system

00:37:35.670 --> 00:37:38.370
to ensure that as far as we possibly can,

00:37:38.370 --> 00:37:40.860
we plan and contingency plan in advance

00:37:40.860 --> 00:37:43.440
to make sure that our
patients are kept safe.

00:37:43.440 --> 00:37:45.030
And very importantly,

00:37:45.030 --> 00:37:47.550
that as much planned care
can be kept on the road

00:37:47.550 --> 00:37:48.390
as is possible,

00:37:48.390 --> 00:37:51.679
given the circumstances in
which we find ourselves.

00:37:51.679 --> 00:37:54.780
Colleagues will talk about
how the national delegations

00:37:54.780 --> 00:37:57.785
have been negotiated and
implemented, as I hand over,

00:37:57.785 --> 00:38:00.630
and also we'll talk about
the national messaging.

00:38:00.630 --> 00:38:05.160
I think that we put in the
system, as we look ahead,

00:38:05.160 --> 00:38:07.050
we will see industrial action

00:38:07.050 --> 00:38:09.996
on four of the five days
ahead of us next week.

00:38:09.996 --> 00:38:12.930
So on Monday, the Royal
College of Nursing members,

00:38:12.930 --> 00:38:15.960
73 trust and ambulance workers
and eight ambulance trusts,

00:38:15.960 --> 00:38:18.510
will take action simultaneously.

00:38:18.510 --> 00:38:20.310
And that is the first time we have seen

00:38:20.310 --> 00:38:23.880
simultaneous industrial action
by both the ambulance service

00:38:23.880 --> 00:38:24.998
and the nurses.

00:38:24.998 --> 00:38:28.200
And we are very, very
focused on ensuring that

00:38:28.200 --> 00:38:30.897
we do have plans in place
to manage what will be

00:38:30.897 --> 00:38:33.120
different levels of risk

00:38:33.120 --> 00:38:34.980
to the risks that we've
had to manage to date.

00:38:34.980 --> 00:38:37.593
And again, colleagues
will comment on this.

00:38:37.593 --> 00:38:41.220
The RCN will stage a second
day of industrial action

00:38:41.220 --> 00:38:42.300
on the Tuesday,

00:38:42.300 --> 00:38:44.531
while the charted physiotherapists

00:38:44.531 --> 00:38:47.103
will also take action at 32 trusts.

00:38:47.103 --> 00:38:48.870
And, of course, they have
really important roles

00:38:48.870 --> 00:38:50.580
in facilitating patient discharge,

00:38:50.580 --> 00:38:53.940
so we should not underestimate
their role in the round.

00:38:53.940 --> 00:38:54.930
On Friday,

00:38:54.930 --> 00:38:57.300
there'll be further industrial
action by unison members

00:38:57.300 --> 00:38:59.610
at five ambulance trusts.

00:38:59.610 --> 00:39:02.335
We did write out last Friday to the system

00:39:02.335 --> 00:39:06.300
to asking them to ensure that
they are absolutely on point

00:39:06.300 --> 00:39:08.400
in terms of planning for this.

00:39:08.400 --> 00:39:10.200
But as I say, it will be challenging.

00:39:10.200 --> 00:39:11.610
It is a different level of challenge

00:39:11.610 --> 00:39:13.980
to the challenge that
we have faced to date.

00:39:13.980 --> 00:39:15.660
So I think it's probably
appropriate at this moment

00:39:15.660 --> 00:39:18.480
for you to hand over to Navina and Ruth

00:39:18.480 --> 00:39:20.433
to add further flavour to this.

00:39:20.433 --> 00:39:21.433
- Navina?

00:39:21.433 --> 00:39:22.266
- Yes.

00:39:22.266 --> 00:39:25.363
So may I just add to David's points

00:39:25.363 --> 00:39:27.390
to give the board assurance

00:39:27.390 --> 00:39:31.920
that we have systems in place
for day-to-day management

00:39:31.920 --> 00:39:34.170
across us, our regions, our providers

00:39:34.170 --> 00:39:35.760
and systems and colleagues.

00:39:35.760 --> 00:39:37.740
Also similarly with the
Department of Health

00:39:37.740 --> 00:39:40.044
and NHS employers.

00:39:40.044 --> 00:39:43.533
And Ruth will say something
about the delegations.

00:39:44.400 --> 00:39:47.490
Going forward, we also have advanced dates

00:39:47.490 --> 00:39:49.860
for the strikes for
the foreseeable future.

00:39:49.860 --> 00:39:52.710
So we are looking at
this as something that

00:39:52.710 --> 00:39:57.710
we will factor into our
planning for, you know,

00:39:57.900 --> 00:40:01.702
the medium term to
longer term, potentially.

00:40:01.702 --> 00:40:04.410
In terms of public messaging,

00:40:04.410 --> 00:40:07.020
we've shared public messaging
through a variety of channels,

00:40:07.020 --> 00:40:11.460
including press releases,
spokespeople, social media,

00:40:11.460 --> 00:40:13.920
the NHS England website, NHS UK,

00:40:13.920 --> 00:40:16.080
you know, and various
other stakeholder groups,

00:40:16.080 --> 00:40:18.510
and key communication
messages have been shared

00:40:18.510 --> 00:40:22.170
via our regional teams
and trusts and ICBs,

00:40:22.170 --> 00:40:24.030
so that there's a kind of consistent

00:40:24.030 --> 00:40:26.970
but locally determined messaging.

00:40:26.970 --> 00:40:28.170
We've been asking patients

00:40:28.170 --> 00:40:32.850
to use services carefully,
wisely during industrial action,

00:40:32.850 --> 00:40:35.220
take simple steps to help to ensure

00:40:35.220 --> 00:40:38.670
that care is available to
those who need it most.

00:40:38.670 --> 00:40:41.730
And this includes the
use of the 111 online

00:40:41.730 --> 00:40:43.052
as the first port of call,

00:40:43.052 --> 00:40:48.052
and also continuing to use 999
in in emergency situations.

00:40:48.600 --> 00:40:53.600
And, you know, the public
have been mindful of this.

00:40:54.867 --> 00:40:57.030
And we've also said that, you know,

00:40:57.030 --> 00:40:59.400
we've told people if
they've not been contacted,

00:40:59.400 --> 00:41:02.201
they should attend their
appointments as planned

00:41:02.201 --> 00:41:04.080
and that, you know,

00:41:04.080 --> 00:41:06.540
we will contact them to
reschedule if necessary.

00:41:06.540 --> 00:41:08.640
And that is a message
that we continue to hold

00:41:08.640 --> 00:41:10.230
going into next week.

00:41:10.230 --> 00:41:11.520
Ruth?

00:41:11.520 --> 00:41:12.772
- Thanks very much.

00:41:12.772 --> 00:41:17.490
It is in my view, the longer
this industrial action lasts,

00:41:17.490 --> 00:41:22.490
the more damaging it is
for patients and our staff.

00:41:23.884 --> 00:41:28.230
I speak to nurses in
the wards, in A and E,

00:41:28.230 --> 00:41:31.350
in all sorts of settings
from across England,

00:41:31.350 --> 00:41:34.350
and I've spoken to nurses
on the the picket line.

00:41:34.350 --> 00:41:36.240
The decisions they've made

00:41:36.240 --> 00:41:38.310
in taking industrial action or not

00:41:38.310 --> 00:41:42.942
have been both personally
and professionally tough.

00:41:42.942 --> 00:41:47.880
The simultaneous industrial
action next week worries me.

00:41:47.880 --> 00:41:51.723
As David says, it's a
different level of challenge.

00:41:52.650 --> 00:41:56.700
It is why and it'll
always be why it is my job

00:41:56.700 --> 00:42:00.330
and the job of all senior
nurses to support nurses

00:42:00.330 --> 00:42:03.450
whether they decide to
take industrial action

00:42:03.450 --> 00:42:06.660
or whether they decide not
to take industrial action.

00:42:06.660 --> 00:42:10.630
It is our job to support all nurses.

00:42:10.630 --> 00:42:12.120
And in that vein,

00:42:12.120 --> 00:42:16.262
I want to pay tribute to
directors of nursing, to the RCN,

00:42:16.262 --> 00:42:17.790
and to NHSE colleagues

00:42:17.790 --> 00:42:21.090
for working together so professionally

00:42:21.090 --> 00:42:23.940
in these very challenging times.

00:42:23.940 --> 00:42:25.823
I'm grateful, too, to the RCN

00:42:25.823 --> 00:42:28.470
that they've kept the delegations the same

00:42:28.470 --> 00:42:30.720
as in the previous dates,

00:42:30.720 --> 00:42:34.230
but they're going to be
much more firmly applied.

00:42:34.230 --> 00:42:37.800
And I will reiterate
what I've said before,

00:42:37.800 --> 00:42:41.130
that it is vital that we do
move towards the resolution

00:42:41.130 --> 00:42:43.165
as soon as possible.

00:42:43.165 --> 00:42:46.353
The NHS and those that we
serve need that to happen.

00:42:48.330 --> 00:42:49.163
Okay.

00:42:49.163 --> 00:42:49.996
- Um, David?

00:42:49.996 --> 00:42:50.829
- Yep, thank you.

00:42:50.829 --> 00:42:51.662
Thank you, Navina.

00:42:51.662 --> 00:42:53.753
So, I think we've clearly
outlined where we are.

00:42:53.753 --> 00:42:55.830
I mean, the longer this is going on,

00:42:55.830 --> 00:42:57.585
the harder it is becoming.

00:42:57.585 --> 00:43:00.690
I think we have really focused on ensuring

00:43:00.690 --> 00:43:01.860
that we've organised ourselves

00:43:01.860 --> 00:43:03.510
in the best way that we possibly can,

00:43:03.510 --> 00:43:06.690
and I think people have exercised
strong leadership there,

00:43:06.690 --> 00:43:08.640
but I think the escalation
we're likely to see

00:43:08.640 --> 00:43:10.500
is going to make this harder, so-

00:43:10.500 --> 00:43:12.000
- [Richard] It's going
to get more challenging.

00:43:12.000 --> 00:43:13.590
- Harder and more challenging.

00:43:13.590 --> 00:43:14.699
- Okay, thank you.

00:43:14.699 --> 00:43:16.170
Thank you.

00:43:16.170 --> 00:43:17.523
Sobering update.

00:43:19.020 --> 00:43:20.970
So financial performance, Julian?

00:43:20.970 --> 00:43:24.660
- So the paper, I take, is read?

00:43:24.660 --> 00:43:25.980
- [Richard] Yeah.

00:43:25.980 --> 00:43:26.820
- As it flags,

00:43:26.820 --> 00:43:29.370
I mean, in one sense not much has changed

00:43:29.370 --> 00:43:31.110
since the report we did last month.

00:43:31.110 --> 00:43:32.790
- [Richard] Yeah.
- The thing I would say is

00:43:32.790 --> 00:43:35.370
in the month eight report, in the paper,

00:43:35.370 --> 00:43:37.110
systems at that point
was still forecasting

00:43:37.110 --> 00:43:38.550
to come in on plan.

00:43:38.550 --> 00:43:41.400
We know that we are sort
of behind trajectory

00:43:41.400 --> 00:43:43.067
at this point in the year.

00:43:43.067 --> 00:43:47.220
By the way, no surprise if
you consider the pressures

00:43:47.220 --> 00:43:50.160
the whole of the NHS has
been operating under,

00:43:50.160 --> 00:43:51.300
the fact that we've basically been running

00:43:51.300 --> 00:43:53.250
winter levels of beds in acute hospitals,

00:43:53.250 --> 00:43:56.760
in our mental health services, you know,

00:43:56.760 --> 00:43:59.040
right the way through the whole year,

00:43:59.040 --> 00:44:00.723
the impact of inflation,

00:44:04.560 --> 00:44:08.100
I mean, the industrial
situation, all sorts of things.

00:44:08.100 --> 00:44:09.000
And then, you know,

00:44:09.000 --> 00:44:12.810
the sort of winter demands
we just spoke about.

00:44:12.810 --> 00:44:14.880
We are anticipating, as the paper says,

00:44:14.880 --> 00:44:17.550
that, actually, by the time
we get to the end of the year

00:44:17.550 --> 00:44:19.740
we will see in aggregate systems

00:44:19.740 --> 00:44:22.506
five to 600 million in deficit.

00:44:22.506 --> 00:44:24.240
I think that is where we will be.

00:44:24.240 --> 00:44:25.620
We have been having conversations

00:44:25.620 --> 00:44:28.230
with every system pretty much
across the country as to,

00:44:28.230 --> 00:44:31.770
you know, where they're going
to come in the end of March.

00:44:31.770 --> 00:44:33.060
We will manage that position

00:44:33.060 --> 00:44:35.370
across the whole of the kind of NHS-

00:44:35.370 --> 00:44:36.203
- [Richard] Yeah.

00:44:36.203 --> 00:44:39.450
- In part because, as we know,

00:44:39.450 --> 00:44:41.880
things like dental activity
have been running behind,

00:44:41.880 --> 00:44:44.850
in part because we have
made a lot more progress

00:44:44.850 --> 00:44:47.010
on things like the savings
we are driving out of

00:44:47.010 --> 00:44:49.110
things like high cost devices,

00:44:49.110 --> 00:44:50.490
some of our medicines spend

00:44:50.490 --> 00:44:53.670
and some of the commercial
things we have done this year,

00:44:53.670 --> 00:44:56.850
as well as the fact that we
are making progress already

00:44:56.850 --> 00:45:00.930
on reducing the size of
the national organisation,

00:45:00.930 --> 00:45:03.903
consistent with the goals
we set out in the summer.

00:45:05.790 --> 00:45:09.713
And now we are, you know,
really looking ahead to '23-'24

00:45:09.713 --> 00:45:13.050
and how we both deliver
the plans and the money

00:45:13.050 --> 00:45:13.883
for next year.

00:45:13.883 --> 00:45:17.193
And I think that really
is where our focus is now.

00:45:18.600 --> 00:45:19.530
The only other thing I'd say is

00:45:19.530 --> 00:45:22.343
on the capital side, the report flags that

00:45:22.343 --> 00:45:25.450
we will be 300 million pounds underspent.

00:45:25.450 --> 00:45:27.780
This is in large part because

00:45:27.780 --> 00:45:29.760
when we agreed the
spending review settlement,

00:45:29.760 --> 00:45:34.282
we front-loaded the investment
into diagnostic capacity.

00:45:34.282 --> 00:45:35.115
- [Richard] Yeah.

00:45:35.115 --> 00:45:37.290
- We did that so that we
basically had no excuse

00:45:37.290 --> 00:45:40.170
not to get on with increasing
it as fast as we could.

00:45:40.170 --> 00:45:42.540
We always knew we might be
likely to underspend that.

00:45:42.540 --> 00:45:43.380
We're in the process of

00:45:43.380 --> 00:45:45.060
trying to agree with the
department and the treasury

00:45:45.060 --> 00:45:47.550
that that money would be
able to flex into next year

00:45:47.550 --> 00:45:49.847
so we can still complete-

00:45:49.847 --> 00:45:50.700
- [Richard] You're hopeful of that?

00:45:50.700 --> 00:45:51.533
- The investment.

00:45:51.533 --> 00:45:52.467
Yeah, I'm, you know,

00:45:52.467 --> 00:45:56.163
I mean, it's not a done
deal, but I'm hopeful.

00:45:57.450 --> 00:46:02.340
So, you know, we can carry on
with the plans that we have

00:46:02.340 --> 00:46:04.020
for that capacity,

00:46:04.020 --> 00:46:06.360
which the NHS needs, not
just for the recovery,

00:46:06.360 --> 00:46:08.790
but, actually, for the long term.

00:46:08.790 --> 00:46:12.930
So, you know, basically everything else,

00:46:12.930 --> 00:46:14.460
we're pretty much on track to do,

00:46:14.460 --> 00:46:19.260
systems to spend the
funding and, you know,

00:46:19.260 --> 00:46:20.400
we will continue.

00:46:20.400 --> 00:46:21.233
- Okay, thank you.

00:46:21.233 --> 00:46:24.810
Any comments or questions for Julian?

00:46:24.810 --> 00:46:25.643
David?

00:46:25.643 --> 00:46:26.476
- Not for Julian.

00:46:26.476 --> 00:46:28.710
Richard, so let me pose.

00:46:28.710 --> 00:46:29.663
- No, no, pose.

00:46:31.107 --> 00:46:32.430
- So, two things.

00:46:32.430 --> 00:46:34.350
If I can just jump back
to the performance report.

00:46:34.350 --> 00:46:36.510
Can I say thank you to David Sloman

00:46:36.510 --> 00:46:38.129
for presenting as he did-
- [Richard] Yeah.

00:46:38.129 --> 00:46:40.110
- Which is just drawing out

00:46:40.110 --> 00:46:45.000
what the demand side of
the current situation is.

00:46:45.000 --> 00:46:47.430
I think a lot at this board,
we've had the supply side,

00:46:47.430 --> 00:46:50.550
but I think marrying the
demand side and the supply side

00:46:50.550 --> 00:46:51.383
is quite important,

00:46:51.383 --> 00:46:53.007
without being overly technical about this.

00:46:53.007 --> 00:46:57.150
And I found it very vivid
for him to talk about

00:46:57.150 --> 00:46:58.320
the increased numbers

00:46:58.320 --> 00:47:02.880
and to talk about scarlet fever,

00:47:02.880 --> 00:47:05.040
which was a thing of my childhood,

00:47:05.040 --> 00:47:07.638
being at the levels of the fifties,

00:47:07.638 --> 00:47:10.647
just punches through, I think,

00:47:10.647 --> 00:47:12.990
and makes a message very, very clearly

00:47:12.990 --> 00:47:15.720
that what the NHS has been dealing with

00:47:15.720 --> 00:47:18.150
is some people who've been pretty poorly,

00:47:18.150 --> 00:47:20.520
and that's the demand that has
been coming through the door.

00:47:20.520 --> 00:47:23.460
So I just want to encourage us

00:47:23.460 --> 00:47:26.070
when we do these performance
reports in the future

00:47:26.070 --> 00:47:28.530
to try and capture that
demand side narrative,

00:47:28.530 --> 00:47:31.290
not just a supply side
narrative as we go along

00:47:31.290 --> 00:47:33.840
because I hope it makes it more explicable

00:47:33.840 --> 00:47:36.240
to the wider public

00:47:36.240 --> 00:47:38.400
just exactly what's been going on,

00:47:38.400 --> 00:47:43.253
that there is a real capacity
and resilience issue here

00:47:43.253 --> 00:47:47.010
and it's not a competence issue,

00:47:47.010 --> 00:47:49.380
which is a danger it gets
interpreted in that way.

00:47:49.380 --> 00:47:51.120
So that was the first
thing I wanted to say.

00:47:51.120 --> 00:47:52.233
Second thing is,

00:47:53.730 --> 00:47:56.430
there's a lot in this report
that we didn't look at

00:47:56.430 --> 00:47:59.910
because of the urgency of managing today,

00:47:59.910 --> 00:48:03.300
but I'd really welcome if the board could

00:48:03.300 --> 00:48:07.140
spend some time on the
paragraphs on genomics.

00:48:07.140 --> 00:48:07.973
- [Richard] Yeah.

00:48:07.973 --> 00:48:09.480
- Because I think looking to the future

00:48:09.480 --> 00:48:14.040
about what science is offering us

00:48:14.040 --> 00:48:16.830
as a way of operating
differently is really important.

00:48:16.830 --> 00:48:21.830
And whilst today's challenges
are rightly highlighted,

00:48:21.990 --> 00:48:23.220
I do think, as a board,

00:48:23.220 --> 00:48:25.953
we need to keep hold of
those strategic opportunities

00:48:25.953 --> 00:48:27.540
that are coming along.

00:48:27.540 --> 00:48:29.880
And I thought there was a lot
of really important material

00:48:29.880 --> 00:48:30.960
in those paragraphs

00:48:30.960 --> 00:48:34.050
that we shouldn't really get in and out of

00:48:34.050 --> 00:48:35.190
this public board meeting

00:48:35.190 --> 00:48:37.560
without giving some oxygen and sunlight to

00:48:37.560 --> 00:48:41.640
because this is the
future as we move forward.

00:48:41.640 --> 00:48:43.620
So, sorry that sounds a bit of a speech,

00:48:43.620 --> 00:48:45.450
rather than a question, but.

00:48:45.450 --> 00:48:47.480
- I mean, it is a really good-
- Mm-hmm.

00:48:47.480 --> 00:48:50.220
- It's a report that
touches on many things

00:48:50.220 --> 00:48:53.013
that we don't actually sort of verbalise.

00:48:54.540 --> 00:48:55.740
And actually, the genomics section,

00:48:55.740 --> 00:48:58.380
paragraph 98 onwards are really important

00:48:58.380 --> 00:48:59.730
and you're quite right to identify it.

00:48:59.730 --> 00:49:01.460
And again, paragraph 99,

00:49:01.460 --> 00:49:04.830
in my reading I highlighted
and put a huge tick by it.

00:49:04.830 --> 00:49:07.309
Actually, the launch
on the 12th of October-

00:49:07.309 --> 00:49:08.142
- Yep, right.

00:49:08.142 --> 00:49:09.422
- I mean, it's an excellent thing.

00:49:09.422 --> 00:49:11.580
The question now is,
and now from here where,

00:49:11.580 --> 00:49:13.620
but, actually, it's a really good step.

00:49:13.620 --> 00:49:16.530
- If we look to our strategy
for the future genomics

00:49:16.530 --> 00:49:20.850
and maybe using some of our
deep dive opportunities,

00:49:20.850 --> 00:49:23.580
there might be an -
- [Richard] Or deep dives.

00:49:23.580 --> 00:49:24.870
- Yeah.

00:49:24.870 --> 00:49:26.490
- Thank you.

00:49:26.490 --> 00:49:29.550
No other comments or questions for Julian?

00:49:29.550 --> 00:49:32.940
Got off very lightly there.

00:49:32.940 --> 00:49:33.983
So, let's go on.

00:49:33.983 --> 00:49:36.690
I think we're on item five.

00:49:36.690 --> 00:49:38.940
Sarah Jane, I think
you're leading in on this,

00:49:38.940 --> 00:49:39.773
I think, Sarah?

00:49:39.773 --> 00:49:41.850
The "Urgent Emergency Care Recovery Plan."

00:49:41.850 --> 00:49:43.650
And just to say to you and to Julian,

00:49:43.650 --> 00:49:45.000
thank you for all the
work you've been doing.

00:49:45.000 --> 00:49:48.480
I mean, I know how intensive
it has been working very hard

00:49:48.480 --> 00:49:49.500
and with government

00:49:49.500 --> 00:49:51.909
who are also working
intensely hard on this.

00:49:51.909 --> 00:49:54.210
So, you know, number 10 and cabinet

00:49:54.210 --> 00:49:56.103
and, certainly, the department.

00:49:56.940 --> 00:49:58.200
So it's been a huge effort.

00:49:58.200 --> 00:49:59.940
But Sarah Jane, to you.

00:49:59.940 --> 00:50:00.870
- Thank you very much, Chairman,

00:50:00.870 --> 00:50:01.950
and we're delighted to be here.

00:50:01.950 --> 00:50:04.110
So I'm going to just
sort of set out the plan

00:50:04.110 --> 00:50:05.464
and our approach to it,

00:50:05.464 --> 00:50:06.570
and obviously focus quite a bit on

00:50:06.570 --> 00:50:08.010
how we move the planning to delivery,

00:50:08.010 --> 00:50:08.843
'cause I know that will be

00:50:08.843 --> 00:50:10.590
what the board's the most interested in.

00:50:10.590 --> 00:50:11.700
Julian's going to support,

00:50:11.700 --> 00:50:13.950
particularly just coming in
to talk a little bit about

00:50:13.950 --> 00:50:15.887
the way we plan to engage the
clinical community in this,

00:50:15.887 --> 00:50:17.670
'cause we are only going to deliver it

00:50:17.670 --> 00:50:20.790
if it's a whole team effort.

00:50:20.790 --> 00:50:22.320
So the first thing I just wanted to say is

00:50:22.320 --> 00:50:24.570
thank you to everybody
that's been involved in this.

00:50:24.570 --> 00:50:27.690
There has been literally
hundreds of stakeholders

00:50:27.690 --> 00:50:30.240
engaged over many months
to get us to this point.

00:50:30.240 --> 00:50:31.440
And that is all the different

00:50:31.440 --> 00:50:32.670
professional groups and bodies,

00:50:32.670 --> 00:50:36.300
and, of course, importantly,
patients and families as well.

00:50:36.300 --> 00:50:39.330
So this does really represent
the view of hundreds of people

00:50:39.330 --> 00:50:42.420
that have been put into this work,

00:50:42.420 --> 00:50:44.400
so to get us to this point.

00:50:44.400 --> 00:50:45.370
So in terms of the strategy,

00:50:45.370 --> 00:50:47.550
and I think people will recognise that

00:50:47.550 --> 00:50:50.010
the two things that we
were originally asked

00:50:50.010 --> 00:50:51.780
to address in it

00:50:51.780 --> 00:50:53.130
were the two important standards

00:50:53.130 --> 00:50:55.170
which were part of the autumn statement,

00:50:55.170 --> 00:50:58.967
which is the timeliness of
the way that we see, treat

00:50:58.967 --> 00:51:02.281
and discharge patients who
attend A and E departments

00:51:02.281 --> 00:51:06.300
so that we are in a position
to improve to 76% performance

00:51:06.300 --> 00:51:08.310
by March 24,

00:51:08.310 --> 00:51:11.310
and also to improve our
ambulance response times

00:51:11.310 --> 00:51:14.010
for category two incidents
to 30 minutes on average

00:51:14.010 --> 00:51:15.330
during the course of next year.

00:51:15.330 --> 00:51:19.230
Now, they're two really important
standards and indicators,

00:51:19.230 --> 00:51:20.100
but I think we all know,

00:51:20.100 --> 00:51:22.590
and the discussion's obviously
touched on it already,

00:51:22.590 --> 00:51:24.540
that the way to solve those two things

00:51:24.540 --> 00:51:27.060
isn't necessarily to focus on ambulances

00:51:27.060 --> 00:51:28.346
and A and E departments.

00:51:28.346 --> 00:51:32.130
We have to look much more
broadly for the solutions,

00:51:32.130 --> 00:51:34.890
and therefore really the
plan is centred around

00:51:34.890 --> 00:51:36.247
asking ourselves the question,

00:51:36.247 --> 00:51:39.270
How do we reduce the bed occupancy

00:51:39.270 --> 00:51:41.670
and create that system flow

00:51:41.670 --> 00:51:44.520
that enables our patients
to move out of A and E

00:51:44.520 --> 00:51:45.690
when they need to be admitted,

00:51:45.690 --> 00:51:48.090
that allows us to be able
to offload ambulances?

00:51:48.090 --> 00:51:50.070
And there's a lot more in the planners,

00:51:50.070 --> 00:51:51.660
everybody will see, I think,

00:51:51.660 --> 00:51:54.060
around how we create
that whole system flow

00:51:54.060 --> 00:51:55.050
and reduce occupancy.

00:51:55.050 --> 00:51:57.930
There is some discussion
in it about access route

00:51:57.930 --> 00:52:00.720
and there's a part at the end around 111,

00:52:00.720 --> 00:52:03.210
but the main body of it is about

00:52:03.210 --> 00:52:04.740
that flow and bed occupancy,

00:52:04.740 --> 00:52:06.960
'cause that is the thing that has caused

00:52:06.960 --> 00:52:09.960
all of the difficulties and
issues that David talked about

00:52:09.960 --> 00:52:12.330
in the performance report.

00:52:12.330 --> 00:52:13.320
So that's where I'd start.

00:52:13.320 --> 00:52:15.660
Before I go onto the sort
of the what part of it,

00:52:15.660 --> 00:52:18.143
I just want to really reassure everybody

00:52:18.143 --> 00:52:20.220
that we've tried to look at this

00:52:20.220 --> 00:52:23.640
down the lens of the four groups

00:52:23.640 --> 00:52:25.830
that are the most vulnerable often,

00:52:25.830 --> 00:52:26.663
but the ones that

00:52:26.663 --> 00:52:28.980
if we can get urgent and
emergency care right for,

00:52:28.980 --> 00:52:31.830
we will be getting it right
for everybody else by default.

00:52:31.830 --> 00:52:35.010
The biggest group by far
there are older people.

00:52:35.010 --> 00:52:37.680
The more we can do to
improve the quality of care

00:52:37.680 --> 00:52:40.590
that we deliver to older
people outside of hospital,

00:52:40.590 --> 00:52:42.750
reducing the chances
that they need to attend

00:52:42.750 --> 00:52:43.800
and have long waits

00:52:43.800 --> 00:52:46.140
and then potentially be also delayed

00:52:46.140 --> 00:52:47.670
in moving out of hospital,

00:52:47.670 --> 00:52:50.190
the better quality but
also the better efficiency

00:52:50.190 --> 00:52:51.510
for the system as a whole.

00:52:51.510 --> 00:52:52.770
So there's lots of stuff in here

00:52:52.770 --> 00:52:54.090
that's really through the eyes of

00:52:54.090 --> 00:52:55.770
the older people and family.

00:52:55.770 --> 00:52:57.480
The second group is
children and young people.

00:52:57.480 --> 00:53:00.480
Again, it's been referred
to in the meeting already,

00:53:00.480 --> 00:53:02.820
but young people are
often one of the reasons

00:53:02.820 --> 00:53:05.580
for the surges in demand
in our urgent care system,

00:53:05.580 --> 00:53:06.900
particularly through 111.

00:53:06.900 --> 00:53:08.550
And we know that there are ways

00:53:08.550 --> 00:53:10.710
that we can support children better.

00:53:10.710 --> 00:53:12.780
The third group is our
mental health patients,

00:53:12.780 --> 00:53:13.613
who, of course,

00:53:13.613 --> 00:53:15.837
have their own urgent and
emergency care system,

00:53:15.837 --> 00:53:17.670
and we need to get the right response

00:53:17.670 --> 00:53:18.990
to our mental health patients,

00:53:18.990 --> 00:53:20.817
and there's lots through
the report around that.

00:53:20.817 --> 00:53:23.550
And then the fourth is
the group of population

00:53:23.550 --> 00:53:25.800
that's subject to health inequalities,

00:53:25.800 --> 00:53:28.175
that are more likely to attend A and E

00:53:28.175 --> 00:53:31.890
as their primary step

00:53:31.890 --> 00:53:33.916
because they've not made
contact with other services,

00:53:33.916 --> 00:53:35.697
and the more we can do to
intensively support them,

00:53:35.697 --> 00:53:36.530
the better.

00:53:36.530 --> 00:53:38.250
And I know that's really important,

00:53:38.250 --> 00:53:40.110
because we've not approached this as

00:53:40.110 --> 00:53:43.231
just a pure sort of
efficiency-occupancy exercise.

00:53:43.231 --> 00:53:45.900
It has been about how
we look at those groups

00:53:45.900 --> 00:53:47.305
and improve quality of service

00:53:47.305 --> 00:53:49.304
and what that gives us in return

00:53:49.304 --> 00:53:53.490
with means to sort of
efficiency and effectiveness.

00:53:53.490 --> 00:53:57.360
So the plan's set out under five headings,

00:53:57.360 --> 00:54:00.120
and within each of those
headings there are a series of

00:54:00.120 --> 00:54:02.400
what we've called work
streams, work packages,

00:54:02.400 --> 00:54:04.290
that are the thing that we are
going to move to action on.

00:54:04.290 --> 00:54:06.270
I won't go through them all,

00:54:06.270 --> 00:54:08.760
but just to highlight the
sort of five key areas.

00:54:08.760 --> 00:54:10.500
The first is about increasing capacity.

00:54:10.500 --> 00:54:11.940
We've talked about it here.

00:54:11.940 --> 00:54:13.440
We know we need more physical beds,

00:54:13.440 --> 00:54:18.440
we know we need more
ambulance vehicles and staff,

00:54:18.690 --> 00:54:21.270
and we know we need to think
much more about what we can do

00:54:21.270 --> 00:54:23.010
to free up resource inside hospital

00:54:23.010 --> 00:54:25.050
by being more efficient and effective.

00:54:25.050 --> 00:54:27.425
The second group is around workforce.

00:54:27.425 --> 00:54:29.790
The third is around discharge

00:54:29.790 --> 00:54:33.180
and the NHS and social care
responsibilities there.

00:54:33.180 --> 00:54:34.200
The fourth is about

00:54:34.200 --> 00:54:36.240
avoiding people needing
to come to hospital

00:54:36.240 --> 00:54:37.590
if we can in the first place

00:54:37.590 --> 00:54:40.590
through our out of hospital
care and our virtual wards.

00:54:40.590 --> 00:54:42.720
And the fifth is around our access routes

00:54:42.720 --> 00:54:45.390
and the way we use our 111 services.

00:54:45.390 --> 00:54:47.700
So I think the plan as we've set out,

00:54:47.700 --> 00:54:48.870
those five areas, as I say,

00:54:48.870 --> 00:54:50.970
that we've broken down into 10 headings,

00:54:50.970 --> 00:54:52.440
they're the things that
we are now starting to

00:54:52.440 --> 00:54:53.380
turn our attention to.

00:54:53.380 --> 00:54:55.170
And I just want to talk a little bit about

00:54:55.170 --> 00:54:57.298
the way that we plan to do that.

00:54:57.298 --> 00:55:00.030
So before the plan was actually published,

00:55:00.030 --> 00:55:02.760
we'd already started to think
through the link between this

00:55:02.760 --> 00:55:05.160
and how we approach
planning for the year ahead.

00:55:05.160 --> 00:55:07.530
I think we are all firmly of the view that

00:55:07.530 --> 00:55:10.140
all the actions and capacity
that needs to be in place

00:55:10.140 --> 00:55:11.250
in time for next winter

00:55:11.250 --> 00:55:13.530
really needs to be there
by the end of October

00:55:13.530 --> 00:55:14.880
in the year that we're now in.

00:55:14.880 --> 00:55:16.020
We can't have a winter plan

00:55:16.020 --> 00:55:17.880
that we don't deliver 'til next February,

00:55:17.880 --> 00:55:19.290
to state the obvious.

00:55:19.290 --> 00:55:24.290
So we've got to use this
planning process for every system

00:55:24.600 --> 00:55:28.830
to essentially devise and
then deliver their version of

00:55:28.830 --> 00:55:30.990
the UEC recovery plan.

00:55:30.990 --> 00:55:33.852
So that guidance is just about to go out,

00:55:33.852 --> 00:55:36.690
and that will get every system
with the money available

00:55:36.690 --> 00:55:40.620
to think about the right mix
of capacity in their local area

00:55:40.620 --> 00:55:43.080
to meet the demands of their population.

00:55:43.080 --> 00:55:46.578
And that won't just be done
through a health and NHS lens.

00:55:46.578 --> 00:55:49.200
That's also incorporating the money

00:55:49.200 --> 00:55:52.170
that's available for
discharge into social care.

00:55:52.170 --> 00:55:55.526
So we've genuinely got a
demand and capacity plan

00:55:55.526 --> 00:55:58.410
at a system level for
health and social care.

00:55:58.410 --> 00:56:00.257
- You're talking there
predominantly, forgive me,

00:56:00.257 --> 00:56:02.190
on bed capacity rather than ambulances?

00:56:02.190 --> 00:56:03.840
Or both?
- I'm talking about

00:56:03.840 --> 00:56:06.330
all the different sorts of capacity

00:56:06.330 --> 00:56:08.970
that will help us to achieve the goal of

00:56:08.970 --> 00:56:10.230
reducing bed occupancy

00:56:10.230 --> 00:56:11.260
and improving system flow.
- [Richard] Okay.

00:56:11.260 --> 00:56:12.670
- So it will be physical beds.

00:56:12.670 --> 00:56:15.403
- [Richard] Yeah.
- It will be ambulances,

00:56:15.403 --> 00:56:16.236
it will be virtual wards.
- [Richard] Virtual wards.

00:56:16.236 --> 00:56:17.910
- It will be admissions avoidance schemes.

00:56:17.910 --> 00:56:21.270
It will be domiciliary care and care home.

00:56:21.270 --> 00:56:25.020
- So each ICB will have
its own locally tailored

00:56:25.020 --> 00:56:27.930
or locally more relevant
assessment of that

00:56:27.930 --> 00:56:30.000
and funding to build that capacity,

00:56:30.000 --> 00:56:32.454
hopefully in time for, as
you say, by end October?

00:56:32.454 --> 00:56:36.690
- That is the goal of the planning round.

00:56:36.690 --> 00:56:39.330
Now obviously, as those plans emerge,

00:56:39.330 --> 00:56:42.010
in some places we envision that
we will obviously see gaps.

00:56:42.010 --> 00:56:43.500
- [Richard] Yeah.
- But what we want to do

00:56:43.500 --> 00:56:46.320
is get a shared understanding
of what those gaps are,

00:56:46.320 --> 00:56:48.510
particularly where they
might sit at the interface

00:56:48.510 --> 00:56:49.560
between health and care.

00:56:49.560 --> 00:56:51.430
So we are really transparent about-

00:56:51.430 --> 00:56:53.100
- [Richard] Yeah.
- How we can move.

00:56:53.100 --> 00:56:55.170
But if we don't do something about them,

00:56:55.170 --> 00:56:57.120
we will be in the same
position in that system

00:56:57.120 --> 00:56:57.953
as we go into the winter.

00:56:57.953 --> 00:57:00.960
And I think, obviously,
hitting these standards

00:57:00.960 --> 00:57:02.490
in the autumn statement are important,

00:57:02.490 --> 00:57:04.500
but we can't hit them by
hitting them really well

00:57:04.500 --> 00:57:08.640
in 70% of places, but 30%
of places are left behind.

00:57:08.640 --> 00:57:11.190
So we have to have a plan
for patients and citizens

00:57:11.190 --> 00:57:12.480
in all of those.

00:57:12.480 --> 00:57:14.970
So we're just moving to
that part of action now.

00:57:14.970 --> 00:57:17.640
I just want to stress, as Andrew said,

00:57:17.640 --> 00:57:21.030
about the accountability being
at system level to do this,

00:57:21.030 --> 00:57:21.990
of course,

00:57:21.990 --> 00:57:23.640
but also to make it really clear that

00:57:23.640 --> 00:57:24.750
every part of the system

00:57:24.750 --> 00:57:26.610
needs to do their bit
individually as well.

00:57:26.610 --> 00:57:29.130
There's not another
thing called the system.

00:57:29.130 --> 00:57:30.600
We are all the system.

00:57:30.600 --> 00:57:31.800
So it makes it really clear

00:57:31.800 --> 00:57:34.080
what we expect of ambulance trusts,

00:57:34.080 --> 00:57:35.070
of acute trusts,

00:57:35.070 --> 00:57:36.720
of community and mental health providers,

00:57:36.720 --> 00:57:39.450
of local authority colleagues and so on.

00:57:39.450 --> 00:57:40.860
What we are trying to do now is

00:57:40.860 --> 00:57:42.810
build a leadership community around this.

00:57:42.810 --> 00:57:44.760
Yes, we obviously will
do the planning round,

00:57:44.760 --> 00:57:47.820
we've got the technical
guidance and so on,

00:57:47.820 --> 00:57:50.220
but we also need to ignite all the energy

00:57:50.220 --> 00:57:51.330
that people have got for this,

00:57:51.330 --> 00:57:53.640
because I think it's
back to what Amanda said.

00:57:53.640 --> 00:57:55.110
Yes, it's challenging,

00:57:55.110 --> 00:57:57.150
but it's really the thing that

00:57:57.150 --> 00:57:59.130
people want to get behind and do

00:57:59.130 --> 00:58:01.800
because it genuinely improves quality

00:58:01.800 --> 00:58:03.390
at the same time as it improves

00:58:03.390 --> 00:58:04.470
efficiency and effectiveness.

00:58:04.470 --> 00:58:05.520
And that's the sort of thing that

00:58:05.520 --> 00:58:07.770
gets the managerial and the
clinical leadership community

00:58:07.770 --> 00:58:08.603
coming together,

00:58:08.603 --> 00:58:11.340
'cause we all want to
achieve the same things.

00:58:11.340 --> 00:58:13.200
So I'll pause there and
I'll just hand to Julian,

00:58:13.200 --> 00:58:15.150
just to say a few words,
'cause Julian will provide,

00:58:15.150 --> 00:58:16.710
we almost are the example, aren't we?

00:58:16.710 --> 00:58:18.150
The managerial and clinical leadership

00:58:18.150 --> 00:58:20.340
that we're looking to see down the system,

00:58:20.340 --> 00:58:21.173
it's Julian and I.

00:58:21.173 --> 00:58:22.160
So, Julian,

00:58:22.160 --> 00:58:23.250
do you want to talk a little
bit about the clinical?

00:58:23.250 --> 00:58:24.630
- No, thank you very much, Sarah Jane.

00:58:24.630 --> 00:58:27.210
And I think the important
part to this plan is

00:58:27.210 --> 00:58:28.710
the development of it started very early,

00:58:28.710 --> 00:58:30.780
and Steve Powis was very much involved in

00:58:30.780 --> 00:58:33.210
helping us develop that
clinical engagement

00:58:33.210 --> 00:58:35.340
to really get all parts of the system

00:58:35.340 --> 00:58:37.740
thinking about how this could be improved.

00:58:37.740 --> 00:58:40.260
So I do think we've got
really good ownership

00:58:40.260 --> 00:58:44.610
around the solutions that
we're putting into the plan.

00:58:44.610 --> 00:58:46.023
And I think that's really important,

00:58:46.023 --> 00:58:47.880
that the clinical community,

00:58:47.880 --> 00:58:49.590
I mean the whole broad clinical community,

00:58:49.590 --> 00:58:51.840
have really welcomed this focus

00:58:51.840 --> 00:58:54.267
and the areas that we're
focusing on at the moment.

00:58:54.267 --> 00:58:56.430
And I think the important
part here is also

00:58:56.430 --> 00:58:58.530
how we develop that
fellowship and leadership.

00:58:58.530 --> 00:59:01.830
How do we bring together for
the acutes and communities,

00:59:01.830 --> 00:59:03.540
the triumvirate we often talk about,

00:59:03.540 --> 00:59:06.150
which is the sort of head
manager, the head clinician,

00:59:06.150 --> 00:59:07.110
the head nurse,

00:59:07.110 --> 00:59:09.837
coming together to start
to sort those problems out.

00:59:09.837 --> 00:59:13.440
And I do think that as we
get into the system working

00:59:13.440 --> 00:59:15.660
and all parts of the system,
as Sarah Jane has said,

00:59:15.660 --> 00:59:16.590
coming together,

00:59:16.590 --> 00:59:19.023
will be absolutely vital
to deliver this plan.

00:59:21.120 --> 00:59:22.049
- Very good.

00:59:22.049 --> 00:59:23.340
Thank you.

00:59:23.340 --> 00:59:24.330
Comments, questions?

00:59:24.330 --> 00:59:25.671
Mary?

00:59:25.671 --> 00:59:27.930
- I think the plan is great,

00:59:27.930 --> 00:59:30.570
and, you know, as a mental health nurse,

00:59:30.570 --> 00:59:32.460
I'm thrilled about the new ambulances

00:59:32.460 --> 00:59:36.300
and the focus on mental health issues.

00:59:36.300 --> 00:59:40.199
I'm just wondering
whether you've considered

00:59:40.199 --> 00:59:45.199
really intensive
rehabilitation on discharge,

00:59:45.240 --> 00:59:46.710
because when I read it,

00:59:46.710 --> 00:59:50.400
it looks as if we might
get people out of hospital,

00:59:50.400 --> 00:59:52.720
but they might be coming
back fairly quickly

00:59:53.580 --> 00:59:56.700
if we're not got interprofessional teams

00:59:56.700 --> 00:59:58.200
focusing on rehabilitation,

00:59:58.200 --> 01:00:01.050
particularly after
something like, you know,

01:00:01.050 --> 01:00:02.553
fractured hip or femur.

01:00:04.050 --> 01:00:05.093
- I'm very happy to answer.

01:00:06.090 --> 01:00:08.360
We feel massively passionate about this

01:00:08.360 --> 01:00:11.070
in the urgent and emergency
care and intermediate care teams

01:00:11.070 --> 01:00:14.426
because if we can get it right
at the point of discharge,

01:00:14.426 --> 01:00:17.160
actually, we're not only
avoiding an admission,

01:00:17.160 --> 01:00:19.740
but we are helping that
person to live their best life

01:00:19.740 --> 01:00:22.050
and to make sure that we
don't institutionalise them

01:00:22.050 --> 01:00:24.990
because we haven't provided
the support that's needed.

01:00:24.990 --> 01:00:28.410
So there's a section in the
plan around intermediate care,

01:00:28.410 --> 01:00:30.300
and this is going to be a really big focus

01:00:30.300 --> 01:00:31.920
over the next 18 months or so

01:00:31.920 --> 01:00:34.560
about how we can get that four weeks right

01:00:34.560 --> 01:00:36.690
when a patient first leaves hospital,

01:00:36.690 --> 01:00:38.490
get that intensive rehabilitation

01:00:38.490 --> 01:00:40.590
and reablement support to them.

01:00:40.590 --> 01:00:43.200
And then if they do need any
ongoing care beyond that,

01:00:43.200 --> 01:00:46.110
we're making assessments
for that ongoing care

01:00:46.110 --> 01:00:48.150
at the right time and the right place.

01:00:48.150 --> 01:00:50.400
So absolutely, it's going to be a focus.

01:00:50.400 --> 01:00:51.570
I would say it's an area,

01:00:51.570 --> 01:00:53.520
from a workforce planning perspective,

01:00:53.520 --> 01:00:55.313
we need to put more focus and priority on.

01:00:55.313 --> 01:00:57.420
I think we do need to
make sure that we've got

01:00:57.420 --> 01:01:00.480
some of those services
sort of building over time.

01:01:00.480 --> 01:01:02.220
But we know there's lots
we can do about new roles.

01:01:02.220 --> 01:01:04.290
We've been talking to Navina's team.

01:01:04.290 --> 01:01:05.123
But absolutely,

01:01:05.123 --> 01:01:07.890
and I really think this is the return of

01:01:07.890 --> 01:01:11.490
the era of intermediate care
and a focus on rehabilitation

01:01:11.490 --> 01:01:14.160
that we've perhaps not
had for a few years.

01:01:14.160 --> 01:01:16.860
So I look forward to
updating you on it, Mary.

01:01:16.860 --> 01:01:18.237
- [Mary] Thank you very much.

01:01:18.237 --> 01:01:19.162
- [Richard] Andrew?

01:01:19.162 --> 01:01:21.180
- So it's great plan and,

01:01:21.180 --> 01:01:23.104
I mean, just to reflect on the fact that

01:01:23.104 --> 01:01:27.121
all of these initiatives here
already exist in the NHS,

01:01:27.121 --> 01:01:29.900
so we're not taking flyers.

01:01:29.900 --> 01:01:32.730
They're there, they're
established and working,

01:01:32.730 --> 01:01:33.900
which is great.

01:01:33.900 --> 01:01:35.367
I suppose when I just stand back here

01:01:35.367 --> 01:01:38.520
and just look at the
workforce implications,

01:01:38.520 --> 01:01:41.940
that would be the kind of
rate limiter I would suggest,

01:01:41.940 --> 01:01:44.520
given that, you know,
we've got problem now

01:01:44.520 --> 01:01:47.610
with vacancy rates at around 10%.

01:01:47.610 --> 01:01:50.850
So if we need another, I
don't know what this comes to,

01:01:50.850 --> 01:01:55.050
five, 6,000 people across the NHS,

01:01:55.050 --> 01:01:57.660
that's an an additional requirement

01:01:57.660 --> 01:02:00.150
on an already, you know, stretched system.

01:02:00.150 --> 01:02:03.360
So just be good to hear your reflections

01:02:03.360 --> 01:02:05.730
on how you think we can best meet that.

01:02:05.730 --> 01:02:06.900
- So, clearly,

01:02:06.900 --> 01:02:08.850
there's more we need to do
in the medium to long term.

01:02:08.850 --> 01:02:11.190
We talked about that at the
beginning of the meeting,

01:02:11.190 --> 01:02:13.680
but we have worked this plan out

01:02:13.680 --> 01:02:16.050
on the basis of what we think is possible

01:02:16.050 --> 01:02:19.380
with either the workforce we already have

01:02:19.380 --> 01:02:21.780
or those that we can train quickly

01:02:21.780 --> 01:02:25.830
in the case of some of
the ambulance technicians.

01:02:25.830 --> 01:02:28.830
So certainly on the physical beds,

01:02:28.830 --> 01:02:31.770
we've done a a lot under
Pauline's leadership

01:02:31.770 --> 01:02:33.720
to really get additional beds in place

01:02:33.720 --> 01:02:36.240
over the course of the last few months.

01:02:36.240 --> 01:02:38.610
But we don't think that
that's necessarily been done

01:02:38.610 --> 01:02:39.600
in a sustainable way

01:02:39.600 --> 01:02:41.430
because of the way we've
had to do it really quickly.

01:02:41.430 --> 01:02:43.620
So we've not necessarily
created the new wards,

01:02:43.620 --> 01:02:46.260
converted areas and so
on we've added on here.

01:02:46.260 --> 01:02:49.920
So, the staff to provide
that care is there,

01:02:49.920 --> 01:02:51.990
but it's not necessarily organised

01:02:51.990 --> 01:02:54.000
or in a way that helps it to, you know,

01:02:54.000 --> 01:02:57.030
be the most efficient and
effective for the patients

01:02:57.030 --> 01:02:58.410
that we're serving.

01:02:58.410 --> 01:03:00.870
So we've scaled it on that basis,

01:03:00.870 --> 01:03:03.780
back to the kind of it's
ambitious but realistic.

01:03:03.780 --> 01:03:05.095
I think to go beyond that,

01:03:05.095 --> 01:03:07.530
we absolutely need to obviously be aligned

01:03:07.530 --> 01:03:09.570
to the workforce plan and so on.

01:03:09.570 --> 01:03:11.460
But for everything that's in here,

01:03:11.460 --> 01:03:13.812
we have got a path to
be able to deliver it

01:03:13.812 --> 01:03:16.230
over the course of the next 12 months.

01:03:16.230 --> 01:03:17.910
Otherwise, we've not put it in here

01:03:17.910 --> 01:03:19.740
'cause it has to be in place

01:03:19.740 --> 01:03:22.770
and delivered within that time period.

01:03:22.770 --> 01:03:23.861
- Thank you.

01:03:23.861 --> 01:03:28.140
I mean, the comment you made about,

01:03:28.140 --> 01:03:31.380
I'm going back to greater
provision of intermediate care.

01:03:31.380 --> 01:03:34.980
I mean, what do I know,
but to me, reading it,

01:03:34.980 --> 01:03:36.314
it sounds very logical.

01:03:36.314 --> 01:03:37.338
- [Sarah Jane] Mm-hmm.

01:03:37.338 --> 01:03:38.970
- Actually that we do build
out that intermediate capacity

01:03:38.970 --> 01:03:42.600
and then think in stages about
the levels of care provisions

01:03:42.600 --> 01:03:45.658
that are offered and then
perhaps enhanced or increased

01:03:45.658 --> 01:03:48.930
slightly later down the process.

01:03:48.930 --> 01:03:50.880
But it strikes me that's very logical.

01:03:50.880 --> 01:03:52.720
Can I ask you a question about

01:03:53.730 --> 01:03:56.999
to what extent in the
challenges of discharge-

01:03:56.999 --> 01:03:58.665
- [Sarah Jane] Mm-hmm.
- To what extent do you think

01:03:58.665 --> 01:04:01.440
it's a standardisation or process issue?

01:04:01.440 --> 01:04:04.200
How much improvement can we get

01:04:04.200 --> 01:04:09.200
if we had standardised
processes in all of our acutes?

01:04:09.690 --> 01:04:13.080
- There is a lot of potential and scope

01:04:13.080 --> 01:04:14.910
in the standardisation of processes.

01:04:14.910 --> 01:04:16.560
And this is one of the
things that's in the plan

01:04:16.560 --> 01:04:17.490
under the heading of

01:04:17.490 --> 01:04:19.680
transfer of care hubs.
- [Richard] Yeah, yeah.

01:04:19.680 --> 01:04:21.000
- We've been doing quite a lot of work

01:04:21.000 --> 01:04:22.590
with a few places across the country,

01:04:22.590 --> 01:04:24.570
including our discharge frontrunners,

01:04:24.570 --> 01:04:27.090
to really understand
what works in this area.

01:04:27.090 --> 01:04:29.803
It's a little bit like the
system control centres model.

01:04:29.803 --> 01:04:33.600
The more that we can do to
have shared information,

01:04:33.600 --> 01:04:35.700
shared data, decision making,

01:04:35.700 --> 01:04:38.910
to have social care,
social workers on site,

01:04:38.910 --> 01:04:41.400
social care providers
helping us to provide

01:04:41.400 --> 01:04:43.740
sort of care navigation
for people in hospitals

01:04:43.740 --> 01:04:46.320
so we are finding those
placements for people

01:04:46.320 --> 01:04:48.990
before they actually
become ready for discharge,

01:04:48.990 --> 01:04:51.090
all those things make
a massive difference.

01:04:51.090 --> 01:04:53.460
And one of the things that
we've committed to is to

01:04:53.460 --> 01:04:56.790
providing a sort of specification for

01:04:56.790 --> 01:05:00.096
what a transfer of care hub
should look and work like.

01:05:00.096 --> 01:05:02.183
Not having to say
precisely who works in it-

01:05:02.183 --> 01:05:03.016
- [Richard] Yeah.

01:05:03.016 --> 01:05:04.227
- But the sorts of things
that need to happen in there

01:05:04.227 --> 01:05:07.200
and particularly having autonomous
clinical decision makers.

01:05:07.200 --> 01:05:09.287
So there's definitely scope.

01:05:09.287 --> 01:05:11.040
The way I always describe it is

01:05:11.040 --> 01:05:13.320
we need to get demand
and capacity aligned,

01:05:13.320 --> 01:05:15.000
but unless you've got process excellence

01:05:15.000 --> 01:05:16.350
going between the two,

01:05:16.350 --> 01:05:17.760
people can still still get stuck,

01:05:17.760 --> 01:05:19.230
even if the capacity's there,

01:05:19.230 --> 01:05:20.880
unless you've got the process excellence

01:05:20.880 --> 01:05:22.530
to link those two together.

01:05:22.530 --> 01:05:25.230
So I think that's another
big part of this as well.

01:05:25.230 --> 01:05:26.787
- And if I just probe a bit further,

01:05:26.787 --> 01:05:28.295
and this may be completely wrong,

01:05:28.295 --> 01:05:30.460
do we find there's a sort of

01:05:31.680 --> 01:05:36.150
an activity fall off at
weekends in discharge?

01:05:36.150 --> 01:05:38.224
And if so, is that going to get alleviated

01:05:38.224 --> 01:05:39.333
through this plan?

01:05:39.333 --> 01:05:40.740
- So, that's correct.
- In other words,

01:05:40.740 --> 01:05:43.540
the interconnectivity
all across the system.

01:05:43.540 --> 01:05:44.670
- Yeah, so that's correct.

01:05:44.670 --> 01:05:47.310
There are fewer discharges at the weekend.

01:05:47.310 --> 01:05:50.700
We know there are things
that certain places do

01:05:50.700 --> 01:05:52.530
where we can actually
enhance that discharge

01:05:52.530 --> 01:05:54.450
in a cost effective way.

01:05:54.450 --> 01:05:56.070
And there's lots of
examples of doing that.

01:05:56.070 --> 01:05:56.903
For example,

01:05:56.903 --> 01:05:59.310
being able to discharge into
a care home at a weekend

01:05:59.310 --> 01:06:01.260
if that care home knows that
it's going to be able to get

01:06:01.260 --> 01:06:02.190
primary care support

01:06:02.190 --> 01:06:03.570
would be a good example of

01:06:03.570 --> 01:06:05.550
how we can make that seven days a week.

01:06:05.550 --> 01:06:07.890
However, just to be really honest,

01:06:07.890 --> 01:06:09.870
it does hit a kind of threshold,

01:06:09.870 --> 01:06:12.630
'cause to have a genuine seven day service

01:06:12.630 --> 01:06:15.240
in everything that's in
NHS and NHS social care

01:06:15.240 --> 01:06:17.970
isn't something that
we're set up or scaled

01:06:17.970 --> 01:06:18.803
to be able to do.

01:06:18.803 --> 01:06:21.090
The workforce implications
of that are huge.

01:06:21.090 --> 01:06:22.590
So we do need to be realistic that

01:06:22.590 --> 01:06:25.770
we won't always be able to do
everything on an even basis,

01:06:25.770 --> 01:06:28.023
seven days a week, but
there's a lot more we can do

01:06:28.023 --> 01:06:29.970
with the resources that we've already got.

01:06:29.970 --> 01:06:32.447
And again, that's part of this work.

01:06:32.447 --> 01:06:33.513
- Thank you.

01:06:34.646 --> 01:06:35.490
I'm going to pause here

01:06:35.490 --> 01:06:38.820
if there are any other comments
or questions on this topic.

01:06:38.820 --> 01:06:40.230
And if not, Pauline,

01:06:40.230 --> 01:06:42.573
I just wanted to repeat
the thanks, actually,

01:06:43.680 --> 01:06:45.092
from the start of the board,

01:06:45.092 --> 01:06:47.975
because a lot of what Sarah
Jane has been talking about

01:06:47.975 --> 01:06:50.160
builds on your shoulders, actually,

01:06:50.160 --> 01:06:51.540
builds on the work that you've done.

01:06:51.540 --> 01:06:54.420
So again, thank you for all
you've done in this space.

01:06:54.420 --> 01:06:55.380
- [Pauline] Could I just say one thing?

01:06:55.380 --> 01:06:56.213
- And your team.

01:06:57.840 --> 01:07:00.120
- So if I could just make a comment,

01:07:00.120 --> 01:07:02.310
and I think as Sarah Jane has said,

01:07:02.310 --> 01:07:04.560
a lot of what's in this plan,

01:07:04.560 --> 01:07:06.780
the new models of care behind it,

01:07:06.780 --> 01:07:08.610
the transformation, et cetera,

01:07:08.610 --> 01:07:11.310
has been developed, you
know, over a period of time

01:07:11.310 --> 01:07:13.500
and it's really to just acknowledge

01:07:13.500 --> 01:07:16.920
all of the staff out there
who developed those models.

01:07:16.920 --> 01:07:19.560
Clearly there's variation
in implementation,

01:07:19.560 --> 01:07:21.916
but I think it's really
to acknowledge people

01:07:21.916 --> 01:07:23.850
right across all services.

01:07:23.850 --> 01:07:26.130
In particular, I'd like
to acknowledge staff

01:07:26.130 --> 01:07:28.770
who have been working in
emergency medicine, et cetera,

01:07:28.770 --> 01:07:30.690
who have been behind quite a bit of it.

01:07:30.690 --> 01:07:31.830
Thank you.

01:07:31.830 --> 01:07:32.663
- Very good.

01:07:33.660 --> 01:07:37.321
So I'm going to move us on
to the next agenda item.

01:07:37.321 --> 01:07:41.631
Claire, I think you're going to introduce

01:07:41.631 --> 01:07:44.117
mental health transformation programme.

01:07:44.117 --> 01:07:46.089
- I am, and thank you so much.

01:07:46.089 --> 01:07:47.760
And obviously people
will have read the paper.

01:07:47.760 --> 01:07:49.890
I would just like to set
a tiny bit of context,

01:07:49.890 --> 01:07:53.310
which is this particular
transformation programme

01:07:53.310 --> 01:07:56.580
is part of the overall long term plan.

01:07:56.580 --> 01:07:59.873
It's 1 billion pounds
worth, in monetary terms,

01:07:59.873 --> 01:08:03.300
of the 2.3 billion pounds commitment.

01:08:03.300 --> 01:08:06.210
And we've promised we'd
see 2 million more people

01:08:06.210 --> 01:08:11.210
in different services by
the end of the period.

01:08:11.340 --> 01:08:15.593
The other areas are crisis,
children and young people-

01:08:15.593 --> 01:08:16.426
- [Richard] Yeah.

01:08:16.426 --> 01:08:17.790
- And common mental health problems.

01:08:17.790 --> 01:08:20.040
This community transformation

01:08:20.040 --> 01:08:23.610
is the absolute cornerstone of service

01:08:23.610 --> 01:08:27.930
for about half a million
people in specialist services

01:08:27.930 --> 01:08:31.290
living with SMI, serious
mental health problems.

01:08:31.290 --> 01:08:32.578
And I did just want to say

01:08:32.578 --> 01:08:36.188
that refers to people
who might have bipolar,

01:08:36.188 --> 01:08:38.640
what was called personality disorder,

01:08:38.640 --> 01:08:40.833
now complex emotional needs,

01:08:41.970 --> 01:08:45.000
schizophrenia, psychosis
and so on and so forth.

01:08:45.000 --> 01:08:46.290
I thought I should bring to life

01:08:46.290 --> 01:08:47.760
who we're talking about actually.

01:08:47.760 --> 01:08:48.749
- [Richard] Right.

01:08:48.749 --> 01:08:51.030
- So it's a very major
part of our delivery,

01:08:51.030 --> 01:08:56.030
and it's hugely intended
to close the gap between

01:08:57.018 --> 01:09:00.600
specialists and primary care services

01:09:00.600 --> 01:09:03.660
so that we can detect
earlier, intervene earlier,

01:09:03.660 --> 01:09:06.196
and wrap care around individuals,

01:09:06.196 --> 01:09:10.560
too many of whom die
prematurely as a result, also,

01:09:10.560 --> 01:09:12.870
of physical health problems

01:09:12.870 --> 01:09:15.090
linked with their serious mental illness.

01:09:15.090 --> 01:09:18.120
So it's a big integration programme,

01:09:18.120 --> 01:09:20.730
really trying to challenge inequalities

01:09:20.730 --> 01:09:24.210
and give people a better,
more stable quality of life.

01:09:24.210 --> 01:09:26.777
Some of the system benefits,
if we get that right,

01:09:26.777 --> 01:09:31.777
are service users will relapse less often,

01:09:31.860 --> 01:09:33.900
go into crisis less often,

01:09:33.900 --> 01:09:37.320
lose their housing and
other huge disruptors

01:09:37.320 --> 01:09:39.150
to a stable quality of life.

01:09:39.150 --> 01:09:41.850
And we also, as far as we possibly could,

01:09:41.850 --> 01:09:43.980
developed it in co-production with

01:09:43.980 --> 01:09:48.240
an enormous amount of service
users and professionals

01:09:48.240 --> 01:09:51.690
and with Simon Wessely, actually,

01:09:51.690 --> 01:09:55.080
to try and anticipate
some of the key changes

01:09:55.080 --> 01:09:57.930
that a new mental health act might spell

01:09:57.930 --> 01:10:01.742
for doing things differently
in the community.

01:10:01.742 --> 01:10:05.910
So I really want to hand
over now to, if I might,

01:10:05.910 --> 01:10:09.330
my colleagues who will,
I hope, bring to life

01:10:09.330 --> 01:10:12.225
how this looks in the real
world of frontline delivery,

01:10:12.225 --> 01:10:14.247
how care is localised

01:10:14.247 --> 01:10:18.210
and how we work as one
team across the country.

01:10:18.210 --> 01:10:19.043
- I'd be really grateful

01:10:19.043 --> 01:10:21.088
if you'd introduce your
colleagues, Claire.

01:10:21.088 --> 01:10:21.921
That would be great.
- [Claire] Thank you,

01:10:21.921 --> 01:10:22.754
I would love to.

01:10:22.754 --> 01:10:24.120
- And you should expect Simon, I suspect,

01:10:24.120 --> 01:10:26.320
to come in at some stage
in this conversation.

01:10:26.320 --> 01:10:27.153
- Thank you.

01:10:27.153 --> 01:10:29.022
I would be disappointed if he didn't.

01:10:29.022 --> 01:10:29.855
Thank you.

01:10:29.855 --> 01:10:32.790
Can I hand over first to Sam,
who'll introduce herself,

01:10:32.790 --> 01:10:34.770
and then you'll handover to Adam?

01:10:34.770 --> 01:10:35.603
- Yeah.

01:10:35.603 --> 01:10:36.627
- So, thank you.

01:10:36.627 --> 01:10:37.460
- Good afternoon, everybody.

01:10:37.460 --> 01:10:38.293
I'm Sam Allen.

01:10:38.293 --> 01:10:39.810
I'm the Chief Executive of
the North East North Cumbria

01:10:39.810 --> 01:10:40.643
Integrated Care Board.

01:10:40.643 --> 01:10:42.360
It's a pleasure to be here this afternoon.

01:10:42.360 --> 01:10:44.580
I just thought I'd start, really,

01:10:44.580 --> 01:10:47.970
this will be stuff that our
experts around the table know,

01:10:47.970 --> 01:10:49.980
but in terms of us as an
integrated care board,

01:10:49.980 --> 01:10:52.830
you'll know that we've got
sort four key aims, really.

01:10:52.830 --> 01:10:55.110
Improving the health of our population

01:10:55.110 --> 01:10:56.760
by improving their outcomes,

01:10:56.760 --> 01:10:59.670
really tackling inequalities,

01:10:59.670 --> 01:11:01.980
enhancing productivity and value for money

01:11:01.980 --> 01:11:03.390
and really supporting that sort of

01:11:03.390 --> 01:11:05.370
social and economic development.

01:11:05.370 --> 01:11:08.220
So over the last seven
months, I can tell you,

01:11:08.220 --> 01:11:11.460
we have absolutely been
working relentlessly on that,

01:11:11.460 --> 01:11:14.790
alongside addressing some of
the operational challenges

01:11:14.790 --> 01:11:16.740
that you've heard about today.

01:11:16.740 --> 01:11:18.553
In terms of mental health,

01:11:18.553 --> 01:11:19.980
yesterday, in fact,

01:11:19.980 --> 01:11:21.870
our integrated care board published our

01:11:21.870 --> 01:11:23.850
10 year integrated care strategy.

01:11:23.850 --> 01:11:25.365
So we've worked really closely with

01:11:25.365 --> 01:11:26.820
our 13 local authorities,

01:11:26.820 --> 01:11:28.920
our directors of public
health, with housing,

01:11:28.920 --> 01:11:31.920
the community and voluntary
sector, with citizens.

01:11:31.920 --> 01:11:34.500
We had 400 individual pieces of feedback

01:11:34.500 --> 01:11:36.720
from our population about our strategy.

01:11:36.720 --> 01:11:39.690
And it won't surprise you that
there is a chapter in there

01:11:39.690 --> 01:11:40.837
which is simply titled,

01:11:40.837 --> 01:11:43.500
"There is No Health
Without Mental Health."

01:11:43.500 --> 01:11:45.270
Now, just to paint a
little bit of a picture

01:11:45.270 --> 01:11:47.460
around the North East and North Cumbria,

01:11:47.460 --> 01:11:49.530
about a third of our population

01:11:49.530 --> 01:11:52.500
live in areas of high deprivation.

01:11:52.500 --> 01:11:55.710
Nearly three in five
children live in poverty.

01:11:55.710 --> 01:11:56.543
And whilst we've got

01:11:56.543 --> 01:11:59.270
some of the leading healthcare
services, I'd like to say,

01:11:59.270 --> 01:12:00.450
in the country,

01:12:00.450 --> 01:12:02.340
delivering excellent outcomes,

01:12:02.340 --> 01:12:03.450
we know when it comes to

01:12:03.450 --> 01:12:05.670
improving the health of our population,

01:12:05.670 --> 01:12:07.980
it is not the sole domain
of health services.

01:12:07.980 --> 01:12:10.590
And that's the whole
reason why you created us.

01:12:10.590 --> 01:12:13.310
And I think that's where
our exciting opportunity is.

01:12:13.310 --> 01:12:17.190
So the transformation of
community mental health services

01:12:17.190 --> 01:12:19.650
is absolutely at the
heart of what we're doing.

01:12:19.650 --> 01:12:22.440
And why that's even more important

01:12:22.440 --> 01:12:25.020
when we're developing our strategy is

01:12:25.020 --> 01:12:27.300
the leading cause of death in our region

01:12:27.300 --> 01:12:31.560
for men age 15 to 49
and women age 20 to 34

01:12:31.560 --> 01:12:35.670
isn't respiratory illness,
isn't cancer, it is suicide.

01:12:35.670 --> 01:12:38.070
And actually, for men in our region,

01:12:38.070 --> 01:12:40.440
the years of quality of life lived

01:12:40.440 --> 01:12:42.930
starts to decline at the age of 60.

01:12:42.930 --> 01:12:45.719
So we've got some really
significant work to do.

01:12:45.719 --> 01:12:47.820
I know Professor Wessely will know this,

01:12:47.820 --> 01:12:50.820
but mental health costs the economy

01:12:50.820 --> 01:12:53.550
about 117 billion pounds a year.

01:12:53.550 --> 01:12:55.707
That's about 5% of the UK GDP,

01:12:55.707 --> 01:12:57.780
and about three quarters of that

01:12:57.780 --> 01:12:58.747
results in lost productivity.

01:12:58.747 --> 01:13:01.560
For us in our region, that's 39 billion.

01:13:01.560 --> 01:13:03.690
So when we think about the link between

01:13:03.690 --> 01:13:05.610
health and mental health,

01:13:05.610 --> 01:13:07.560
between health and the economy,

01:13:07.560 --> 01:13:10.650
there is a very, very strong business case

01:13:10.650 --> 01:13:12.120
to do this well and do it right.

01:13:12.120 --> 01:13:15.500
So locating the transformation
of community services

01:13:15.500 --> 01:13:17.280
is actually at the heart of what we do,

01:13:17.280 --> 01:13:19.320
we've touched on some of
the workforce pressures

01:13:19.320 --> 01:13:21.810
felt right across the health service.

01:13:21.810 --> 01:13:24.420
We know that we've got
some real supply issues

01:13:24.420 --> 01:13:28.320
around mental health nurses,
around psychiatrists.

01:13:28.320 --> 01:13:30.690
So I think one of the things
that mental health services

01:13:30.690 --> 01:13:31.530
have really innovated,

01:13:31.530 --> 01:13:34.530
and this programme I think
has been very leading in,

01:13:34.530 --> 01:13:37.410
is looking at how we work
far more closely with

01:13:37.410 --> 01:13:38.550
the community and voluntary sector.

01:13:38.550 --> 01:13:39.720
'cause quite simply,

01:13:39.720 --> 01:13:42.210
they're better at doing some
of the stuff than we are.

01:13:42.210 --> 01:13:43.230
And quite simply,

01:13:43.230 --> 01:13:47.100
they're much better about
putting people in touch with

01:13:47.100 --> 01:13:49.500
everything that sits in
neighbourhoods and communities

01:13:49.500 --> 01:13:51.240
to support their mental health.

01:13:51.240 --> 01:13:53.670
So the programme we've been embarking on,

01:13:53.670 --> 01:13:55.655
we've got 64 primary care networks

01:13:55.655 --> 01:13:58.050
in our integrated care system,

01:13:58.050 --> 01:14:02.749
we've been working around
extending the additional roles

01:14:02.749 --> 01:14:04.500
into primary care

01:14:04.500 --> 01:14:06.990
and not seeing this as a silo development,

01:14:06.990 --> 01:14:09.180
but absolutely integral
to the implementation of

01:14:09.180 --> 01:14:10.788
the fuller stock take.

01:14:10.788 --> 01:14:11.850
I think in terms of

01:14:11.850 --> 01:14:15.390
what our community and voluntary
sector providers offer us

01:14:15.390 --> 01:14:19.607
is a workforce, a completely
untapped workforce,

01:14:19.607 --> 01:14:23.344
which is they're able to recruit quicker,

01:14:23.344 --> 01:14:25.380
they're far more agile.

01:14:25.380 --> 01:14:26.880
So developing those partnerships

01:14:26.880 --> 01:14:29.490
in our community volunteer
sector is critical.

01:14:29.490 --> 01:14:30.870
And as integrated care system,

01:14:30.870 --> 01:14:34.830
we've invested in an
infrastructure organisation.

01:14:34.830 --> 01:14:36.870
So they've sat around our
integrated care board,

01:14:36.870 --> 01:14:38.490
they're working closely with us.

01:14:38.490 --> 01:14:41.280
It's not to say we've
got more to do, we do.

01:14:41.280 --> 01:14:45.510
Digital data infrastructure
is, it'd be fair to say,

01:14:45.510 --> 01:14:46.980
relatively poor.

01:14:46.980 --> 01:14:48.750
And you'll know from your own papers,

01:14:48.750 --> 01:14:50.700
perhaps there's more we can do around

01:14:50.700 --> 01:14:53.220
sharing the outcomes for mental health

01:14:53.220 --> 01:14:55.080
and what they're delivering.

01:14:55.080 --> 01:14:56.400
But just two final points to me

01:14:56.400 --> 01:14:57.810
before I hand over to the brilliant Adam,

01:14:57.810 --> 01:15:00.000
is I think it's been brilliant

01:15:00.000 --> 01:15:02.640
that you've protected the mental
health investment standard.

01:15:02.640 --> 01:15:04.350
I think this is so critical.

01:15:04.350 --> 01:15:06.720
We cannot take our lens off of it,

01:15:06.720 --> 01:15:09.030
particularly given the
additional operational pressures

01:15:09.030 --> 01:15:10.200
we're under.

01:15:10.200 --> 01:15:13.590
And equally, whilst we
have a national framework

01:15:13.590 --> 01:15:15.360
that sets a programme,

01:15:15.360 --> 01:15:17.670
we are getting on and
delivering this in systems,

01:15:17.670 --> 01:15:19.590
it's integral to our strategies

01:15:19.590 --> 01:15:23.550
and the solutions sit in our
communities with our citizens,

01:15:23.550 --> 01:15:24.900
not necessarily around

01:15:24.900 --> 01:15:27.120
large-scale delivery plans led nationally,

01:15:27.120 --> 01:15:30.900
but the programme is really
important to set the direction,

01:15:30.900 --> 01:15:34.290
ensure that we are measuring
our success and sharing that

01:15:34.290 --> 01:15:37.350
and that also we've got the resources

01:15:37.350 --> 01:15:38.970
to enable us to deliver that.

01:15:38.970 --> 01:15:40.110
I'm going to hand over to Adam now.

01:15:40.110 --> 01:15:41.820
You'll be able to introduce yourself.

01:15:41.820 --> 01:15:42.690
- Thank you, Sam.

01:15:42.690 --> 01:15:44.130
Thank you, everyone, for having me today.

01:15:44.130 --> 01:15:45.180
My name's Adam Crampsie,

01:15:45.180 --> 01:15:47.910
I'm Chief Executive at
Mental Health Concern.

01:15:47.910 --> 01:15:50.370
So we're a specialist
mental health charity

01:15:50.370 --> 01:15:51.720
and provided to the NHS,

01:15:51.720 --> 01:15:52.620
and we work nationally,

01:15:52.620 --> 01:15:54.780
but we are very much
rooted in the North East,

01:15:54.780 --> 01:15:57.320
in covering the entirety of
the integrated care board

01:15:57.320 --> 01:15:59.120
of the North East and North Cumbria.

01:15:59.970 --> 01:16:02.100
So I'm going to put some flavour on this

01:16:02.100 --> 01:16:05.460
around how it actually
matters to us on the ground.

01:16:05.460 --> 01:16:08.490
I've been provided to the
NHS and various organisations

01:16:08.490 --> 01:16:09.960
for around 15 years

01:16:09.960 --> 01:16:12.720
and I've always felt
quite on the periphery,

01:16:12.720 --> 01:16:15.390
delivering great services,
but on the periphery.

01:16:15.390 --> 01:16:17.850
The community mental health
transformation has brought us

01:16:17.850 --> 01:16:19.080
right into the centre of it.

01:16:19.080 --> 01:16:22.320
And as a provider and
as a specialist provider

01:16:22.320 --> 01:16:23.790
of mental health services,

01:16:23.790 --> 01:16:26.370
we are able to work as part
of that multidisciplinary team

01:16:26.370 --> 01:16:28.260
in solving problems.

01:16:28.260 --> 01:16:30.420
And as Sam mentioned there,

01:16:30.420 --> 01:16:35.280
untapped workforce of individuals
with high skills around

01:16:35.280 --> 01:16:36.540
lived experience,

01:16:36.540 --> 01:16:38.850
around different types of workforce models

01:16:38.850 --> 01:16:41.310
that can work together with
our statutory colleagues

01:16:41.310 --> 01:16:42.900
to deliver massive difference

01:16:42.900 --> 01:16:44.763
to the people in our communities.

01:16:46.500 --> 01:16:49.770
We are able to co-create
with our communities

01:16:49.770 --> 01:16:52.020
and co-create with our
colleagues within the NHS

01:16:52.020 --> 01:16:52.950
and local authorities.

01:16:52.950 --> 01:16:55.620
And I'll just give you
one example of that.

01:16:55.620 --> 01:16:57.120
We have worked on a problem,

01:16:57.120 --> 01:17:00.270
which is waiting lists for
our community treatment teams.

01:17:00.270 --> 01:17:02.280
We've got some very long
waiting lists, unfortunately,

01:17:02.280 --> 01:17:03.990
on community treatment teams.

01:17:03.990 --> 01:17:05.250
Looking at it differently,

01:17:05.250 --> 01:17:07.350
we've been able to
introduce new models of care

01:17:07.350 --> 01:17:09.389
and new workforce solutions

01:17:09.389 --> 01:17:12.480
that mean that we take
it from episodic care

01:17:12.480 --> 01:17:13.740
and just looking at someone

01:17:13.740 --> 01:17:16.200
through the lens of episodic
care throughout the year

01:17:16.200 --> 01:17:18.120
to introducing link workers,

01:17:18.120 --> 01:17:19.470
people with lived experience

01:17:19.470 --> 01:17:23.285
who work with those individuals
for 12 to 16 to 18 weeks

01:17:23.285 --> 01:17:26.040
and take them off the community
treatment team waiting list

01:17:26.040 --> 01:17:26.873
because they're looking at

01:17:26.873 --> 01:17:29.580
the social aspect of their
psychological issues.

01:17:29.580 --> 01:17:31.200
And what we've seen is

01:17:31.200 --> 01:17:32.670
some of the trusts that
we're working with,

01:17:32.670 --> 01:17:35.130
some waiting lists going
from two year waiting lists

01:17:35.130 --> 01:17:37.440
to six month waiting lists.

01:17:37.440 --> 01:17:39.390
Now, that's a remarkable change.

01:17:39.390 --> 01:17:42.030
That's seeing a reduction
in A and E appointments,

01:17:42.030 --> 01:17:43.980
and people turning up to A and E,

01:17:43.980 --> 01:17:45.450
GP appointments.

01:17:45.450 --> 01:17:47.340
It's having a whole system approach,

01:17:47.340 --> 01:17:48.330
so not just looking at them

01:17:48.330 --> 01:17:49.740
and taking 'em off waiting lists,

01:17:49.740 --> 01:17:51.750
but it's the impact on the wider system.

01:17:51.750 --> 01:17:53.190
And now that's only because of

01:17:53.190 --> 01:17:55.740
the community transformation
work that we're doing

01:17:55.740 --> 01:17:57.900
and being front and centre
of the core production

01:17:57.900 --> 01:17:58.740
with all of our colleagues.

01:17:58.740 --> 01:18:00.423
So I just want to say a huge thank you

01:18:00.423 --> 01:18:02.820
for being invited here
today to talk about that

01:18:02.820 --> 01:18:05.640
and also being part of that change

01:18:05.640 --> 01:18:07.990
that's coming to the
people of our communities.

01:18:09.690 --> 01:18:11.460
- Very good, thank you.

01:18:11.460 --> 01:18:13.860
Comments or questions or challenges?

01:18:13.860 --> 01:18:14.693
Simon?

01:18:14.693 --> 01:18:16.557
Oh, well, Simon, do you
want to go first, Simon,

01:18:16.557 --> 01:18:18.150
and then David?

01:18:18.150 --> 01:18:20.950
- Yeah, this is my first day here

01:18:22.519 --> 01:18:23.477
and I don't want-

01:18:23.477 --> 01:18:24.310
- I think you know something on the topic.

01:18:24.310 --> 01:18:25.500
- I know, but I don't want to badge myself

01:18:25.500 --> 01:18:27.453
as the right honourable mental health.

01:18:28.583 --> 01:18:29.817
I know I'm also-
- You're much broader.

01:18:29.817 --> 01:18:33.450
- I'm also losing my voice,
but just a couple of things.

01:18:33.450 --> 01:18:35.070
One thing, Claire, that comes out,

01:18:35.070 --> 01:18:39.300
you are introducing an annual
health check for, I assume,

01:18:39.300 --> 01:18:41.310
people with severe mental illness.

01:18:41.310 --> 01:18:42.445
That's right, isn't it?

01:18:42.445 --> 01:18:43.278
- Yes.

01:18:43.278 --> 01:18:46.380
Yes, so the premature mortality-

01:18:46.380 --> 01:18:47.213
- Yeah.

01:18:47.213 --> 01:18:49.200
- Of people with serious mental illness,

01:18:49.200 --> 01:18:52.950
from things with hearts, hips,
well, not hips, but lungs,

01:18:52.950 --> 01:18:56.760
you know, serious disorders,
diabetes, obesity,

01:18:56.760 --> 01:18:57.741
other things.

01:18:57.741 --> 01:18:59.040
It's eye watering.

01:18:59.040 --> 01:19:00.249
It's one of the great inequities.

01:19:00.249 --> 01:19:01.290
- [Simon] Yeah.

01:19:01.290 --> 01:19:05.220
- We have introduced the
annual health checks, in fact,

01:19:05.220 --> 01:19:06.053
some time ago,

01:19:06.053 --> 01:19:08.160
and there are six aspects to that check.

01:19:08.160 --> 01:19:09.957
But what I would say,

01:19:09.957 --> 01:19:12.450
and it will absolutely
feature in some of what

01:19:12.450 --> 01:19:14.430
Adam and Sam were pointing to,

01:19:14.430 --> 01:19:15.540
was that first of all,

01:19:15.540 --> 01:19:17.430
you've got to get people to those checks

01:19:17.430 --> 01:19:19.830
and have a system that enables them,

01:19:19.830 --> 01:19:21.360
but that's not enough.

01:19:21.360 --> 01:19:23.430
You've got to have
different kinds of workers

01:19:23.430 --> 01:19:25.590
and it's the plan that comes from it,

01:19:25.590 --> 01:19:27.300
whether it's making
sure they follow up with

01:19:27.300 --> 01:19:29.730
any hospital appointments or checks-

01:19:29.730 --> 01:19:30.817
- [Simon] Could I just ask you-

01:19:30.817 --> 01:19:32.040
- And also a lifestyle

01:19:32.040 --> 01:19:35.310
and how you pluck them into walking groups

01:19:35.310 --> 01:19:38.430
or activity that isn't just sort of badged

01:19:38.430 --> 01:19:39.602
physical activity.

01:19:39.602 --> 01:19:40.435
- [Simon] True.

01:19:40.435 --> 01:19:42.330
- That is about equality
of life and inclusion.

01:19:42.330 --> 01:19:43.380
- I just wanted to ask you about

01:19:43.380 --> 01:19:44.790
just one of those six points, actually,

01:19:44.790 --> 01:19:46.920
which is simply who's doing them?

01:19:46.920 --> 01:19:49.110
- Yeah, so a few years ago,

01:19:49.110 --> 01:19:53.190
we've incentivized GPs
through our QOF system,

01:19:53.190 --> 01:19:55.290
but so mainly primary care,

01:19:55.290 --> 01:19:57.930
but we have asked that
mental health services,

01:19:57.930 --> 01:20:01.140
if they admit patients
or have got them in CMHTs

01:20:01.140 --> 01:20:02.862
and they have a better relationship,

01:20:02.862 --> 01:20:03.930
as you all know,

01:20:03.930 --> 01:20:06.720
some of this is about
relationship and trust,

01:20:06.720 --> 01:20:10.235
particularly with people
who are very perhaps unwell.

01:20:10.235 --> 01:20:14.700
So primarily it's wrapped
around primary care and GPs,

01:20:14.700 --> 01:20:17.130
but we have got a newer model

01:20:17.130 --> 01:20:19.333
because we've put money into this,

01:20:19.333 --> 01:20:22.110
but also given creative thought to

01:20:22.110 --> 01:20:23.970
a different kind of workforce.

01:20:23.970 --> 01:20:27.060
We've got mental health
practitioners and the R's roles

01:20:27.060 --> 01:20:30.870
working in primary care and
others, healthcare assistants,

01:20:30.870 --> 01:20:33.120
who will do a huge part of those checks

01:20:33.120 --> 01:20:35.940
and then discuss the outcomes,
obviously, with the doctor

01:20:35.940 --> 01:20:37.620
and what the plan should be.

01:20:37.620 --> 01:20:40.260
So we're introducing
new workforce as well.

01:20:40.260 --> 01:20:42.450
But the third sector are helping hugely.

01:20:42.450 --> 01:20:45.570
Some of it's getting
people across the threshold

01:20:45.570 --> 01:20:47.630
and then further follow up.

01:20:47.630 --> 01:20:49.680
So a sort of new workforce,

01:20:49.680 --> 01:20:52.620
but working in support of primary care.

01:20:52.620 --> 01:20:55.320
One area I visited that
had the best scheme

01:20:55.320 --> 01:20:57.420
was a borough, up north,

01:20:57.420 --> 01:21:00.090
who it was in Lincolnshire,

01:21:00.090 --> 01:21:02.760
had two health care
assistants and a matron

01:21:02.760 --> 01:21:04.320
working with primary care.

01:21:04.320 --> 01:21:06.780
It's not an infinite list in any area

01:21:06.780 --> 01:21:08.580
of people with serious mental illness.

01:21:08.580 --> 01:21:09.736
- [Richard] True.

01:21:09.736 --> 01:21:10.569
- You should know who they are,

01:21:10.569 --> 01:21:12.360
you should be inviting them in

01:21:12.360 --> 01:21:16.140
and actually working your way
through what it is they need.

01:21:16.140 --> 01:21:16.980
I would just like to say

01:21:16.980 --> 01:21:21.570
we have just completed
more physical health checks

01:21:21.570 --> 01:21:22.830
than ever before.

01:21:22.830 --> 01:21:25.200
We're on a trajectory,
we've made a promise.

01:21:25.200 --> 01:21:29.717
So we were 240 in terms of
the rolling annual average

01:21:29.717 --> 01:21:32.477
at the most recent count,

01:21:32.477 --> 01:21:36.600
which is some 28% higher
than we were pre-pandemic.

01:21:36.600 --> 01:21:39.630
So thanks to everyone across
the system for making that.

01:21:39.630 --> 01:21:40.830
- [Richard] Okay.

01:21:40.830 --> 01:21:42.104
- Could I just follow up?
- Yeah, yeah.

01:21:42.104 --> 01:21:44.430
- One just that, I mean, you know,

01:21:44.430 --> 01:21:46.770
this has been an issue
for many, many years,

01:21:46.770 --> 01:21:48.060
and, on the whole,

01:21:48.060 --> 01:21:50.640
previous ways and systems
of doing health checks,

01:21:50.640 --> 01:21:51.473
people with SMI,

01:21:51.473 --> 01:21:54.090
have not really made much of a difference.

01:21:54.090 --> 01:21:55.980
Do you have a new ingredient

01:21:55.980 --> 01:21:57.387
that you think might change that?

01:21:57.387 --> 01:21:58.650
And I guess the other thing you say,

01:21:58.650 --> 01:21:59.483
how would you know?

01:21:59.483 --> 01:22:00.810
We can't wait 25 years

01:22:00.810 --> 01:22:03.120
to see what the results are going to be.

01:22:03.120 --> 01:22:06.600
How will you know if this
is actually, you know,

01:22:06.600 --> 01:22:09.453
doing what hasn't really
happened before, IE work?

01:22:10.483 --> 01:22:14.580
- Yeah, I mean, I'm not
dodging that because I think

01:22:14.580 --> 01:22:16.920
we absolutely need to make
sure the checks are happening.

01:22:16.920 --> 01:22:17.753
We then need to,

01:22:17.753 --> 01:22:19.950
and we're going to lean
into it more this year,

01:22:19.950 --> 01:22:21.000
look at the plans,

01:22:21.000 --> 01:22:24.510
the health plans and the
inclusion plans that support it

01:22:24.510 --> 01:22:26.430
because this is about having a life,

01:22:26.430 --> 01:22:28.916
walking more, you know, et cetera.

01:22:28.916 --> 01:22:31.664
But, Sam, you referenced it.

01:22:31.664 --> 01:22:33.330
- [Sam] Yeah.
- I wanted to come back to you

01:22:33.330 --> 01:22:34.824
because you referenced it
very much as well as your-

01:22:34.824 --> 01:22:35.657
- Yeah, that's fine.

01:22:35.657 --> 01:22:37.020
I was looking at the data
a few days ago, actually,

01:22:37.020 --> 01:22:40.350
and I think every system's got a register

01:22:40.350 --> 01:22:42.090
for people with severe
mental illness on it.

01:22:42.090 --> 01:22:42.990
So the names are known.

01:22:42.990 --> 01:22:44.490
I think the national average

01:22:44.490 --> 01:22:46.170
in terms of undertaking the checks

01:22:46.170 --> 01:22:48.390
is just above the 40% mark at the moment.

01:22:48.390 --> 01:22:51.360
And our system, we're
just about the 60% market,

01:22:51.360 --> 01:22:54.160
and in our integrated care
strategy for our system,

01:22:54.160 --> 01:22:56.760
we've set an ambition for 2030

01:22:56.760 --> 01:23:00.720
where we want to get up
towards the 100% mark for that.

01:23:00.720 --> 01:23:02.940
What we've found is, actually,

01:23:02.940 --> 01:23:06.420
the solutions are in an
appointment in a GP practise;

01:23:06.420 --> 01:23:09.180
they're very much around
looking at alternatives.

01:23:09.180 --> 01:23:11.400
And also, I think as Claire said,

01:23:11.400 --> 01:23:14.340
developing opportunities for

01:23:14.340 --> 01:23:16.080
more lifestyle questions as well.

01:23:16.080 --> 01:23:19.890
So we've had various schemes underway

01:23:19.890 --> 01:23:22.260
in our integrated care system.

01:23:22.260 --> 01:23:24.570
Probably the most successful
has been in South Tyneside

01:23:24.570 --> 01:23:27.090
where they've developed
community link workers

01:23:27.090 --> 01:23:30.180
who were on the whole
supporting the checks

01:23:30.180 --> 01:23:31.050
to be undertaken,

01:23:31.050 --> 01:23:33.720
but also having an extended relationship

01:23:33.720 --> 01:23:35.224
with the individuals

01:23:35.224 --> 01:23:38.040
to support the choices that they're making

01:23:38.040 --> 01:23:39.210
and their recovery.

01:23:39.210 --> 01:23:40.043
- Okay.

01:23:40.043 --> 01:23:40.903
Thank you.

01:23:40.903 --> 01:23:42.451
I know David had a question
and Ruth had a question,

01:23:42.451 --> 01:23:43.284
or a comment, I think.

01:23:44.287 --> 01:23:47.519
- Yeah, it's a comment, I think, Richard.

01:23:47.519 --> 01:23:51.360
Thanks to all three of
you for your presentation,

01:23:51.360 --> 01:23:52.533
Claire, Simon, Adam.

01:23:54.240 --> 01:23:55.497
So as a board,

01:23:55.497 --> 01:23:57.990
and I think the executive
have protected the investment

01:23:57.990 --> 01:23:58.860
in mental health-
- [Richard] Yeah.

01:23:58.860 --> 01:24:01.380
- Over quite an extended period now.

01:24:01.380 --> 01:24:06.000
And I want to commend that,

01:24:06.000 --> 01:24:08.130
but also, I think, Sam,

01:24:08.130 --> 01:24:10.920
your point about the
opportunity presented by ICBs

01:24:10.920 --> 01:24:14.550
to begin to weave different
patterns at a local level

01:24:14.550 --> 01:24:17.340
of the partnerships that are required.

01:24:17.340 --> 01:24:20.880
And the comment really,
Richard, building on that

01:24:20.880 --> 01:24:25.880
is what I think this report is setting out

01:24:26.550 --> 01:24:29.231
is the benefit of intervening early.

01:24:29.231 --> 01:24:30.270
- [Richard] Yeah.

01:24:30.270 --> 01:24:33.420
- And I think the data that
is in paragraph 18 about

01:24:33.420 --> 01:24:36.570
the reduction in both
primary care and acute demand

01:24:36.570 --> 01:24:38.400
as a consequence of that,

01:24:38.400 --> 01:24:39.900
really helpful to see,

01:24:39.900 --> 01:24:42.858
because I think the question I've got is

01:24:42.858 --> 01:24:47.850
does this approach and does
this model offer us a solution

01:24:47.850 --> 01:24:49.800
in other areas where we're under pressure?

01:24:49.800 --> 01:24:52.770
Sarah Jane was talking earlier
about different ways of

01:24:52.770 --> 01:24:55.770
actually preventing people who were frail

01:24:55.770 --> 01:24:59.310
coming into the system,
and perhaps it does,

01:24:59.310 --> 01:25:01.930
but the other thing I wanted to call out

01:25:04.050 --> 01:25:07.071
was the importance of those partnerships,

01:25:07.071 --> 01:25:10.170
the ICB opportunity of the
voluntary and community services.

01:25:10.170 --> 01:25:13.480
And I think what this is demonstrating is

01:25:15.540 --> 01:25:19.170
Adam's organisation and many
like it can reach people

01:25:19.170 --> 01:25:22.170
that ordinary, statutory
services don't reach.

01:25:22.170 --> 01:25:24.570
And I think that's why there's
some improvements in figures

01:25:24.570 --> 01:25:26.610
and reduction in demand,

01:25:26.610 --> 01:25:28.200
but also, critically,

01:25:28.200 --> 01:25:31.650
it attracts a workforce that
perhaps the statutory services

01:25:31.650 --> 01:25:33.270
wouldn't otherwise attract.

01:25:33.270 --> 01:25:34.408
- [Richard] Mm-hmm.

01:25:34.408 --> 01:25:37.080
- And whilst workforce has
been one of the key challenges,

01:25:37.080 --> 01:25:39.660
we actually need to
think about the advantage

01:25:39.660 --> 01:25:42.098
that aligning with the
volunteering community set to

01:25:42.098 --> 01:25:44.970
bringing forward a new workforce.

01:25:44.970 --> 01:25:46.200
And these aren't people

01:25:46.200 --> 01:25:50.163
that are taking other
people's clinical roles.

01:25:50.163 --> 01:25:52.770
They're often providing
that social support

01:25:52.770 --> 01:25:54.090
that is so critical

01:25:54.090 --> 01:25:55.800
to actually support
people in the community.

01:25:55.800 --> 01:26:00.750
So, not only is this good news
in its own right for people

01:26:00.750 --> 01:26:03.000
who experience mental ill health,

01:26:03.000 --> 01:26:06.990
I think it's good news as a
way of actually conceiving of

01:26:06.990 --> 01:26:09.780
an intervention that actually
makes a significant difference

01:26:09.780 --> 01:26:11.190
in the lives of others.

01:26:11.190 --> 01:26:12.450
So that's-
- [Richard] Thank you.

01:26:12.450 --> 01:26:13.800
- Just full of admiration.

01:26:13.800 --> 01:26:14.633
Thanks, Claire.

01:26:14.633 --> 01:26:15.466
- Ruth?

01:26:15.466 --> 01:26:16.590
- One comment and one question.

01:26:16.590 --> 01:26:18.050
So, thank you, all three of you.

01:26:18.050 --> 01:26:22.830
And this is, for me, a real
example of voluntary sector

01:26:22.830 --> 01:26:26.496
working as equal partners with our NHS

01:26:26.496 --> 01:26:28.470
and then having the benefit,

01:26:28.470 --> 01:26:31.350
and that's what the task
force report talks about,

01:26:31.350 --> 01:26:34.620
is working in partnership
for real added benefit.

01:26:34.620 --> 01:26:36.180
So my question would be,

01:26:36.180 --> 01:26:39.090
you talked about the
support for adults, I guess,

01:26:39.090 --> 01:26:42.300
but just checking that there
was also the same for children,

01:26:42.300 --> 01:26:45.960
young people and that
voluntary partnership across?

01:26:45.960 --> 01:26:47.730
- Yeah, absolutely.

01:26:47.730 --> 01:26:48.563
Absolutely.

01:26:48.563 --> 01:26:50.610
And also pick up on David's point,

01:26:50.610 --> 01:26:52.260
we have got many examples of

01:26:52.260 --> 01:26:56.010
working with other CVSE partners
around diabetes pathways,

01:26:56.010 --> 01:26:57.163
respiratory pathways,

01:26:57.163 --> 01:27:01.020
that are helping us address
issues around demand

01:27:01.020 --> 01:27:03.421
and also prevention.

01:27:03.421 --> 01:27:04.254
- Yeah.

01:27:04.254 --> 01:27:07.140
- May I just say, as
well, in response to that,

01:27:07.140 --> 01:27:10.530
where this is adults and older adults?

01:27:10.530 --> 01:27:11.367
- [Richard] Yeah.

01:27:11.367 --> 01:27:14.040
- And I do want to keep the
silver thread as it were

01:27:14.040 --> 01:27:15.510
in our mental health plan.

01:27:15.510 --> 01:27:16.920
It's across the lifespan,

01:27:16.920 --> 01:27:21.920
but it's also got quite a
focus on the 16 to 25 year old,

01:27:22.410 --> 01:27:24.840
where this isn't about more.

01:27:24.840 --> 01:27:26.670
It is a transformation.

01:27:26.670 --> 01:27:29.790
We want to grow by more
than 5% every year,

01:27:29.790 --> 01:27:33.082
so it is about more people
accessing care sooner,

01:27:33.082 --> 01:27:36.030
but it's about accessing care differently.

01:27:36.030 --> 01:27:36.863
- [Richard] Thank you.

01:27:36.863 --> 01:27:38.100
- I would say there are
a couple of risks to it;

01:27:38.100 --> 01:27:41.490
the nursing workforce one, Ruth.

01:27:41.490 --> 01:27:45.870
So, we've got something
like 21% nursing vacancies

01:27:45.870 --> 01:27:47.190
in mental health.

01:27:47.190 --> 01:27:48.690
We're also growing the offer.

01:27:48.690 --> 01:27:51.083
We desperately need them in community-

01:27:51.083 --> 01:27:51.916
- [Richard] Yeah.
- As well as inpatients.

01:27:51.916 --> 01:27:52.830
So that will be,

01:27:52.830 --> 01:27:54.810
you've mentioned the
long term workforce plan,

01:27:54.810 --> 01:27:58.260
but it absolutely needs
to be a key part of that.

01:27:58.260 --> 01:28:00.060
And Sam, you've mentioned the economy,

01:28:00.060 --> 01:28:03.180
I think we're all jointly worried about

01:28:03.180 --> 01:28:05.220
the impact of what's happening,

01:28:05.220 --> 01:28:08.820
which is beyond us in some
ways in the economy right now,

01:28:08.820 --> 01:28:12.750
but often these groups
of people or populations

01:28:12.750 --> 01:28:14.040
are most affected.

01:28:14.040 --> 01:28:16.260
So I can only emphasise that

01:28:16.260 --> 01:28:21.260
the greatest defence against
that is belonging, being known,

01:28:21.300 --> 01:28:25.320
having people really
working with you differently

01:28:25.320 --> 01:28:28.170
in addition, obviously,
to good clinical care

01:28:28.170 --> 01:28:29.970
and evidence-based treatment.

01:28:29.970 --> 01:28:33.163
And so, this really is
the bringing together of

01:28:33.163 --> 01:28:36.627
the clinical with the local.

01:28:36.627 --> 01:28:38.700
And I did feel so proud, I might say-

01:28:38.700 --> 01:28:39.533
- [Richard] Very good.

01:28:39.533 --> 01:28:40.366
- If I'm honest, listening to colleagues.

01:28:40.366 --> 01:28:42.420
- Can I, yeah, Simon, then Julian

01:28:42.420 --> 01:28:43.483
and then I've got a comment.

01:28:43.483 --> 01:28:45.720
- A comment, really, about workforce.

01:28:45.720 --> 01:28:48.000
I'm sure you know you have a
new medical and nursing school

01:28:48.000 --> 01:28:48.896
coming to Cumbria.

01:28:48.896 --> 01:28:49.729
- Yes.

01:28:49.729 --> 01:28:51.630
- And people tend to
work where they qualify.

01:28:51.630 --> 01:28:53.460
So I hope you're making
strong links there.

01:28:53.460 --> 01:28:55.260
- Absolutely, with Imperial, yeah.

01:28:55.260 --> 01:28:56.100
- Very good.

01:28:56.100 --> 01:28:56.933
Julian?

01:28:56.933 --> 01:28:59.970
- I think that David's challenge,
I think, is a good one of

01:28:59.970 --> 01:29:02.430
even just how do we understand
patterns of provision

01:29:02.430 --> 01:29:03.413
across the country?

01:29:03.413 --> 01:29:07.110
I mean, ultimately is for
ICBs to commission locally

01:29:07.110 --> 01:29:09.030
for their circumstances.

01:29:09.030 --> 01:29:11.400
I mean, we might say the
same in physical care, too,

01:29:11.400 --> 01:29:14.550
but just how we understand
patterns of community provision

01:29:14.550 --> 01:29:16.654
and if we believe they are the answer

01:29:16.654 --> 01:29:19.590
to reducing inpatient demand,

01:29:19.590 --> 01:29:23.850
I think we have a challenge
to work out, you know,

01:29:23.850 --> 01:29:26.250
where we're about right and
where it clearly isn't right

01:29:26.250 --> 01:29:27.840
and how we work with ICBs

01:29:27.840 --> 01:29:30.814
to make sure that pattern of provision

01:29:30.814 --> 01:29:33.660
is more consistent.

01:29:33.660 --> 01:29:34.613
- So I was going to
slightly build on that.

01:29:34.613 --> 01:29:36.570
I mean, it's a slightly jarring note

01:29:36.570 --> 01:29:38.670
to what's a very good initiative

01:29:38.670 --> 01:29:42.613
and I was disappointed

01:29:44.370 --> 01:29:46.203
when I read paragraph 10,

01:29:47.220 --> 01:29:50.910
which is about the PCN's take up.

01:29:50.910 --> 01:29:53.250
And I was okay,

01:29:53.250 --> 01:29:58.250
but on paragraph 12 when I
find that 57% of the PCNs

01:29:59.816 --> 01:30:02.163
now have a mental health practitioner.

01:30:03.960 --> 01:30:05.560
So there's a point for me about,

01:30:06.720 --> 01:30:08.310
really, it's such a good initiative,

01:30:08.310 --> 01:30:12.720
why is the pattern of
take up actually poor?

01:30:12.720 --> 01:30:14.386
I think it's poor. You
might tell me it's not

01:30:14.386 --> 01:30:16.080
versus workforce vacancies and so on,

01:30:16.080 --> 01:30:19.560
but it's somewhat a challenge.

01:30:19.560 --> 01:30:21.507
And then my fundamental challenge,

01:30:21.507 --> 01:30:24.060
and it's a challenge of the paper,

01:30:24.060 --> 01:30:26.580
is there are lots of
stats and numbers in it,

01:30:26.580 --> 01:30:30.660
but I don't know what proportion
they represent of the need.

01:30:30.660 --> 01:30:33.120
So I can see 504,000 people access

01:30:33.120 --> 01:30:35.010
community mental health service
in the preceding 12 months,

01:30:35.010 --> 01:30:37.620
but what proportion of the need is that?

01:30:37.620 --> 01:30:40.230
And so when I read this paper
and I look at all the stuff

01:30:40.230 --> 01:30:43.170
and we're putting in allied health, sorry,

01:30:43.170 --> 01:30:45.150
we're looking mental
health practitioners in,

01:30:45.150 --> 01:30:50.150
I've got no idea of how
much of the challenge

01:30:50.160 --> 01:30:54.993
this initiative, to date,
has started to meet.

01:30:55.860 --> 01:30:59.220
And then there'll be stuff
about how much more we expect,

01:30:59.220 --> 01:31:02.400
given pressures and so
on, the demand to go up.

01:31:02.400 --> 01:31:04.770
So if I had a request for a follow up,

01:31:04.770 --> 01:31:06.180
not in necessarily the board,

01:31:06.180 --> 01:31:09.090
I'd just quite like to know
the base load data, actually,

01:31:09.090 --> 01:31:10.170
and therefore some sense of

01:31:10.170 --> 01:31:12.213
proportionate contribution of what is

01:31:12.213 --> 01:31:15.210
and then I'll come back to be less jarring

01:31:15.210 --> 01:31:16.560
and what is I think a very good,

01:31:16.560 --> 01:31:17.790
absolutely commendable initiative.

01:31:17.790 --> 01:31:18.623
I agree with David,

01:31:18.623 --> 01:31:21.600
it's a very, very important intervention,

01:31:21.600 --> 01:31:24.720
but I just can't quite work
out from the paper, you know,

01:31:24.720 --> 01:31:26.913
how material it is proportionately.

01:31:28.080 --> 01:31:29.200
- May I just come to that one first?

01:31:29.200 --> 01:31:32.400
So it would be really good to
follow up with more detail,

01:31:32.400 --> 01:31:33.390
which we do have.

01:31:33.390 --> 01:31:36.030
And, obviously, it varies
across the country,

01:31:36.030 --> 01:31:40.655
partly because of historic
investment in mental health

01:31:40.655 --> 01:31:44.400
and how local areas have
prioritised it over the years.

01:31:44.400 --> 01:31:46.470
So there is a big variation,

01:31:46.470 --> 01:31:49.860
but on average we would say
that this would have us seeing

01:31:49.860 --> 01:31:51.950
probably about one in three of the people

01:31:51.950 --> 01:31:53.990
who really would most-

01:31:53.990 --> 01:31:55.350
- So the kids are at
third of their need is met

01:31:55.350 --> 01:31:56.403
by this initiative?
- Yes, that is-

01:31:56.403 --> 01:31:57.930
- Once it's fully rolled out?

01:31:57.930 --> 01:31:58.763
- Yes.

01:31:58.763 --> 01:32:01.590
And we were always clear from the outset

01:32:01.590 --> 01:32:05.370
it would go this far but not this far.

01:32:05.370 --> 01:32:09.180
But what we're seeking to show, I suppose,

01:32:09.180 --> 01:32:12.120
is that if you have a really
methodical national programme

01:32:12.120 --> 01:32:13.510
that's properly modelled

01:32:14.460 --> 01:32:17.520
and you're working well
across the country locally,

01:32:17.520 --> 01:32:19.830
actually, you can close
that treatment gap.

01:32:19.830 --> 01:32:20.760
You can.

01:32:20.760 --> 01:32:23.550
It's not some never ending treatment gap.

01:32:23.550 --> 01:32:27.180
This is a huge step both
towards access and waiting,

01:32:27.180 --> 01:32:30.450
but also the transformation
and the nature of services.

01:32:30.450 --> 01:32:32.340
Just on the R's roles, if I might,

01:32:32.340 --> 01:32:35.135
or that you point to there,

01:32:35.135 --> 01:32:38.730
so in some defence of
primary care colleagues

01:32:38.730 --> 01:32:40.380
or colleagues locally,

01:32:40.380 --> 01:32:42.843
they're still relatively new roles.

01:32:42.843 --> 01:32:47.130
And we have, I was going to say insisted,

01:32:47.130 --> 01:32:50.216
but that's very out with the
new operating model isn't it?

01:32:50.216 --> 01:32:54.320
But we've insisted that
they should be joint roles

01:32:54.320 --> 01:32:57.056
in this space because,

01:32:57.056 --> 01:33:00.910
so jointly funded based in primary care

01:33:02.040 --> 01:33:06.240
because we know that service
users sometimes feel bounced

01:33:06.240 --> 01:33:08.340
between primary and secondary care.

01:33:08.340 --> 01:33:11.615
And so I think some areas have
been much quicker than others

01:33:11.615 --> 01:33:15.003
to say, "We know what we could
do, we want one of these,

01:33:15.003 --> 01:33:16.530
et cetera, et cetera.

01:33:16.530 --> 01:33:18.540
We'll train them, we'll recruit them."

01:33:18.540 --> 01:33:22.050
We've built in more flexibility
into the scheme as well.

01:33:22.050 --> 01:33:24.930
So some of the feedback
we had nationally is that

01:33:24.930 --> 01:33:28.380
our first sets of criterion
were too restrictive.

01:33:28.380 --> 01:33:30.450
So for example-
- [Richard] Okay.

01:33:30.450 --> 01:33:31.650
- Some primary care groups

01:33:31.650 --> 01:33:35.610
would like to have
voluntary services sector

01:33:35.610 --> 01:33:38.250
fill some of those R's roles.

01:33:38.250 --> 01:33:42.330
We initially said adult
nurses, most likely.

01:33:42.330 --> 01:33:44.940
Now it can be any professional

01:33:44.940 --> 01:33:47.883
or a peer support worker or VCS

01:33:47.883 --> 01:33:50.430
if it's been put on jointly.

01:33:50.430 --> 01:33:51.990
And that's what the local need is.

01:33:51.990 --> 01:33:54.960
So we are hoping that by
being less restrictive,

01:33:54.960 --> 01:33:56.403
more freedoms,

01:33:57.540 --> 01:34:00.469
what we do want them to be
is evidence-based, though.

01:34:00.469 --> 01:34:01.942
- [Richard] Okay.
- You know, we do want to know

01:34:01.942 --> 01:34:03.650
we're getting value back.

01:34:03.650 --> 01:34:06.030
- All right, well, I'm
just conscious of time.

01:34:06.030 --> 01:34:08.160
I'm going to close the topic down,

01:34:08.160 --> 01:34:11.790
but just to say thank you
very much, Sam and Adam.

01:34:11.790 --> 01:34:12.810
Adam, for your partnership,

01:34:12.810 --> 01:34:15.240
and, Sam, for your
leadership in this space.

01:34:15.240 --> 01:34:17.490
And Claire, as always,
thank you very much.

01:34:17.490 --> 01:34:18.323
Good.

01:34:18.323 --> 01:34:20.199
I mean, it's a good
initiative to hear about

01:34:20.199 --> 01:34:21.032
and it's a good update.

01:34:21.032 --> 01:34:22.233
So, thank you.
- Yeah.

01:34:24.270 --> 01:34:27.720
- Should we go on, then, to
it's the seventh item, I think.

01:34:27.720 --> 01:34:29.130
John Stewart?

01:34:29.130 --> 01:34:30.240
It's a really important area,

01:34:30.240 --> 01:34:35.240
23 billion to spend over
150 commissioned services.

01:34:35.700 --> 01:34:37.290
But John, you've going
to introduce it to us

01:34:37.290 --> 01:34:39.960
along with actually further
delegation, I think,

01:34:39.960 --> 01:34:42.720
out of NHS England into down to ICBs.

01:34:42.720 --> 01:34:43.553
- Thank you, Chair.

01:34:43.553 --> 01:34:45.570
So can I just begin by
actually thanking the board

01:34:45.570 --> 01:34:47.610
for taking the time and attention today

01:34:47.610 --> 01:34:49.590
to listen to these important proposals

01:34:49.590 --> 01:34:52.560
and actually to the many
thousands or hundreds, actually,

01:34:52.560 --> 01:34:56.220
of people across our national and regional

01:34:56.220 --> 01:34:57.420
commissioning teams,

01:34:57.420 --> 01:34:59.460
integrated care boards,

01:34:59.460 --> 01:35:02.310
as well as our clinical
leaders and other stakeholders

01:35:02.310 --> 01:35:04.530
for their collaboration and expertise

01:35:04.530 --> 01:35:07.530
in formulating these plans?

01:35:07.530 --> 01:35:11.027
The paper itself is seeking board approval

01:35:11.027 --> 01:35:13.170
for NHS England to enter into

01:35:13.170 --> 01:35:15.090
formal joint commissioning arrangements

01:35:15.090 --> 01:35:16.620
with integrated care boards

01:35:16.620 --> 01:35:18.930
across nine geographical footprints.

01:35:18.930 --> 01:35:22.907
They're set out in the Annex
B, I think, to the paper,

01:35:22.907 --> 01:35:25.320
for a subset of that portfolio.

01:35:25.320 --> 01:35:27.390
- Just to interrupt a minute,
I mean, when I saw the nine,

01:35:27.390 --> 01:35:29.970
the nine versus the seven
regions, what's happening?

01:35:29.970 --> 01:35:32.220
- There is a split in the Midlands

01:35:32.220 --> 01:35:33.773
between West and East Midlands.

01:35:33.773 --> 01:35:34.606
- [Richard] Okay.

01:35:34.606 --> 01:35:37.020
- Following kind of natural
clinical patient flows.

01:35:37.020 --> 01:35:41.400
And in the North East, it will
be a similar split between

01:35:41.400 --> 01:35:44.413
Northeast and Yorkshire and
the rest of the North East.

01:35:44.413 --> 01:35:45.840
- [Richard] Okay, thank you.

01:35:45.840 --> 01:35:50.313
- So I think a logic in terms
of kind of patient flows.

01:35:52.140 --> 01:35:53.460
So since 2013,

01:35:55.120 --> 01:35:57.450
NHS England's been the
accountable commissioner

01:35:57.450 --> 01:36:01.907
for this diverse portfolio
of over 150 services.

01:36:01.907 --> 01:36:05.340
And that ranges from
relatively high volume services

01:36:05.340 --> 01:36:08.560
such as chemotherapy, kidney dialysis,

01:36:08.560 --> 01:36:13.350
through to highly complex, low
volume services for patients,

01:36:13.350 --> 01:36:15.870
often with very rare diseases.

01:36:15.870 --> 01:36:19.400
So an example of that is we
commissioned just one centre

01:36:19.400 --> 01:36:23.070
to treat patients with
a specific gene therapy

01:36:23.070 --> 01:36:27.270
for the treatment of
metachromatic leukodystrophy,

01:36:27.270 --> 01:36:29.070
which is, in fact, just
one of five centres

01:36:29.070 --> 01:36:30.270
in the whole of Europe.

01:36:30.270 --> 01:36:34.110
So it's a very broad spectrum of services

01:36:34.110 --> 01:36:36.180
within the portfolio.

01:36:36.180 --> 01:36:40.080
The benefits of being a single, national,

01:36:40.080 --> 01:36:42.570
accountable commissioner
for this portfolio

01:36:42.570 --> 01:36:43.740
have been significant.

01:36:43.740 --> 01:36:47.940
And I'll draw out maybe four
which are set out in the paper.

01:36:47.940 --> 01:36:48.960
Firstly, I think,

01:36:48.960 --> 01:36:52.317
the importance of setting
consistent national standards

01:36:52.317 --> 01:36:56.820
and service specifications
across the entire portfolio.

01:36:56.820 --> 01:36:59.460
Secondly, the implementation of a robust

01:36:59.460 --> 01:37:01.950
and evidence-based approach to developing

01:37:01.950 --> 01:37:04.080
clinical commissioning policy,

01:37:04.080 --> 01:37:06.900
which has really meant that
there is no postcode lottery

01:37:06.900 --> 01:37:10.170
in terms of whether new
treatments or procedures

01:37:10.170 --> 01:37:13.563
are available to patients
anywhere in the country.

01:37:14.430 --> 01:37:18.240
Thirdly, the ability to hold
single commercial negotiations

01:37:18.240 --> 01:37:20.490
with the pharmaceutical industry

01:37:20.490 --> 01:37:23.550
to support patient access
to the latest drugs.

01:37:23.550 --> 01:37:25.367
And that is a topic that we
discussed, actually, I think,

01:37:25.367 --> 01:37:28.670
at the last board meeting,
some of the benefits.

01:37:28.670 --> 01:37:30.720
So I won't go through those again.

01:37:30.720 --> 01:37:31.590
And finally,

01:37:31.590 --> 01:37:34.830
I think through our clinical
reference group infrastructure,

01:37:34.830 --> 01:37:37.140
you know, the way in which
we've placed clinical leadership

01:37:37.140 --> 01:37:39.810
at the heart of our
commissioning approach.

01:37:39.810 --> 01:37:42.630
But I think it's also
important to point out

01:37:42.630 --> 01:37:44.959
there have been some relative weaknesses

01:37:44.959 --> 01:37:48.360
arising from the fragmented
commissioning responsibilities

01:37:48.360 --> 01:37:50.430
where NHS England commissions

01:37:50.430 --> 01:37:52.860
23 billion pounds worth of services,

01:37:52.860 --> 01:37:55.050
and previously clinical
commissioning groups

01:37:55.050 --> 01:37:59.410
and now, of course, integrated
care boards are commissioning

01:37:59.410 --> 01:38:01.563
you know, all other services.

01:38:02.400 --> 01:38:03.690
And, you know,

01:38:03.690 --> 01:38:07.740
that has led to a misalignment
sometimes in incentives,

01:38:07.740 --> 01:38:11.250
barriers to joint working,

01:38:11.250 --> 01:38:12.540
making it harder, in fact,

01:38:12.540 --> 01:38:14.940
to invest in upstream interventions

01:38:14.940 --> 01:38:17.310
and really take action to reduce demand

01:38:17.310 --> 01:38:18.903
for specialised services.

01:38:19.830 --> 01:38:23.220
The way in which specialised
services has also been funded

01:38:23.220 --> 01:38:25.080
over the last decade,

01:38:25.080 --> 01:38:29.010
which has been based on historic
levels of provider activity

01:38:29.010 --> 01:38:31.260
rather than population health need,

01:38:31.260 --> 01:38:33.750
has also had an impact on
our ability to focus on

01:38:33.750 --> 01:38:35.520
tackling health inequalities

01:38:35.520 --> 01:38:38.100
and ultimately in terms of, you know,

01:38:38.100 --> 01:38:40.140
taking action to address, you know,

01:38:40.140 --> 01:38:44.223
access, outcomes, quality
from these services.

01:38:45.360 --> 01:38:48.150
The establishment, though,
of integrated care boards

01:38:48.150 --> 01:38:51.180
alongside new powers given to us

01:38:51.180 --> 01:38:55.530
in the 2022 Health and Care Act,

01:38:55.530 --> 01:38:58.733
I think presents some really
important opportunities now

01:38:58.733 --> 01:39:02.026
to both maintain some of the
strengths of the current system

01:39:02.026 --> 01:39:05.340
but also address some of the
weaknesses I've highlighted

01:39:05.340 --> 01:39:08.550
around the fragmented
commissioning responsibilities.

01:39:08.550 --> 01:39:11.400
And if I just then finish
by turning specifically

01:39:11.400 --> 01:39:13.500
to the proposals,

01:39:13.500 --> 01:39:15.780
the first thing I think
is important to say

01:39:15.780 --> 01:39:18.060
is that we are not planning any changes

01:39:18.060 --> 01:39:20.100
to the list of specialised services.

01:39:20.100 --> 01:39:22.350
And that is really important

01:39:22.350 --> 01:39:24.630
because it means that NHS England

01:39:24.630 --> 01:39:26.640
will remain the accountable commissioner

01:39:26.640 --> 01:39:28.500
for all of these services

01:39:28.500 --> 01:39:31.320
and will continue to
set national standards,

01:39:31.320 --> 01:39:33.300
service specifications,

01:39:33.300 --> 01:39:36.780
clinical access policies
across the entire portfolio,

01:39:36.780 --> 01:39:38.970
and we will also continue
to be able to hold

01:39:38.970 --> 01:39:43.500
single commercial negotiations
on behalf of the entire NHS

01:39:43.500 --> 01:39:46.200
in relation to high-cost
drugs and devices.

01:39:46.200 --> 01:39:48.840
So that's a really important first point.

01:39:48.840 --> 01:39:50.310
But for new powers

01:39:50.310 --> 01:39:53.460
that we have been given by the Health Act,

01:39:53.460 --> 01:39:55.110
do allow us now to enter into

01:39:55.110 --> 01:39:58.140
formal joint commissioning
arrangements with ICBs

01:39:58.140 --> 01:40:00.840
or to delegate
commissioning responsibility

01:40:00.840 --> 01:40:03.450
to ICBs or groups of ICBs

01:40:03.450 --> 01:40:07.050
for some or all of that
portfolio of services.

01:40:07.050 --> 01:40:09.120
So an important task, actually,

01:40:09.120 --> 01:40:12.150
has been to look across the
entire portfolio of services

01:40:12.150 --> 01:40:14.310
and determine which
ones would benefit from

01:40:14.310 --> 01:40:16.740
more integrated
commissioning arrangements.

01:40:16.740 --> 01:40:19.020
Now paragraphs nine to 10 of the paper

01:40:19.020 --> 01:40:21.639
described the process we've been through,

01:40:21.639 --> 01:40:23.190
the clinical engagement,

01:40:23.190 --> 01:40:26.310
the patient involvement
to determine, you know,

01:40:26.310 --> 01:40:28.890
which services we think would benefit.

01:40:28.890 --> 01:40:33.290
And essentially we've ended
up placing services into,

01:40:33.290 --> 01:40:35.850
you know, three kind of broad categories.

01:40:35.850 --> 01:40:38.700
Firstly, those services that
we think are suitable and ready

01:40:38.700 --> 01:40:40.620
for greater ICB leadership.

01:40:40.620 --> 01:40:43.470
So 59 in total out of the portfolio,

01:40:43.470 --> 01:40:46.770
but representing about 13
billion pounds of spend.

01:40:46.770 --> 01:40:49.170
So a large amount of spend.

01:40:49.170 --> 01:40:52.380
Secondly, a group of services
that we do think are suitable,

01:40:52.380 --> 01:40:55.860
but for a variety of
reasons are not yet ready

01:40:55.860 --> 01:40:57.510
and where commissioning responsibility

01:40:57.510 --> 01:40:59.820
will be retained by NHS England.

01:40:59.820 --> 01:41:02.638
So 29 services in that category,

01:41:02.638 --> 01:41:05.730
about 1.5 billion pounds of spend.

01:41:05.730 --> 01:41:08.313
And then a final, important
category of services

01:41:08.313 --> 01:41:11.310
that we think should remain
nationally commissioned,

01:41:11.310 --> 01:41:13.200
so 1.3 billion pounds.

01:41:13.200 --> 01:41:16.719
And this is really all of our
highly specialised portfolio

01:41:16.719 --> 01:41:17.670
where, actually,

01:41:17.670 --> 01:41:20.760
the number of patients
requiring these services

01:41:20.760 --> 01:41:23.640
is normally kind of less than 500,

01:41:23.640 --> 01:41:28.110
a very, very small number of
specialist centres required.

01:41:28.110 --> 01:41:32.070
So we're asking them all to
kind of endorse that, you know,

01:41:32.070 --> 01:41:34.110
analysis of the portfolio

01:41:34.110 --> 01:41:36.570
and the services that
we do think are ready

01:41:36.570 --> 01:41:39.300
for ICB leadership.

01:41:39.300 --> 01:41:42.720
The second task, then, was to
assess ICB system readiness

01:41:42.720 --> 01:41:47.130
to take on commissioning
responsibility for these services.

01:41:47.130 --> 01:41:50.550
And here we followed a similar
process to the one followed

01:41:50.550 --> 01:41:53.130
for the delegation of
pharmaceutical, ophthalmic

01:41:53.130 --> 01:41:55.320
and dental services,

01:41:55.320 --> 01:41:58.710
with ICBs undertaking a self-assessment

01:41:58.710 --> 01:42:00.453
of system readiness.

01:42:00.453 --> 01:42:03.990
And that was assured by our regional teams

01:42:03.990 --> 01:42:05.527
and by a national moderation panel

01:42:05.527 --> 01:42:08.580
that was, in fact, shared by Julian Kelly.

01:42:08.580 --> 01:42:10.320
And the conclusion of that process

01:42:10.320 --> 01:42:13.020
and the recommendations
of the moderation panel

01:42:13.020 --> 01:42:14.466
is that across the country,

01:42:14.466 --> 01:42:15.960
we should move to

01:42:15.960 --> 01:42:19.380
formal joint commissioning
arrangements with ICBs

01:42:19.380 --> 01:42:22.039
discharged through nine
statutory joint committees

01:42:22.039 --> 01:42:24.570
for the 59 services that we said are

01:42:24.570 --> 01:42:27.750
suitable and ready for
greater ICB leadership.

01:42:27.750 --> 01:42:28.800
And importantly,

01:42:28.800 --> 01:42:32.520
that would be a stepping
stone to looking at

01:42:32.520 --> 01:42:34.950
delegating full
commissioning responsibility

01:42:34.950 --> 01:42:39.950
for those services to ICBs
or multi ICB collaborations

01:42:40.080 --> 01:42:42.960
from April 24, so in a year's time.

01:42:42.960 --> 01:42:45.120
- So, I'm going to pause you.

01:42:45.120 --> 01:42:46.200
I want to pause this for a moment

01:42:46.200 --> 01:42:48.630
because I think it's a very good,

01:42:48.630 --> 01:42:50.490
you briefed me last week, actually,

01:42:50.490 --> 01:42:51.773
and so I've had that benefit

01:42:51.773 --> 01:42:54.630
and it's a very good paper,

01:42:54.630 --> 01:42:56.220
but I think I'm going to slightly lose

01:42:56.220 --> 01:42:58.500
if you now walk us through
each of the next steps.

01:42:58.500 --> 01:43:01.500
I just want us to take them
in in chunks, John, I think,

01:43:01.500 --> 01:43:03.690
to make sure we understand
what we're approving.

01:43:03.690 --> 01:43:04.866
And before we just do that,

01:43:04.866 --> 01:43:08.130
Mark, I think has got a question, comment.

01:43:08.130 --> 01:43:08.963
- Yes.

01:43:08.963 --> 01:43:11.439
I mean, I think there
are some risks to this.

01:43:11.439 --> 01:43:13.950
The specialised services are specialised

01:43:13.950 --> 01:43:16.140
because there's a shortage of skills

01:43:16.140 --> 01:43:18.480
amongst the people that deliver them.

01:43:18.480 --> 01:43:22.683
They often require complex
equipment, complex drugs,

01:43:23.670 --> 01:43:27.150
and I don't know whether it was implicit,

01:43:27.150 --> 01:43:29.820
but it was almost implied
that this might be moving to

01:43:29.820 --> 01:43:33.660
a model where these ones
would have nine centres.

01:43:33.660 --> 01:43:36.330
In other words, there'd be one
in each geographic facility.

01:43:36.330 --> 01:43:37.920
Maybe that's not the case,

01:43:37.920 --> 01:43:40.620
but I think there are some
risks of perverse incentives,

01:43:40.620 --> 01:43:44.823
which is that thrown in with
the rest of an ICB budget,

01:43:45.721 --> 01:43:49.110
there may be less keenness to spend that

01:43:49.110 --> 01:43:50.800
in other parts of the country

01:43:51.780 --> 01:43:54.420
and there may be a temptation
to create services locally

01:43:54.420 --> 01:43:58.380
which may not, frankly, be
as good as the centralization

01:43:58.380 --> 01:44:00.480
that goes with these specialised services.

01:44:00.480 --> 01:44:02.460
And I can see you've
done it very carefully

01:44:02.460 --> 01:44:03.660
and you haven't tried to, as it were,

01:44:03.660 --> 01:44:05.990
include the, you know, 10
patients with this disease

01:44:05.990 --> 01:44:07.545
in the entire country.

01:44:07.545 --> 01:44:08.378
- [Richard] No.

01:44:08.378 --> 01:44:10.110
- But nevertheless, I
think that there are risks,

01:44:10.110 --> 01:44:11.370
and I suppose my plea would be

01:44:11.370 --> 01:44:14.070
that I think this needs to
be very carefully evaluated.

01:44:14.070 --> 01:44:15.870
Cancer therapy, for example,

01:44:15.870 --> 01:44:17.790
was devolved many years ago

01:44:17.790 --> 01:44:19.500
and that led to very bad outcomes.

01:44:19.500 --> 01:44:22.440
And so it got brought back
together into centres,

01:44:22.440 --> 01:44:25.050
I mean, obviously, widely
distributed around the UK.

01:44:25.050 --> 01:44:27.273
I think there is a risk
with some of these services

01:44:27.273 --> 01:44:29.760
that the same could happen
if we're not careful.

01:44:29.760 --> 01:44:30.900
- [Richard] So, John?

01:44:30.900 --> 01:44:32.820
- Yeah, can I respond to that?

01:44:32.820 --> 01:44:33.653
- Yeah, do, all right.

01:44:33.653 --> 01:44:35.970
- I think we recognise some of the risks,

01:44:35.970 --> 01:44:39.866
and that is precisely
why I think, you know,

01:44:39.866 --> 01:44:42.570
the ongoing role around NHS England

01:44:42.570 --> 01:44:44.568
setting the national standards-

01:44:44.568 --> 01:44:45.900
- [Richard] That's right.
- Clinical access policies

01:44:45.900 --> 01:44:47.430
is absolutely critical.

01:44:47.430 --> 01:44:49.350
And as the accountable commissioner,

01:44:49.350 --> 01:44:54.037
that means we will be able
to ensure and require ICBs

01:44:55.020 --> 01:44:56.700
for any of these services

01:44:56.700 --> 01:44:58.950
to kind of adhere to both
standards and policies.

01:44:58.950 --> 01:45:03.213
And we will put in place
mechanisms for assuring that

01:45:03.213 --> 01:45:06.546
through our kind of oversight
and assurance arrangements.

01:45:06.546 --> 01:45:09.450
The nine geographical footprints,

01:45:09.450 --> 01:45:10.770
they are commissioning footprints,

01:45:10.770 --> 01:45:13.350
rather than necessarily
the number of centres

01:45:13.350 --> 01:45:14.727
that we will end up commissioning from.

01:45:14.727 --> 01:45:17.760
And that will vary from
service to service.

01:45:17.760 --> 01:45:21.180
I think the issue around access to kind of

01:45:21.180 --> 01:45:23.880
some of the latest treatments
is also critically important

01:45:23.880 --> 01:45:26.790
and that is where I think
over the last five years,

01:45:26.790 --> 01:45:29.970
we've had some real successes
through NHS England's role

01:45:29.970 --> 01:45:32.550
in negotiating with
pharmaceutical companies

01:45:32.550 --> 01:45:35.040
and as part of a nice appraisal process.

01:45:35.040 --> 01:45:35.873
- [Richard] Yeah.

01:45:35.873 --> 01:45:39.270
- That does not change
at all under the plans.

01:45:39.270 --> 01:45:41.852
And, in fact, the way
we fund high cost drugs

01:45:41.852 --> 01:45:43.710
will remain in place,

01:45:43.710 --> 01:45:46.890
which will be essentially
budget centrally hold.

01:45:46.890 --> 01:45:48.413
I think it's covered in the paper-

01:45:48.413 --> 01:45:49.246
- [Richard] It is, yeah.

01:45:49.246 --> 01:45:51.510
- Almost 6 billion pounds worth of money-

01:45:51.510 --> 01:45:52.343
- [Richard] To be held centrally.

01:45:52.343 --> 01:45:53.670
- Held centrally.
- [Richard] Yeah.

01:45:53.670 --> 01:45:57.630
- In order to make sure that
we can continue to, you know,

01:45:57.630 --> 01:45:59.160
fund those treatments, you know,

01:45:59.160 --> 01:46:01.170
normally through pass
through arrangements,

01:46:01.170 --> 01:46:03.600
whether providers pass
through the costs directly

01:46:03.600 --> 01:46:04.920
to NHS England.

01:46:04.920 --> 01:46:07.650
So I think we've recognised
some of those risks,

01:46:07.650 --> 01:46:10.980
and I think in these proposals
tried to make sure that we,

01:46:10.980 --> 01:46:13.590
you know, get that population health focus

01:46:13.590 --> 01:46:15.180
on the appropriate services

01:46:15.180 --> 01:46:19.350
at the same time as not
losing the kind of rigour of

01:46:19.350 --> 01:46:21.506
national standards and clinical policies.

01:46:21.506 --> 01:46:22.866
- Okay, thank you.

01:46:22.866 --> 01:46:24.106
Andrew?

01:46:24.106 --> 01:46:26.719
- So, I think most ICBs would welcome this

01:46:26.719 --> 01:46:27.552
as an initiative-
- Yeah.

01:46:27.552 --> 01:46:28.830
- 'Cause it means, you know,

01:46:28.830 --> 01:46:31.110
there's a lot more kind of localism

01:46:31.110 --> 01:46:34.857
in terms of looking after their patients.

01:46:34.857 --> 01:46:39.090
The one issue historically
has been around money.

01:46:39.090 --> 01:46:42.420
And just, I couldn't find it in the paper,

01:46:42.420 --> 01:46:46.500
who is holding the financial
risk for this coming year?

01:46:46.500 --> 01:46:49.920
And what happens after
the transitional period?

01:46:49.920 --> 01:46:54.000
- Yeah, so during '23-'24,

01:46:54.000 --> 01:46:58.860
financial liability will continue
to remain with NHS England

01:46:58.860 --> 01:47:00.210
and that reflects the kind of

01:47:00.210 --> 01:47:02.883
joint commissioning
arrangements that are in place.

01:47:04.350 --> 01:47:06.720
We will have to come back to the board

01:47:06.720 --> 01:47:08.970
when we get ready for decisions around

01:47:08.970 --> 01:47:11.759
fully delegating these services to ICBs,

01:47:11.759 --> 01:47:14.460
'cause that will be the point
at which the money will then

01:47:14.460 --> 01:47:17.130
transfer across to those ICBs.

01:47:17.130 --> 01:47:21.813
And so, it's probably a good
opportunity during '23-'24

01:47:21.813 --> 01:47:23.550
to really test, you know,

01:47:23.550 --> 01:47:26.250
some of the mechanics of
that and how it works and,

01:47:26.250 --> 01:47:28.759
you know, ICB kind of decisions,

01:47:28.759 --> 01:47:31.410
because we do need to
make sure that we kind of

01:47:31.410 --> 01:47:35.550
don't lose control of a
very large area of spend.

01:47:35.550 --> 01:47:39.150
So I think an important
point, 'but 23-'24,

01:47:39.150 --> 01:47:40.590
it sits with NHS England.

01:47:40.590 --> 01:47:42.000
- And it's at that point, I think, is it,

01:47:42.000 --> 01:47:45.480
that Mark's point about
that extra tension,

01:47:45.480 --> 01:47:47.100
that's where we need to
be very comfortable about

01:47:47.100 --> 01:47:49.020
the tension that Mark draws out?

01:47:49.020 --> 01:47:49.853
Is that right?

01:47:49.853 --> 01:47:50.760
- Yeah, that's absolutely right.

01:47:50.760 --> 01:47:53.490
And there were all sorts
of other safeguards

01:47:53.490 --> 01:47:55.830
not fully described in this paper

01:47:55.830 --> 01:47:58.950
that will try and make
sure that we don't have

01:47:58.950 --> 01:48:01.320
any kind of inappropriate proliferation

01:48:01.320 --> 01:48:03.030
of specialised services,

01:48:03.030 --> 01:48:06.030
whilst at the same time recognising that

01:48:06.030 --> 01:48:08.226
we do want ICBs to think about

01:48:08.226 --> 01:48:11.610
the needs of their local populations

01:48:11.610 --> 01:48:13.320
and consider whether some services

01:48:13.320 --> 01:48:15.180
actually may be appropriate to be provided

01:48:15.180 --> 01:48:16.650
on a more local basis.

01:48:16.650 --> 01:48:19.530
But as I said, national
standards in place, you know,

01:48:19.530 --> 01:48:21.780
particularly in areas where, you know,

01:48:21.780 --> 01:48:24.226
very strong evidence
around kind of, you know,

01:48:24.226 --> 01:48:27.600
maximum-minimum volumes for procedures,

01:48:27.600 --> 01:48:30.240
whether that's in congenital
heart disease surfaces

01:48:30.240 --> 01:48:31.199
or other areas.

01:48:31.199 --> 01:48:34.493
You know, we'll continue to
have those safeguards in place.

01:48:34.493 --> 01:48:36.690
- Do we know, the question I had was

01:48:36.690 --> 01:48:37.710
as we shift from

01:48:37.710 --> 01:48:41.987
a provider-based to a
population-based insight

01:48:43.350 --> 01:48:47.173
or process,

01:48:47.173 --> 01:48:48.300
what does it do?

01:48:48.300 --> 01:48:51.420
What do we think it does
to demand for services?

01:48:51.420 --> 01:48:55.740
- Well, what I think importantly
it does is it provides,

01:48:55.740 --> 01:48:59.010
you know, visibility
for the first time to-

01:48:59.010 --> 01:49:00.437
- No, I completely agree.

01:49:00.437 --> 01:49:02.250
I highly agree with it.

01:49:02.250 --> 01:49:06.030
Head for population base
and also give the ICBs more.

01:49:06.030 --> 01:49:08.640
I mean, if they're going to
look after their populations

01:49:08.640 --> 01:49:09.870
and analyse their populations,

01:49:09.870 --> 01:49:11.760
actually the next step ought to be,

01:49:11.760 --> 01:49:13.770
and what services do we commission for you

01:49:13.770 --> 01:49:15.810
where we have that ability?

01:49:15.810 --> 01:49:16.770
So I completely agree.

01:49:16.770 --> 01:49:18.090
I was just wondering what it does

01:49:18.090 --> 01:49:22.230
to the levels of demand pressure
for particular services,

01:49:22.230 --> 01:49:25.680
if it's driven by aggregated
population health metrics

01:49:25.680 --> 01:49:29.130
as opposed to provider insight
as to presuming an offer.

01:49:29.130 --> 01:49:32.640
- Well, I think '23-'24 begins
to provide us an opportunity

01:49:32.640 --> 01:49:35.829
to understand that a bit
better in kind of, you know,

01:49:35.829 --> 01:49:38.936
the constraints of kind of
joint working agreement.

01:49:38.936 --> 01:49:39.769
- [Richard] Yeah.

01:49:39.769 --> 01:49:41.490
- But I think it's something
that we'll have to kind of

01:49:41.490 --> 01:49:43.203
keep under constant kind of-
- [Richard] Yeah.

01:49:43.203 --> 01:49:44.403
- You know, evaluations-

01:49:44.403 --> 01:49:45.840
- [Richard] It will emerge-
- As we do that.

01:49:45.840 --> 01:49:49.083
But, you know, what we do
hope is it will result in

01:49:49.083 --> 01:49:53.490
local kind of systems
thinking more clearly about

01:49:53.490 --> 01:49:55.320
what the needs are of those populations.

01:49:55.320 --> 01:49:56.153
- [Richard] Yeah.

01:49:56.153 --> 01:49:57.109
- And, you know,

01:49:57.109 --> 01:49:58.363
what the best way of
providing those services

01:49:58.363 --> 01:50:02.070
and actually making sure
that those providers

01:50:02.070 --> 01:50:05.197
who may well continue to
provide services for patients,

01:50:05.197 --> 01:50:07.770
you know, who come in out of area,

01:50:07.770 --> 01:50:10.680
but there is a connection
between those services

01:50:10.680 --> 01:50:12.390
and those local services

01:50:12.390 --> 01:50:14.220
in a way that I think the current system

01:50:14.220 --> 01:50:15.540
is not really set up-
- [Richard] Okay.

01:50:15.540 --> 01:50:17.670
- Particularly well to facilitate.

01:50:17.670 --> 01:50:19.349
- So, Mary, did you have a?

01:50:19.349 --> 01:50:20.826
Go ahead.
- I did.

01:50:20.826 --> 01:50:21.870
This might be a red herring,

01:50:21.870 --> 01:50:24.480
but because you've put in 11B

01:50:24.480 --> 01:50:29.480
that 29 services include a
group of autism services,

01:50:32.400 --> 01:50:35.880
there is great pressure
to get people with autism

01:50:35.880 --> 01:50:36.720
out of hospital.

01:50:36.720 --> 01:50:40.050
And I thought there was
a central need on that.

01:50:40.050 --> 01:50:43.080
Is this going to be
affected by this delegation

01:50:43.080 --> 01:50:45.993
or is it not included at all?

01:50:46.935 --> 01:50:51.502
- So, mental health services, you know-

01:50:51.502 --> 01:50:53.220
- [Mary] autism.

01:50:53.220 --> 01:50:55.860
- With the exception of some
of the really specialised,

01:50:55.860 --> 01:50:58.830
so the high secure services
where we commission, you know,

01:50:58.830 --> 01:51:00.570
Broadmoor, Rampton, Ashworth.

01:51:00.570 --> 01:51:01.403
- [Mary] Mm.

01:51:01.403 --> 01:51:03.790
- But mental health, learning
disability in autism services

01:51:05.130 --> 01:51:08.583
are currently in that category of services

01:51:08.583 --> 01:51:09.930
that we think are suitable

01:51:09.930 --> 01:51:12.630
but are not yet ready
for, you know, delegation.

01:51:12.630 --> 01:51:14.490
And there are two reasons for that.

01:51:14.490 --> 01:51:15.323
Firstly,

01:51:15.323 --> 01:51:17.910
we've already done something
different with these services

01:51:17.910 --> 01:51:19.890
through what we've called

01:51:19.890 --> 01:51:22.020
the provider collaboratives process,

01:51:22.020 --> 01:51:23.760
mental health provider collaboratives,

01:51:23.760 --> 01:51:26.940
where we have already
actually delegated the money

01:51:26.940 --> 01:51:28.860
down to a lead provider

01:51:28.860 --> 01:51:31.830
who then kind of
commissions those services

01:51:31.830 --> 01:51:33.510
across a geographical footprint.

01:51:33.510 --> 01:51:35.280
So we're already getting an integration

01:51:35.280 --> 01:51:36.480
and better integration

01:51:36.480 --> 01:51:39.690
and linking up community
services through that route.

01:51:39.690 --> 01:51:41.520
But we also recognise that

01:51:41.520 --> 01:51:44.010
there are some significant quality issues

01:51:44.010 --> 01:51:45.330
that we need to address

01:51:45.330 --> 01:51:47.970
within inpatient services in particular.

01:51:47.970 --> 01:51:49.710
And so, I think for now

01:51:49.710 --> 01:51:52.627
we felt it was appropriate
for NHS England to kind of,

01:51:52.627 --> 01:51:56.190
you know, continues to hold
and retain that responsibility

01:51:56.190 --> 01:51:57.540
for those services.

01:51:57.540 --> 01:52:00.240
But of course, for joint
committees that we're setting up,

01:52:00.240 --> 01:52:02.790
we'll provide a mechanism for now having

01:52:02.790 --> 01:52:06.240
more joined up conversations
with our integrated care boards

01:52:06.240 --> 01:52:08.070
to try and address some of those issues

01:52:08.070 --> 01:52:10.620
and try and make sure
that we make more progress

01:52:10.620 --> 01:52:14.550
in moving patients out of
those inpatient settings.

01:52:14.550 --> 01:52:16.052
- [Mary] Thank you.

01:52:16.052 --> 01:52:21.052
- So, John, I'll just make
sure just you walk us through

01:52:21.120 --> 01:52:21.953
the question,

01:52:21.953 --> 01:52:23.190
just, I mean there were
highlighted in bold.

01:52:23.190 --> 01:52:24.570
Can we just walk through the,

01:52:24.570 --> 01:52:26.996
I think there were four or five
steps you wanted us to note?

01:52:26.996 --> 01:52:28.449
- [John] Yeah, I'll take
you through each of them.

01:52:28.449 --> 01:52:29.310
So there are five things

01:52:29.310 --> 01:52:30.450
we were asking the board.
- Yeah.

01:52:30.450 --> 01:52:32.688
- The first one, I think
hopefully straightforward.

01:52:32.688 --> 01:52:33.521
- [Richard] Yeah.

01:52:33.521 --> 01:52:36.090
- To note how the commissioning
of specialised services

01:52:36.090 --> 01:52:37.290
has evolved,

01:52:37.290 --> 01:52:40.050
you know, the strengths that
we are looking to maintain.

01:52:40.050 --> 01:52:40.883
- [Richard] Yeah.

01:52:40.883 --> 01:52:41.782
- And the opportunities.
- [Richard] Yeah.

01:52:41.782 --> 01:52:46.782
- The second request is to
endorse the list of services

01:52:46.800 --> 01:52:50.130
that are appropriate for
greater ICB leadership

01:52:50.130 --> 01:52:51.742
from April this year.

01:52:51.742 --> 01:52:52.575
- [Richard] Yeah.

01:52:52.575 --> 01:52:54.570
- As well as those that
we think will be ready

01:52:54.570 --> 01:52:56.250
at the appropriate point in time,

01:52:56.250 --> 01:52:57.755
and the list of services,

01:52:57.755 --> 01:53:00.540
you know, principally those
that are highly specialised,

01:53:00.540 --> 01:53:01.797
but we think must be-

01:53:01.797 --> 01:53:03.064
- [Richard] And they're
attached in the appendix?

01:53:03.064 --> 01:53:04.050
- By NHS England.

01:53:04.050 --> 01:53:06.210
So I dunno if you want
to pause after each,

01:53:06.210 --> 01:53:07.560
or I'll go through all of them?

01:53:07.560 --> 01:53:09.360
- Go through all of them
first, and I'll just see.

01:53:09.360 --> 01:53:12.900
- The third area is to
accept the recommendations

01:53:12.900 --> 01:53:14.640
of the national moderation panel

01:53:14.640 --> 01:53:18.840
to delegate commissioning
responsibility to these nine

01:53:18.840 --> 01:53:21.510
statutory joint committees-
- [Richard] Yeah, yeah.

01:53:21.510 --> 01:53:23.820
- For the 59 services
that we've identified as

01:53:23.820 --> 01:53:25.356
suitable and ready.
- [Richard] Yeah.

01:53:25.356 --> 01:53:26.755
But we still maintain

01:53:26.755 --> 01:53:28.810
accountability.
- [John] We still remain-

01:53:28.810 --> 01:53:31.110
- '23-'24, yeah.
- We still remain accountable

01:53:31.110 --> 01:53:32.400
for all of those services.
- [Richard] Yeah.

01:53:32.400 --> 01:53:35.280
- And even when we fully
delegate them in due course.

01:53:35.280 --> 01:53:36.630
- [Richard] Yeah.

01:53:36.630 --> 01:53:40.410
- The fourth area we're
asking is just to approve

01:53:40.410 --> 01:53:41.700
the joint working model,

01:53:41.700 --> 01:53:43.710
which is essentially the joint committees

01:53:43.710 --> 01:53:48.690
where NHS England comes
together with the ICBs

01:53:48.690 --> 01:53:50.760
in that geographical patch

01:53:50.760 --> 01:53:52.137
and really to check that

01:53:52.137 --> 01:53:54.660
the board is happy to
delegate authority to

01:53:54.660 --> 01:53:58.570
our regional directors to sign
the joint working agreement

01:53:58.570 --> 01:54:03.060
that we have developed
with our legal team.

01:54:03.060 --> 01:54:04.560
And then finally,

01:54:04.560 --> 01:54:06.840
the fifth area is just to note the move to

01:54:06.840 --> 01:54:09.210
population-based budgets

01:54:09.210 --> 01:54:13.380
to support this whole move to
more integrated commissioning.

01:54:13.380 --> 01:54:16.080
And actually, the importance,
I think, of that shift

01:54:16.080 --> 01:54:19.260
in terms of supporting
integrated care systems

01:54:19.260 --> 01:54:22.650
to address inequalities in access to

01:54:22.650 --> 01:54:24.693
and outcomes from specialised services.

01:54:25.830 --> 01:54:27.277
- So the series of steps,

01:54:27.277 --> 01:54:32.250
I mean, personally, I think
that this is very sensible.

01:54:32.250 --> 01:54:35.850
It goes in the direction of
more devolution down to ICBs

01:54:35.850 --> 01:54:37.170
and empowering ICBs.

01:54:37.170 --> 01:54:38.400
And this is specialised commissioning

01:54:38.400 --> 01:54:40.690
where we maintain accountability,

01:54:40.690 --> 01:54:43.020
actually, long run accountability,

01:54:43.020 --> 01:54:45.810
but we're jointly working
with ICBs as a first step

01:54:45.810 --> 01:54:47.717
and then gradually going further.

01:54:47.717 --> 01:54:50.360
Mark, we hear your cautionary words,

01:54:50.360 --> 01:54:51.990
to be quite careful, actually,

01:54:51.990 --> 01:54:55.860
we don't suddenly either
lose control or lose quality

01:54:55.860 --> 01:54:58.083
by overly delegating to, you know,

01:54:59.580 --> 01:55:02.280
a group of individuals, perhaps.

01:55:02.280 --> 01:55:06.177
But we can monitor that as we go through.

01:55:06.177 --> 01:55:09.370
And I also see in next steps
the statement of, you know,

01:55:09.370 --> 01:55:10.860
we're going to work closely with the ICBs

01:55:10.860 --> 01:55:12.510
through the joint statutories.

01:55:12.510 --> 01:55:15.300
And there were two ICBs in
particular in South London

01:55:15.300 --> 01:55:16.860
who want to take more responsibility for

01:55:16.860 --> 01:55:18.870
appropriate services
earlier feeling ready to go.

01:55:18.870 --> 01:55:22.170
And you are going to work with
them on that as a pathfinder,

01:55:22.170 --> 01:55:26.455
which is also quite interesting
and will inform us, I think,

01:55:26.455 --> 01:55:29.584
as to Mark's question.

01:55:29.584 --> 01:55:31.380
So before I ask the board

01:55:31.380 --> 01:55:33.630
if they're happy to support those five,

01:55:33.630 --> 01:55:35.010
either to note or to recommend,

01:55:35.010 --> 01:55:36.240
can I just go around for last comments?

01:55:36.240 --> 01:55:37.920
So Mark, I can see
David, I can see Amanda.

01:55:37.920 --> 01:55:39.362
Do you want to go first?

01:55:39.362 --> 01:55:40.660
- Yeah, actually, it's
a specific question,

01:55:40.660 --> 01:55:42.270
but I just thought it might be useful

01:55:42.270 --> 01:55:45.510
to get John to just tease this out for us

01:55:45.510 --> 01:55:47.670
in relation to the final recommendation.

01:55:47.670 --> 01:55:48.503
- Right.

01:55:48.503 --> 01:55:50.400
- So the move to population-based budgets,

01:55:50.400 --> 01:55:52.800
which I have to say for all
the reasons we've discussed,

01:55:52.800 --> 01:55:56.130
I think it's really obvious
why that has benefit,

01:55:56.130 --> 01:55:58.050
but could you just say
a couple of words on

01:55:58.050 --> 01:56:00.570
how we're going to prevent destabilisation

01:56:00.570 --> 01:56:01.620
as a consequence of that?

01:56:01.620 --> 01:56:03.960
Because I'm conscious
that there are providers

01:56:03.960 --> 01:56:06.881
who are anxious about the
potential implications.

01:56:06.881 --> 01:56:11.881
- Yeah, so I think a couple
of things to say on this.

01:56:13.320 --> 01:56:17.370
Firstly in '23-'24,

01:56:17.370 --> 01:56:20.700
you know, the move to
population-based budgets is important,

01:56:20.700 --> 01:56:23.437
but the way the money is
going to end up flowing back,

01:56:23.437 --> 01:56:25.470
you know, to providers

01:56:25.470 --> 01:56:28.290
is essentially, you know, they
are going to end up getting

01:56:28.290 --> 01:56:30.480
in '23-'24, you know,

01:56:30.480 --> 01:56:32.250
essentially the same kind of income

01:56:32.250 --> 01:56:33.300
that they're currently getting.

01:56:33.300 --> 01:56:38.300
What becomes really important
is from '24-'25 onward,

01:56:38.916 --> 01:56:41.520
yeah, '24-'25 onwards,

01:56:41.520 --> 01:56:43.860
where we will be looking at introducing

01:56:43.860 --> 01:56:48.741
a new kind of needs-based
weighted allocation formula.

01:56:48.741 --> 01:56:50.250
And, you know,

01:56:50.250 --> 01:56:54.990
there is obviously a further
debate and discussion to have

01:56:54.990 --> 01:56:56.280
probably with this board

01:56:56.280 --> 01:56:58.710
around then what the pace of change is

01:56:58.710 --> 01:57:02.220
as we look to move kind
of those allocations

01:57:02.220 --> 01:57:04.140
towards target.

01:57:04.140 --> 01:57:06.594
So that is work that is still ongoing.

01:57:06.594 --> 01:57:08.640
You know, we've been
talking just to Julian

01:57:08.640 --> 01:57:10.350
in the last few days about that,

01:57:10.350 --> 01:57:12.968
but I think during '23-'24,

01:57:12.968 --> 01:57:15.420
I think, you know,
first step is, you know,

01:57:15.420 --> 01:57:18.480
make allocations on a population basis.

01:57:18.480 --> 01:57:22.530
But '24-'25 onwards will
be when we will look at

01:57:22.530 --> 01:57:26.910
what the, you know, appropriate
pace to move people towards

01:57:26.910 --> 01:57:29.430
kind of these new needs-weighted,

01:57:29.430 --> 01:57:32.040
you know, allocations should be.

01:57:32.040 --> 01:57:34.200
I don't know if Julian
wants to add anything.

01:57:34.200 --> 01:57:35.883
- I mean, look, I think that is,

01:57:37.620 --> 01:57:42.620
we need to make sure it
cannot just be a free for all,

01:57:43.020 --> 01:57:44.160
to put it quite frankly.

01:57:44.160 --> 01:57:48.141
We're going to have to put
some controls in place,

01:57:48.141 --> 01:57:51.060
I mean, just for some of the
reasons Mark has discussed,

01:57:51.060 --> 01:57:53.040
as well as the reason
that you have raised.

01:57:53.040 --> 01:57:55.050
And I think that is
something we should work out,

01:57:55.050 --> 01:57:57.120
when is the right time to come back here

01:57:57.120 --> 01:57:59.100
and formally set out

01:57:59.100 --> 01:58:02.490
what we are planning
those sort of rules to be,

01:58:02.490 --> 01:58:06.030
at least in the first year
of operation, '24-'25,

01:58:06.030 --> 01:58:08.194
and how we see it operating beyond,

01:58:08.194 --> 01:58:10.200
and actually what we've
learned through the first,

01:58:10.200 --> 01:58:12.930
you know, six, seven months of operation.

01:58:12.930 --> 01:58:16.440
I mean, it'll be early, but as you said,

01:58:16.440 --> 01:58:20.010
the real thing here, and
to Richard's question is,

01:58:20.010 --> 01:58:22.387
there are areas of the country
which are not getting the,

01:58:22.387 --> 01:58:24.150
you know, people in the country

01:58:24.150 --> 01:58:26.220
who are not getting the
services they should get

01:58:26.220 --> 01:58:31.220
because they are not near
major centres of provision.

01:58:31.620 --> 01:58:36.030
We have to find a way in which
we are actually providing

01:58:36.030 --> 01:58:37.380
a more consistent level of service.

01:58:37.380 --> 01:58:39.330
I mean, my anticipation
is actually, if anything,

01:58:39.330 --> 01:58:43.620
we risk, from a financial
perspective, stimulating demand,

01:58:43.620 --> 01:58:45.900
but that would actually
be the right outcome

01:58:45.900 --> 01:58:47.700
in terms of people getting
the services and care

01:58:47.700 --> 01:58:48.660
that they should.

01:58:48.660 --> 01:58:49.950
- Thank you.

01:58:49.950 --> 01:58:51.990
I recall challenging
you on exactly the point

01:58:51.990 --> 01:58:54.540
we're now bringing to fruition
about three years ago, John.

01:58:54.540 --> 01:58:55.741
So it's great to see this,

01:58:55.741 --> 01:58:57.447
but I think just important to
have that on the safeguard.

01:58:57.447 --> 01:58:58.350
- The thing I would say

01:58:58.350 --> 01:59:00.060
for some of our major
specialist sentences,

01:59:00.060 --> 01:59:02.610
their challenges in the next two years,

01:59:02.610 --> 01:59:03.990
they've got to work out how they improve

01:59:03.990 --> 01:59:05.310
their own referral networks

01:59:05.310 --> 01:59:08.400
into bits of the country
they haven't so far reached.

01:59:08.400 --> 01:59:09.233
That is actually their challenge

01:59:09.233 --> 01:59:10.923
so that we don't lead to-

01:59:12.684 --> 01:59:14.223
- Postcode whatever it's call.

01:59:15.120 --> 01:59:18.060
Okay, I can see Mark,
then Mike, then David,

01:59:18.060 --> 01:59:21.314
and then we'll bring this
very good item to a close.

01:59:21.314 --> 01:59:22.147
So, Mark?

01:59:22.147 --> 01:59:23.430
- So, I suppose my plea is that

01:59:23.430 --> 01:59:25.410
as part of the evaluation of this,

01:59:25.410 --> 01:59:27.600
we measure clinical outcome measures.

01:59:27.600 --> 01:59:28.521
- [Richard] Yeah.

01:59:28.521 --> 01:59:30.180
- We tend to measure inputs far too often,

01:59:30.180 --> 01:59:34.320
and that we know particularly
for complex surgery,

01:59:34.320 --> 01:59:37.290
the volume of operations
that individual surgeons do

01:59:37.290 --> 01:59:39.450
makes the difference to their skills.

01:59:39.450 --> 01:59:40.800
We've seen things go wrong

01:59:40.800 --> 01:59:44.183
in paediatric cardiothoracic surgery,

01:59:44.183 --> 01:59:47.070
and, you know, frankly, as a consumer,

01:59:47.070 --> 01:59:49.890
I would go a thousand miles

01:59:49.890 --> 01:59:52.893
to get my child operated on competently.

01:59:54.330 --> 01:59:55.163
- Got it.

01:59:55.163 --> 01:59:56.274
Okay, thank you.

01:59:56.274 --> 01:59:57.273
Mike?

01:59:57.273 --> 01:59:59.637
- Just by striking the relative size of

01:59:59.637 --> 02:00:02.973
the different geographies
in population terms,

02:00:03.900 --> 02:00:06.330
to the point that Julian's made around

02:00:06.330 --> 02:00:09.430
the desire to delegate and improve service

02:00:09.430 --> 02:00:12.524
in some specific geographies,

02:00:12.524 --> 02:00:14.310
if you run it the other way around,

02:00:14.310 --> 02:00:15.431
do you think there's a danger that

02:00:15.431 --> 02:00:18.420
the joint committees will start
joining up with each other

02:00:18.420 --> 02:00:21.930
in consolidating services
for economic reasons?

02:00:21.930 --> 02:00:24.240
And is that a good thing or a bad thing?

02:00:24.240 --> 02:00:27.450
How do we envisage that
potentially working in the future?

02:00:27.450 --> 02:00:32.250
- Yeah, so, I think one
of the challenges here is

02:00:32.250 --> 02:00:35.910
integrated care boards vary
in size quite considerably.

02:00:35.910 --> 02:00:38.900
And so what we did to support ICBs

02:00:38.900 --> 02:00:40.830
in thinking through these proposals

02:00:40.830 --> 02:00:43.830
is essentially to set out
what we felt, you know,

02:00:43.830 --> 02:00:46.150
were indicative population levels

02:00:47.040 --> 02:00:49.260
to plan and commission these services

02:00:49.260 --> 02:00:50.910
on a sensible footprint.

02:00:50.910 --> 02:00:54.300
And that has effectively,
you know, driven, you know,

02:00:54.300 --> 02:00:58.800
these kind of nine
collaborations of ICBs together.

02:00:58.800 --> 02:01:01.200
And actually I think, you
know, for a long time,

02:01:01.200 --> 02:01:03.780
well before I got involved
in specialised services,

02:01:03.780 --> 02:01:04.613
I think, you know,

02:01:04.613 --> 02:01:06.600
things have tended to be organised around

02:01:06.600 --> 02:01:11.190
kind of nine or 10 geographies,
very similar to this.

02:01:11.190 --> 02:01:14.460
So I think, you know,
these footprints, I think,

02:01:14.460 --> 02:01:17.880
are very much connected with

02:01:17.880 --> 02:01:20.711
natural kind of patient clinical flows.

02:01:20.711 --> 02:01:24.570
And I think it's fantastic that

02:01:24.570 --> 02:01:26.910
the ICBs are going to
collaborate in this way.

02:01:26.910 --> 02:01:31.110
And I have already heard
how, you know, those ICBs,

02:01:31.110 --> 02:01:32.730
you know, are beginning to think about

02:01:32.730 --> 02:01:34.470
whether some of those joint committees

02:01:34.470 --> 02:01:37.830
can provide a vehicle for
considering other services,

02:01:37.830 --> 02:01:39.633
say, non-specialized services.

02:01:40.470 --> 02:01:43.200
You know, could be, for
example, ambulance services,

02:01:43.200 --> 02:01:45.810
but maybe are best thought of on a

02:01:45.810 --> 02:01:48.930
more regional kind of footprint and basis.

02:01:48.930 --> 02:01:50.400
So I think, you know,

02:01:50.400 --> 02:01:52.650
there may be some variation and change,

02:01:52.650 --> 02:01:53.831
but I think, you know,

02:01:53.831 --> 02:01:57.300
a sensible kind of set of
geographical footprints,

02:01:57.300 --> 02:01:59.940
which are certainly all big enough

02:01:59.940 --> 02:02:03.330
to be commissioning for sets
of services we are describing

02:02:03.330 --> 02:02:04.440
for delegation.

02:02:04.440 --> 02:02:05.273
- Thank you.

02:02:05.273 --> 02:02:06.600
David, last point, I think?

02:02:07.444 --> 02:02:10.560
- And so, John, this is
a very, very clear read,

02:02:10.560 --> 02:02:13.170
so thank you, and I understood it.

02:02:13.170 --> 02:02:15.480
Just coming to the point of

02:02:15.480 --> 02:02:18.273
what you're going to ask
us to agree, Richard,

02:02:19.436 --> 02:02:23.790
I, too, Amanda went to the
fifth of the recommendations

02:02:23.790 --> 02:02:25.890
and I just wonder whether
that might be worded

02:02:25.890 --> 02:02:29.370
to be consistent with paragraph five,

02:02:29.370 --> 02:02:33.240
where you set out the national
standards, clinical access,

02:02:33.240 --> 02:02:34.410
et cetera,

02:02:34.410 --> 02:02:37.290
because a way of mitigating
people just go off

02:02:37.290 --> 02:02:39.219
and do their own thing.

02:02:39.219 --> 02:02:40.350
So, if I can,

02:02:40.350 --> 02:02:42.500
nobody's used that
phrase, I have, just to.

02:02:44.340 --> 02:02:46.320
Then one of the things

02:02:46.320 --> 02:02:47.910
that you've been doing
over the years, John,

02:02:47.910 --> 02:02:50.730
is applying those principles
in the way that you've done it.

02:02:50.730 --> 02:02:55.380
And because we as a board can't
delegate our responsibility,

02:02:55.380 --> 02:02:57.300
we are still responsible

02:02:57.300 --> 02:03:00.180
and making sure people operate
within those principles

02:03:00.180 --> 02:03:01.080
seems to me-

02:03:01.080 --> 02:03:01.913
- [Richard] So you're simply-

02:03:01.913 --> 02:03:03.510
- One way of actually securing it.

02:03:03.510 --> 02:03:04.620
- Yeah, we'd add to that approval

02:03:04.620 --> 02:03:07.495
in consistent with the
standards and principles

02:03:07.495 --> 02:03:09.562
in place at the moment.

02:03:09.562 --> 02:03:10.860
- So maybe just to reassure the board,

02:03:10.860 --> 02:03:12.420
for joint agreement,

02:03:12.420 --> 02:03:14.850
which is quite long, and I
haven't shared with the board,

02:03:14.850 --> 02:03:16.650
absolutely builds throughout that

02:03:16.650 --> 02:03:18.210
the requirement to follow

02:03:18.210 --> 02:03:21.180
the national service
specification standards

02:03:21.180 --> 02:03:24.030
and clinical policies
that we have put in place.

02:03:24.030 --> 02:03:26.640
- I wouldn't expect any
less, but I wanted to-

02:03:26.640 --> 02:03:27.473
- [Richard] Yeah.

02:03:27.473 --> 02:03:29.970
- Just is it important that
the minutes of this meeting

02:03:29.970 --> 02:03:31.140
actually captures that?

02:03:31.140 --> 02:03:31.973
- No, I'd like to.

02:03:31.973 --> 02:03:32.806
We will capture that.

02:03:32.806 --> 02:03:33.639
We should capture that,

02:03:33.639 --> 02:03:36.480
then we should approve this

02:03:36.480 --> 02:03:38.820
on the basis that it's in line with

02:03:38.820 --> 02:03:40.950
or consistent with the
standards and principles

02:03:40.950 --> 02:03:42.063
currently operating.

02:03:45.120 --> 02:03:47.883
We'll find the wording, if
everyone's okay with that.

02:03:47.883 --> 02:03:48.780
I'll just say, John,

02:03:48.780 --> 02:03:52.020
thank you for, again
actually, a masterclass again.

02:03:52.020 --> 02:03:53.160
I think you're really good in this space.

02:03:53.160 --> 02:03:56.640
Thank you for your
leadership in this space.

02:03:56.640 --> 02:04:00.540
And just to build off Amanda
and Mark's comments, actually,

02:04:00.540 --> 02:04:01.890
I think we just need to make sure

02:04:01.890 --> 02:04:04.371
as we transition through this,

02:04:04.371 --> 02:04:09.371
that we interact with the
special services providers

02:04:10.620 --> 02:04:11.453
and we listen to them

02:04:11.453 --> 02:04:12.687
and we communicate well with them

02:04:12.687 --> 02:04:16.456
and we are very sensitive
to them, because in the end,

02:04:16.456 --> 02:04:21.150
they're a really important
contributor to patient services.

02:04:21.150 --> 02:04:24.240
And I just reflect Mark's
comment that, actually,

02:04:24.240 --> 02:04:25.657
in the particular example,

02:04:25.657 --> 02:04:27.540
"I'd take my child a thousand miles

02:04:27.540 --> 02:04:29.310
for the particular service,"

02:04:29.310 --> 02:04:30.170
what we don't want to find, actually,

02:04:30.170 --> 02:04:33.896
is we disrupt those specialist providers.

02:04:33.896 --> 02:04:35.850
And there is a level of anxiety about

02:04:35.850 --> 02:04:40.410
how this new system rolls out, so-

02:04:40.410 --> 02:04:41.243
- [John] Great.

02:04:41.243 --> 02:04:42.300
Thank you.
- Thank you.

02:04:42.300 --> 02:04:45.360
Can we go, then, to the
last substantive item,

02:04:45.360 --> 02:04:47.760
which again, they're all
crunchy items, actually,

02:04:47.760 --> 02:04:49.020
really important.

02:04:49.020 --> 02:04:52.380
And so, Tim, it's, I think,
to you and Kath, I think,

02:04:52.380 --> 02:04:54.540
on digital data technology,

02:04:54.540 --> 02:04:56.949
next three years forward, strategic steps.

02:04:56.949 --> 02:04:57.782
- Terrific.

02:04:57.782 --> 02:04:58.615
Thank you, Chair.

02:04:58.615 --> 02:05:01.170
And we'll go expeditiously through this,

02:05:01.170 --> 02:05:02.940
taking the paper as read.

02:05:02.940 --> 02:05:06.240
And we're going to
invert the usual process

02:05:06.240 --> 02:05:08.866
and I'm going to ask Kathy
to give the overview.

02:05:08.866 --> 02:05:12.060
And then as a clinician data scientist,

02:05:12.060 --> 02:05:15.361
I'm going to give a little
colour to some of the specific.

02:05:15.361 --> 02:05:17.490
So Kathy, please.

02:05:17.490 --> 02:05:19.260
- Thank you. For those
of you who don't know me,

02:05:19.260 --> 02:05:20.957
I'm the director of the Joint DHSC

02:05:20.957 --> 02:05:24.270
and NHS England Digital Policy Unit.

02:05:24.270 --> 02:05:27.180
As you mentioned at the
start of the meeting, Amanda,

02:05:27.180 --> 02:05:28.440
this has been a big week

02:05:28.440 --> 02:05:30.660
in terms of digital data and technology,

02:05:30.660 --> 02:05:34.530
bringing together NHS
Digital with NHS England.

02:05:34.530 --> 02:05:37.800
And that means new
responsibilities for NHS England

02:05:37.800 --> 02:05:42.270
in terms of taking on live
services such as the NHS Spine

02:05:42.270 --> 02:05:45.930
and also taking on NHS
Digital's role in collecting,

02:05:45.930 --> 02:05:49.200
analysing, disseminating
and publishing data.

02:05:49.200 --> 02:05:53.040
And we've published a document

02:05:53.040 --> 02:05:55.650
setting out the approach to protecting

02:05:55.650 --> 02:05:57.870
and safely using that data

02:05:57.870 --> 02:05:59.638
and five key promises,

02:05:59.638 --> 02:06:03.063
which is out there
transparently for people to see.

02:06:04.020 --> 02:06:05.670
And I think as we've
worked towards that merger,

02:06:05.670 --> 02:06:08.280
we've already started
to see the benefits of

02:06:08.280 --> 02:06:10.500
working in a more efficient
and integrated way,

02:06:10.500 --> 02:06:13.050
which we look forward
to taking advantage of

02:06:13.050 --> 02:06:15.690
in the months and years to come.

02:06:15.690 --> 02:06:17.880
So the paper, which I
won't go into detail,

02:06:17.880 --> 02:06:19.380
but just summarise,

02:06:19.380 --> 02:06:23.003
sets out our strategy for how
digital data and technology

02:06:23.003 --> 02:06:26.310
can support better services and outcomes

02:06:26.310 --> 02:06:27.990
for health and social care.

02:06:27.990 --> 02:06:30.900
And that was set out last
summer in two documents

02:06:30.900 --> 02:06:33.482
published by DHSE and NHS England,

02:06:33.482 --> 02:06:35.433
"The Plan for Digital
Health and Social Care,"

02:06:35.433 --> 02:06:38.070
and "The Data Strategy: Data Saves Lives."

02:06:38.070 --> 02:06:40.830
And they cover three broad themes.

02:06:40.830 --> 02:06:43.320
So firstly, digitising.

02:06:43.320 --> 02:06:46.050
So that's providing core
infrastructure across the NHS

02:06:46.050 --> 02:06:48.750
and across social care services.

02:06:48.750 --> 02:06:49.650
For example,

02:06:49.650 --> 02:06:52.170
rolling out electronic patient records

02:06:52.170 --> 02:06:54.750
and improving the ones
already in existence

02:06:54.750 --> 02:06:56.310
and supporting social care providers

02:06:56.310 --> 02:06:59.610
to also digitise their records.

02:06:59.610 --> 02:07:03.000
Connecting, so that's using
digital data technology

02:07:03.000 --> 02:07:06.869
to help join up services
and help integrate.

02:07:06.869 --> 02:07:09.810
And examples of that would be

02:07:09.810 --> 02:07:11.340
launching the procurement of

02:07:11.340 --> 02:07:13.680
the federated data platform last month

02:07:13.680 --> 02:07:17.670
and also investing in a series
of secure data environments

02:07:17.670 --> 02:07:20.250
where approved users can safely use data

02:07:20.250 --> 02:07:23.070
without it leaving that environment.

02:07:23.070 --> 02:07:24.330
And then finally,

02:07:24.330 --> 02:07:27.480
using these digitised
and connected platforms

02:07:27.480 --> 02:07:29.670
to transform services.

02:07:29.670 --> 02:07:32.340
So examples as set out
in the paper include

02:07:32.340 --> 02:07:35.793
supporting virtual wards, which
Sarah talked about earlier,

02:07:37.350 --> 02:07:39.210
rolling out digital tools

02:07:39.210 --> 02:07:42.390
that can help clinical decision
making and help efficiency,

02:07:42.390 --> 02:07:44.460
and also, as Amanda mentioned earlier,

02:07:44.460 --> 02:07:46.260
really looking to the NHS app

02:07:46.260 --> 02:07:48.750
to help be the front door for patients

02:07:48.750 --> 02:07:53.283
and provide more services
and access through that.

02:07:54.785 --> 02:07:57.240
The paper also sets
out some of the impacts

02:07:57.240 --> 02:07:59.010
that we're looking to achieve from this.

02:07:59.010 --> 02:08:02.370
So for patients, for example,

02:08:02.370 --> 02:08:04.890
2 million remote prescriptions a month now

02:08:04.890 --> 02:08:09.540
through the NHS app, which is
more convenient for people,

02:08:09.540 --> 02:08:13.380
better outcomes and support
for clinical decision making.

02:08:13.380 --> 02:08:15.540
So we talk about some of the stroke tools

02:08:15.540 --> 02:08:17.160
that are helping make decisions

02:08:17.160 --> 02:08:19.800
and improve outcomes for patients there

02:08:19.800 --> 02:08:21.990
and also contribute towards efficiency.

02:08:21.990 --> 02:08:25.950
So we've found research
shows that trusts with

02:08:25.950 --> 02:08:27.810
high levels of digital maturity

02:08:27.810 --> 02:08:30.183
are 10% more efficient than their peers.

02:08:31.020 --> 02:08:32.460
Finally, just to mention that

02:08:32.460 --> 02:08:35.310
whilst digital tools are
a really great opportunity

02:08:35.310 --> 02:08:37.710
for a new way of engaging with patients,

02:08:37.710 --> 02:08:40.440
we also need to be mindful
of digital inclusion,

02:08:40.440 --> 02:08:44.010
and we intend to publish a
framework on digital inclusion

02:08:44.010 --> 02:08:47.040
to provide guidance later on this year.

02:08:47.040 --> 02:08:48.360
Back to you, Tim.

02:08:48.360 --> 02:08:49.410
- Kathy, thank you very much.

02:08:49.410 --> 02:08:50.986
And let me just say that

02:08:50.986 --> 02:08:54.600
the collaboration that I
get to have with Kathy,

02:08:54.600 --> 02:08:57.960
who reports both into
the NHS and into the DH,

02:08:57.960 --> 02:09:00.810
brings all the DH initiatives

02:09:00.810 --> 02:09:03.000
associated with health and social care

02:09:03.000 --> 02:09:05.790
and the data underpinnings them together

02:09:05.790 --> 02:09:07.317
so that we are working together.

02:09:07.317 --> 02:09:11.719
And one of the most
obvious examples of that

02:09:11.719 --> 02:09:14.340
is the joint cyber unit,

02:09:14.340 --> 02:09:17.250
which covers the entire spectrum

02:09:17.250 --> 02:09:21.030
and enables us to work
very closely together.

02:09:21.030 --> 02:09:24.240
So I'll go very briefly.

02:09:24.240 --> 02:09:27.851
I could easily go on and on
about each of these things.

02:09:27.851 --> 02:09:30.300
As many of my colleagues here know,

02:09:30.300 --> 02:09:31.900
I'm quite passionate about them.

02:09:33.990 --> 02:09:36.990
We have to level up,
if I can use that term,

02:09:36.990 --> 02:09:41.990
the use of electronic
patient records in our trusts

02:09:42.180 --> 02:09:45.079
and in our mental health centres,

02:09:45.079 --> 02:09:47.250
actually, throughout the NHS.

02:09:47.250 --> 02:09:48.660
It is well known,

02:09:48.660 --> 02:09:51.810
documented in literally
hundreds of studies,

02:09:51.810 --> 02:09:54.270
that there are better outcomes

02:09:54.270 --> 02:09:56.190
with an electronic patient record.

02:09:56.190 --> 02:09:57.023
And I'll just,

02:09:57.023 --> 02:10:00.240
at the very core of that
is computerised order entry

02:10:00.240 --> 02:10:02.970
and electronic medication
administration records.

02:10:02.970 --> 02:10:05.670
They have transformed the
delivery of healthcare.

02:10:05.670 --> 02:10:09.060
86% of trusts have an EPR in place.

02:10:09.060 --> 02:10:10.980
That is not 100%.

02:10:10.980 --> 02:10:12.600
It needs to be 100%.

02:10:12.600 --> 02:10:16.710
We have commitments to raise this to 91%

02:10:16.710 --> 02:10:17.610
by the end of this year.

02:10:17.610 --> 02:10:19.879
We are on track to do that,

02:10:19.879 --> 02:10:24.879
and we'll be rolling them out
to the rest of those with,

02:10:26.400 --> 02:10:30.690
there's 2 billion in pounds
in funding committed to this

02:10:30.690 --> 02:10:35.690
and we are on track to make
sure that we get 100% of trusts

02:10:36.120 --> 02:10:36.953
on electronic-

02:10:36.953 --> 02:10:38.100
- [Richard] What would
be the statistics, Tim,

02:10:38.100 --> 02:10:40.290
for primary care practises?

02:10:40.290 --> 02:10:43.800
- So, actually, primary
care in this country

02:10:43.800 --> 02:10:44.647
was one of the first.

02:10:44.647 --> 02:10:46.230
- [Richard] Are we at 100%?

02:10:46.230 --> 02:10:48.000
- Digitally, I couldn't say.

02:10:48.000 --> 02:10:51.392
I would expect we're at 100%.

02:10:51.392 --> 02:10:54.750
- Yeah, and have been, actually,
since the late nineties.

02:10:54.750 --> 02:10:57.960
- So the question is interoperability.

02:10:57.960 --> 02:11:00.453
- So that is a big challenge for us.

02:11:01.590 --> 02:11:04.590
The interoperability, we are
working on, we have a set,

02:11:04.590 --> 02:11:06.450
it's actually one of the
things I was going to mention

02:11:06.450 --> 02:11:07.932
is shared care records.

02:11:07.932 --> 02:11:08.765
- [Richard] Yeah.

02:11:08.765 --> 02:11:11.310
- So when I talk about digitise,
then there's the connect.

02:11:11.310 --> 02:11:12.143
- [Richard] Yeah.

02:11:12.143 --> 02:11:13.290
- And very importantly,

02:11:13.290 --> 02:11:14.760
shared care records are

02:11:14.760 --> 02:11:19.440
one of the several
mechanisms we use to connect.

02:11:19.440 --> 02:11:22.320
It is, from a tech perspective,

02:11:22.320 --> 02:11:25.320
not the most advanced way

02:11:25.320 --> 02:11:30.320
to enable clinicians in
secondary care and primary care

02:11:30.732 --> 02:11:34.350
to have the same information
at the same time.

02:11:34.350 --> 02:11:35.790
But as I go around the country,

02:11:35.790 --> 02:11:40.790
as I was up in Mercyside and
saw a fantastic instance where

02:11:41.610 --> 02:11:45.330
every single clinician
was on the same team

02:11:45.330 --> 02:11:48.570
because they all had the same
primary care, secondary care,

02:11:48.570 --> 02:11:50.610
mental health, social care,

02:11:50.610 --> 02:11:52.380
all seeing the same information

02:11:52.380 --> 02:11:54.270
in the interests of their patients.

02:11:54.270 --> 02:11:58.830
And there wasn't a interoperability
problem between them

02:11:58.830 --> 02:12:01.078
in terms of the transfer of data.

02:12:01.078 --> 02:12:04.710
That's the architecture that we aspire to

02:12:04.710 --> 02:12:05.919
for the whole country.

02:12:05.919 --> 02:12:07.065
- So how did they do that

02:12:07.065 --> 02:12:09.000
and the rest of the country hasn't?

02:12:09.000 --> 02:12:10.800
- Well, actually, there
are different solutions

02:12:10.800 --> 02:12:12.132
in different places.

02:12:12.132 --> 02:12:15.600
And so I think there are

02:12:15.600 --> 02:12:18.900
over eight different shared
care records around the country

02:12:18.900 --> 02:12:22.953
and they are of varying
degrees of development.

02:12:24.150 --> 02:12:27.050
- We're agnostic as to
which particular system-

02:12:27.050 --> 02:12:28.737
- [Tim] As as long as
it gets the job done.

02:12:28.737 --> 02:12:30.968
- And it can link to the FTP?

02:12:30.968 --> 02:12:33.120
- I'll come to the FTP-
- [Richard] Okay.

02:12:33.120 --> 02:12:34.290
- In a minute,

02:12:34.290 --> 02:12:36.661
because what I'm talking about
right now is direct care.

02:12:36.661 --> 02:12:37.494
- [Richard] Okay.

02:12:37.494 --> 02:12:41.640
- So there are four uses
of data in healthcare,

02:12:41.640 --> 02:12:44.250
four types of uses, direct care.

02:12:44.250 --> 02:12:45.840
Usually, direct care,

02:12:45.840 --> 02:12:50.460
the information sits in
a legally enclosed box

02:12:50.460 --> 02:12:52.080
called an electronic patient record

02:12:52.080 --> 02:12:54.450
or a LIM system or a PAC system.

02:12:54.450 --> 02:12:59.100
That is, the access and use of
those is part of the workflow

02:12:59.100 --> 02:13:01.890
of the delivery of direct care.

02:13:01.890 --> 02:13:04.320
Data platforms-
- [Richard] Yeah.

02:13:04.320 --> 02:13:08.901
- Tend to sit outside of
those and are intersectioned,

02:13:08.901 --> 02:13:11.910
where they are sort of highways for data.

02:13:11.910 --> 02:13:13.980
You can also build software systems

02:13:13.980 --> 02:13:18.980
like the electronic
care coordination system

02:13:19.860 --> 02:13:21.960
that is now in 36 trusts-
- [Richard] Yeah.

02:13:21.960 --> 02:13:22.793
- Around the country

02:13:22.793 --> 02:13:27.500
that we are using to enable
the rapid review by clinicians

02:13:28.890 --> 02:13:32.010
of their elective waiting
list, for example,

02:13:32.010 --> 02:13:37.010
which has cleaned up 14%, on
average, of that waiting list.

02:13:37.899 --> 02:13:39.900
So really important.

02:13:39.900 --> 02:13:44.250
But that is data that has
been extracted from the EPRs

02:13:44.250 --> 02:13:47.070
and then manipulated to provide a service,

02:13:47.070 --> 02:13:51.366
often to the managers, but
also often to clinicians.

02:13:51.366 --> 02:13:55.830
Importantly, that information
is often and most,

02:13:55.830 --> 02:13:57.390
and whenever we can,

02:13:57.390 --> 02:13:59.730
used in a de-identified format.

02:13:59.730 --> 02:14:00.665
- [Richard] Yeah.

02:14:00.665 --> 02:14:02.880
- We really stress that
the identified data,

02:14:02.880 --> 02:14:05.040
unless absolutely necessary

02:14:05.040 --> 02:14:08.670
and under very specific
permissioning processes

02:14:08.670 --> 02:14:11.190
remains in the electronic patient record.

02:14:11.190 --> 02:14:12.023
- [Richard] Yeah.

02:14:12.023 --> 02:14:12.856
Okay.

02:14:13.800 --> 02:14:15.540
Sorry to interrupt.
- Should I just stop there or?

02:14:15.540 --> 02:14:17.681
- No, no, go, go, go, go.
- [Tim] A couple more.

02:14:17.681 --> 02:14:18.964
I had a couple more.

02:14:18.964 --> 02:14:19.797
- Go on.

02:14:19.797 --> 02:14:20.730
- I got to go on a little bit longer.

02:14:20.730 --> 02:14:25.322
So several people have mentioned
bed management systems.

02:14:25.322 --> 02:14:27.030
So there's a huge opportunity.

02:14:27.030 --> 02:14:29.370
So I'm going to go to your
point about data platforms.

02:14:29.370 --> 02:14:30.203
- [Richard] Yeah.

02:14:30.203 --> 02:14:32.100
- You can put a bed management system,

02:14:32.100 --> 02:14:32.933
and I've installed them

02:14:32.933 --> 02:14:35.130
in several hospitals in the United States,

02:14:35.130 --> 02:14:37.380
on top of an electronic patient record.

02:14:37.380 --> 02:14:38.700
So electronic patient records

02:14:38.700 --> 02:14:41.970
allow you to assign patients to a bed,

02:14:41.970 --> 02:14:44.700
but they do not manage
the flow of patients

02:14:44.700 --> 02:14:46.530
from multiple places.

02:14:46.530 --> 02:14:49.410
That is more like an air
traffic control system.

02:14:49.410 --> 02:14:53.610
You can put that on top of any platform.

02:14:53.610 --> 02:14:55.380
There are multiple software systems

02:14:55.380 --> 02:14:58.709
that do that meta patient flow.

02:14:58.709 --> 02:15:03.060
And I'll just say that there
is a massive opportunity here

02:15:03.060 --> 02:15:06.150
to extend those bed management systems,

02:15:06.150 --> 02:15:08.190
not just for acute hospitals,

02:15:08.190 --> 02:15:10.770
which is the typical
place to install them,

02:15:10.770 --> 02:15:14.940
but across an ICS, and
including social care.

02:15:14.940 --> 02:15:17.700
Now, there are several
experiments around the world

02:15:17.700 --> 02:15:18.791
where that is going on.

02:15:18.791 --> 02:15:22.470
We have a really terrific opportunity here

02:15:22.470 --> 02:15:26.520
to make sure that we are
optimising the use of data

02:15:26.520 --> 02:15:30.360
to improve the delivery
and flow of patients

02:15:30.360 --> 02:15:31.620
through our system

02:15:31.620 --> 02:15:34.800
in a way that maximises
the scarce resources,

02:15:34.800 --> 02:15:36.150
maximises the use.

02:15:36.150 --> 02:15:39.000
- So we capture trust,
intermediate levels,

02:15:39.000 --> 02:15:40.530
which Sarah Jane was talking about,

02:15:40.530 --> 02:15:43.170
actually into community social care,

02:15:43.170 --> 02:15:44.610
domiciliary, residential,

02:15:44.610 --> 02:15:46.350
not domiciliary, residential.

02:15:46.350 --> 02:15:47.183
- Exactly.

02:15:47.183 --> 02:15:48.990
So Amanda and I were at Maidenstone

02:15:48.990 --> 02:15:50.431
just the other day.
- [Richard] Maidstone?

02:15:50.431 --> 02:15:51.264
Yeah.

02:15:51.264 --> 02:15:56.070
- And saw how they've extended
their acute hospital trust

02:15:56.070 --> 02:15:57.630
patient flow system

02:15:57.630 --> 02:16:01.380
into the community by their virtual wards,

02:16:01.380 --> 02:16:03.780
are managed through the same system.

02:16:03.780 --> 02:16:07.290
So it gives them a whole 360 view.

02:16:07.290 --> 02:16:08.123
- [Richard] Yeah.

02:16:08.123 --> 02:16:10.020
- Yeah, of what the opportunities are

02:16:10.020 --> 02:16:13.110
to maximise the benefit of
the delivery of services

02:16:13.110 --> 02:16:14.133
to patients.

02:16:15.090 --> 02:16:16.803
I want to also mention,

02:16:18.390 --> 02:16:21.240
actually, it doesn't get enough play,

02:16:21.240 --> 02:16:25.410
but in primary care we've
been really pushing hard on

02:16:25.410 --> 02:16:27.363
the voice over the internet.

02:16:28.212 --> 02:16:33.000
And the importance of those
types of phone systems

02:16:33.000 --> 02:16:34.890
for primary care is really important.

02:16:34.890 --> 02:16:35.730
And I'm going to highlight,

02:16:35.730 --> 02:16:40.730
I was at the Stourport Surgery
in Herefordshire last week

02:16:41.310 --> 02:16:44.820
and saw a terrific, did
I mispronounce that?

02:16:44.820 --> 02:16:45.840
- [Richard] I think it's Stourport?

02:16:45.840 --> 02:16:46.673
- Stourport?

02:16:46.673 --> 02:16:47.519
Oops, sorry.
- [Richard] Yeah.

02:16:47.519 --> 02:16:48.930
- Keep going.

02:16:48.930 --> 02:16:50.453
We know what you meant.

02:16:50.453 --> 02:16:51.286
- It's not, is it?

02:16:51.286 --> 02:16:53.200
Stourport is in Worcestershire?

02:16:53.200 --> 02:16:54.033
Warwickshire?

02:16:54.033 --> 02:16:55.025
- Yeah.
- Warwickshire.

02:16:55.025 --> 02:16:55.953
- So-

02:16:55.953 --> 02:16:57.393
- Have you got a passport?

02:17:00.660 --> 02:17:03.180
- So what was brilliant
about this practise

02:17:03.180 --> 02:17:06.626
is the phone operators

02:17:06.626 --> 02:17:11.190
had been centralised
across five practises.

02:17:11.190 --> 02:17:13.830
They were working with a big screen

02:17:13.830 --> 02:17:18.830
on these internet-based phone
systems that have queuing,

02:17:19.080 --> 02:17:21.150
and allow, in real time,

02:17:21.150 --> 02:17:24.960
those operators to see what
the longest person waiting is.

02:17:24.960 --> 02:17:28.470
They were averaging under
one and a half minutes

02:17:28.470 --> 02:17:30.390
for the longest wait
time to answer the phone

02:17:30.390 --> 02:17:33.480
in this very, very busy
primary care practise.

02:17:33.480 --> 02:17:38.480
And the were able to manage that across

02:17:39.150 --> 02:17:43.466
both the normal business hours
and extended business hours.

02:17:43.466 --> 02:17:47.220
I mean, they were really
using the power of technology

02:17:47.220 --> 02:17:50.970
to optimise the use of the
delivery of their services.

02:17:50.970 --> 02:17:53.826
And it is part of our
plan to make sure that

02:17:53.826 --> 02:17:58.826
100% of practises are on
internet-based phone services.

02:17:59.400 --> 02:18:00.233
- By when?

02:18:01.320 --> 02:18:02.153
- [Tim] Excuse me?

02:18:02.153 --> 02:18:03.573
- Oh, don't worry.

02:18:03.573 --> 02:18:06.990
- That will be announced as part of the-

02:18:06.990 --> 02:18:08.490
- [Richard] Oh, sorry.

02:18:08.490 --> 02:18:10.290
- Primary care strategy.

02:18:10.290 --> 02:18:12.660
- Fine, okay.
- [Tim] Coming shortly.

02:18:12.660 --> 02:18:13.493
- Excellent.

02:18:15.065 --> 02:18:17.820
- And then I wanted to
close with the NHS app,

02:18:17.820 --> 02:18:19.413
which Amanda, again-
- [Richard] Yeah, yeah.

02:18:19.413 --> 02:18:21.903
- Mentioned at the beginning.

02:18:22.740 --> 02:18:27.740
So there are currently 31
million users registered,

02:18:27.870 --> 02:18:32.316
and right now we are
providing, as Kathy said,

02:18:32.316 --> 02:18:36.586
2.1 million prescriptions a
month through that system.

02:18:36.586 --> 02:18:37.620
- [Richard] Yeah.

02:18:37.620 --> 02:18:41.790
- There are 300,000 primary
care appointments made.

02:18:41.790 --> 02:18:45.030
And it's not just the app,
it's also the web-based.

02:18:45.030 --> 02:18:48.153
I have to say that this is
digital channels generally.

02:18:49.230 --> 02:18:54.230
And 3 million hours of
GP practise time saved

02:18:55.800 --> 02:18:59.970
through the use of internet-based services

02:18:59.970 --> 02:19:03.060
that are currently delivered
through digital channels.

02:19:03.060 --> 02:19:06.000
So having said all that,

02:19:06.000 --> 02:19:08.130
there is so much more potential.

02:19:08.130 --> 02:19:10.620
- [Richard] Yeah, it must be.
- For the NHS app.

02:19:10.620 --> 02:19:14.730
And so access to records,

02:19:14.730 --> 02:19:19.470
so I'm able to see 100% of
my and my family's records

02:19:19.470 --> 02:19:22.170
on my phone at any time I want.

02:19:22.170 --> 02:19:23.970
I think every citizen in this country

02:19:23.970 --> 02:19:26.906
should have that opportunity
to see their records-

02:19:26.906 --> 02:19:27.930
- In terms of the appointments,

02:19:27.930 --> 02:19:30.600
how fast will the appointments scale?

02:19:30.600 --> 02:19:31.500
Making appointments?

02:19:31.500 --> 02:19:35.190
Because 345,000, I know
that's in the year,

02:19:35.190 --> 02:19:39.510
but we do, what, 350
million GP appointments?

02:19:39.510 --> 02:19:40.343
- [Tim] Yes.
- Yeah,

02:19:40.343 --> 02:19:41.176
so you're doing a thousandth-

02:19:41.176 --> 02:19:42.009
- [Tim] Yes.

02:19:42.009 --> 02:19:43.680
- Of appointments are going through.

02:19:43.680 --> 02:19:44.973
- [Tim] It's a big opportunity.

02:19:44.973 --> 02:19:46.650
- And will it scale fast, do you think?

02:19:46.650 --> 02:19:48.480
What scales it?

02:19:48.480 --> 02:19:52.080
I mean, what drives the
patients or citizens

02:19:52.080 --> 02:19:54.540
to go that route?

02:19:54.540 --> 02:19:55.830
Are we advertising it enough?

02:19:55.830 --> 02:19:56.663
- Well, no.

02:19:56.663 --> 02:20:01.190
So, the technology is
present in the app to do it,

02:20:03.270 --> 02:20:04.103
but it's all the-

02:20:04.103 --> 02:20:05.292
- [Richard] You got to connect.
- The back-end connection.

02:20:05.292 --> 02:20:06.125
- [Richard] Okay.

02:20:06.125 --> 02:20:11.125
- And the size and scale of the NHS

02:20:12.985 --> 02:20:17.985
and the many, many instances
of different systems,

02:20:18.180 --> 02:20:20.610
scheduling systems, creates.

02:20:20.610 --> 02:20:23.940
So even as we are trying to
converge on fewer systems,

02:20:23.940 --> 02:20:26.610
that's the managed convergence approach,

02:20:26.610 --> 02:20:28.560
we are at the same time,

02:20:28.560 --> 02:20:31.740
connecting as many of
those as quickly as we can

02:20:31.740 --> 02:20:35.970
to enable this kind of
appointment functions.

02:20:35.970 --> 02:20:36.803
- [Richard] Okay.

02:20:36.803 --> 02:20:41.730
- And we expect some more
announcements about that shortly.

02:20:41.730 --> 02:20:44.820
- So, I'm going to pause and
we're going to run out of time.

02:20:44.820 --> 02:20:46.826
- [Tim] Yep.
- But I can see some hands up.

02:20:46.826 --> 02:20:47.910
It's sort of like being
an auctioneer, actually,

02:20:47.910 --> 02:20:49.066
looking who's bidding.

02:20:49.066 --> 02:20:50.700
But I think Ruth, you
want to bid, I think, so.

02:20:50.700 --> 02:20:51.540
- I do, indeed.

02:20:51.540 --> 02:20:52.772
So, thank you.

02:20:52.772 --> 02:20:53.940
And, Tim, this is really exciting stuff.

02:20:53.940 --> 02:20:57.390
Of course, the shared care
records is with women as well

02:20:57.390 --> 02:20:58.520
when they're pregnant,

02:20:58.520 --> 02:21:00.540
so it's not just between clinicians.

02:21:00.540 --> 02:21:01.680
- No one is excluded.

02:21:01.680 --> 02:21:02.880
- Exactly, just wanted to say.

02:21:02.880 --> 02:21:06.630
And whilst maternity and
children and red books

02:21:06.630 --> 02:21:07.770
and not in here,

02:21:07.770 --> 02:21:10.860
I'm assuming it's all
mainstream now into this work?

02:21:10.860 --> 02:21:11.700
- That's exactly right.

02:21:11.700 --> 02:21:14.879
So I've personally looked at
the content of the red book.

02:21:14.879 --> 02:21:15.712
- Yeah.

02:21:15.712 --> 02:21:17.730
- And the content of the red book,

02:21:17.730 --> 02:21:19.830
I can see for my kids,

02:21:19.830 --> 02:21:22.000
or, actually, they're no longer minors.

02:21:22.000 --> 02:21:25.350
Yeah, so I actually
can't see them anymore,

02:21:25.350 --> 02:21:26.550
but I could see them.

02:21:26.550 --> 02:21:29.370
All of that content was available through.

02:21:29.370 --> 02:21:32.762
And so yes, that is exactly the plan.

02:21:32.762 --> 02:21:33.595
- Perfect.

02:21:33.595 --> 02:21:35.850
- Okay, well, we're getting
the red books on, aren't we?

02:21:35.850 --> 02:21:36.938
- Yes.

02:21:36.938 --> 02:21:37.771
- Which it's a really
important initiative.

02:21:37.771 --> 02:21:38.850
- It is very important.

02:21:38.850 --> 02:21:39.683
- Yeah.

02:21:39.683 --> 02:21:44.683
- It does require that
we deliver proxy access.

02:21:45.210 --> 02:21:46.043
- Yeah.

02:21:46.043 --> 02:21:46.876
- Across the app.

02:21:46.876 --> 02:21:49.590
And that is one of our commitments.

02:21:49.590 --> 02:21:51.719
- So can I just see,

02:21:51.719 --> 02:21:55.440
are there any other comments or questions

02:21:55.440 --> 02:21:57.159
for Tim and for Kathy?

02:21:57.159 --> 02:21:58.037
Can I just say,

02:21:58.037 --> 02:22:02.040
it's a really good insight into

02:22:02.040 --> 02:22:04.140
the amount of work going on in this space,

02:22:04.980 --> 02:22:09.540
and it offers a potentially
very exciting step up, I think,

02:22:09.540 --> 02:22:12.150
in our ability to offer
really good service,

02:22:12.150 --> 02:22:13.323
good patient service.

02:22:14.310 --> 02:22:15.960
If there aren't any other challenges,

02:22:15.960 --> 02:22:17.640
what I would just say,

02:22:17.640 --> 02:22:19.950
and it probably exists
in a different document,

02:22:19.950 --> 02:22:22.230
I'd love to see you paint,

02:22:22.230 --> 02:22:25.380
imagine casting forward
three years or five years.

02:22:25.380 --> 02:22:28.920
Imagine the NHS after this.

02:22:28.920 --> 02:22:30.586
- [Tim] Yeah.
- What would it look like?

02:22:30.586 --> 02:22:31.419
- [Tim] Yeah.

02:22:31.419 --> 02:22:32.252
- So if we're having this conversation

02:22:32.252 --> 02:22:34.890
in three years time or four years time

02:22:34.890 --> 02:22:37.170
and you assume this has landed,

02:22:37.170 --> 02:22:38.003
what do we then do?

02:22:38.003 --> 02:22:39.390
What do we look like?

02:22:39.390 --> 02:22:40.830
And what's the sort of activity,

02:22:40.830 --> 02:22:43.230
how are patients interacting
and what are we then doing?

02:22:43.230 --> 02:22:46.230
But painting that
picture, the vision thing,

02:22:46.230 --> 02:22:49.710
or, "Imagine if," would be
really helpful here, I think.

02:22:49.710 --> 02:22:52.230
- So a down payment on
that that already exists,

02:22:52.230 --> 02:22:54.030
that anyone can get off the web

02:22:54.030 --> 02:22:57.630
is the last page of the Wade-Gery report.

02:22:57.630 --> 02:22:58.662
- [Richard] I've seen that, yeah.

02:22:58.662 --> 02:22:59.756
This circulation-

02:22:59.756 --> 02:23:00.589
- It has a vision for the future.

02:23:00.589 --> 02:23:01.422
- [Richard] Yeah.

02:23:01.422 --> 02:23:02.910
- And that's a down payment.

02:23:02.910 --> 02:23:04.620
We need to expand that vision,

02:23:04.620 --> 02:23:06.060
make it a little bit more specific,

02:23:06.060 --> 02:23:08.310
but it's directionally correct.

02:23:08.310 --> 02:23:10.650
- Because imagine the model of the NHS

02:23:10.650 --> 02:23:11.730
post-implementing this.

02:23:11.730 --> 02:23:13.200
- [Tim] Right.

02:23:13.200 --> 02:23:14.490
- Very good.

02:23:14.490 --> 02:23:15.333
Thank you very much.

02:23:15.333 --> 02:23:16.470
We're going to get just one more item

02:23:16.470 --> 02:23:19.890
which I can deal with, I
hope, relatively quickly,

02:23:19.890 --> 02:23:23.007
which is an update on board governance,

02:23:23.007 --> 02:23:24.570
NHS England board governance.

02:23:24.570 --> 02:23:28.440
And it's the last item on the agenda.

02:23:28.440 --> 02:23:32.491
And it's to note in particular
the establishment of

02:23:32.491 --> 02:23:35.760
two new board committees,
formal board committees.

02:23:35.760 --> 02:23:37.373
We talked about them this morning,

02:23:38.238 --> 02:23:43.197
and they both flow from the
merger with NHS Digital.

02:23:43.197 --> 02:23:44.550
And as we said this morning,

02:23:44.550 --> 02:23:49.140
NHS Digital board was
running an extremely busy,

02:23:49.140 --> 02:23:52.110
interrogative agenda with its executives

02:23:52.110 --> 02:23:56.220
and we've now absorbed that
workload into NHS England,

02:23:56.220 --> 02:23:57.333
into this board.

02:23:58.276 --> 02:24:00.210
And one of the ways we want to make sure

02:24:00.210 --> 02:24:03.450
we manage that smoothly
and keep good focus,

02:24:03.450 --> 02:24:05.830
is, actually, we've established a separate

02:24:07.890 --> 02:24:10.950
proper subsidiary committee of the board,

02:24:10.950 --> 02:24:13.050
which is the Data, Digital
and Technology Committee,

02:24:13.050 --> 02:24:14.400
which is going, essentially, to make sure

02:24:14.400 --> 02:24:17.490
we keep the same levels of focus on

02:24:17.490 --> 02:24:20.697
the operations of Digital as
we absorb it and we learn it

02:24:20.697 --> 02:24:23.220
and we think what to focus upon.

02:24:23.220 --> 02:24:25.470
And then from that oversight subcommittee

02:24:25.470 --> 02:24:28.710
we'll bring up to the NHS
England board particular items.

02:24:28.710 --> 02:24:30.090
And again, we talked to
the board this morning,

02:24:30.090 --> 02:24:30.960
the private board.

02:24:30.960 --> 02:24:33.420
So what are the types of areas
that we should expect to see

02:24:33.420 --> 02:24:34.770
in our normal reporting?

02:24:34.770 --> 02:24:37.110
But just in terms of
managing the governance

02:24:37.110 --> 02:24:41.301
as digital has come into England,

02:24:41.301 --> 02:24:42.436
we are setting up that

02:24:42.436 --> 02:24:45.330
Data, Digital and Technology Committee.

02:24:45.330 --> 02:24:46.980
And you can see the
background, we describe it,

02:24:46.980 --> 02:24:48.930
I think it's going to
meet six times a year.

02:24:48.930 --> 02:24:51.120
I said five this morning,
it's six times a year,

02:24:51.120 --> 02:24:53.523
alternating months between boards.

02:24:54.690 --> 02:24:55.523
Separately,

02:24:55.523 --> 02:24:58.890
we've also established a separate
cyber security committee.

02:24:58.890 --> 02:25:00.600
We've put that under the
Audit and Risk Committee.

02:25:00.600 --> 02:25:03.810
It's a really important area
of defence and protection

02:25:03.810 --> 02:25:07.650
where proactively we need to
think about our cyber defences.

02:25:07.650 --> 02:25:09.840
And so, actually, we
didn't want to lose that.

02:25:09.840 --> 02:25:13.740
And we have put it as a specific

02:25:13.740 --> 02:25:15.870
subject matter expert committee

02:25:15.870 --> 02:25:19.020
reporting into the Audit
and Risk Committee.

02:25:19.020 --> 02:25:20.160
What's also been helpful is

02:25:20.160 --> 02:25:24.240
we have had join us as
associate members of

02:25:24.240 --> 02:25:28.770
the Data, Digital and
Technology Committee,

02:25:28.770 --> 02:25:32.070
three of the non-executives
who were on the Digital board,

02:25:32.070 --> 02:25:35.460
and that will provide us
with consistent parallel run,

02:25:35.460 --> 02:25:40.260
and we don't lose the
knowledge base that they had.

02:25:40.260 --> 02:25:44.220
And they have joined us,
which I'm delighted about.

02:25:44.220 --> 02:25:49.220
So that's how we're dealing
with the merger with Digital.

02:25:49.500 --> 02:25:51.930
The paper also sets out
the current committees

02:25:51.930 --> 02:25:53.010
that we run.

02:25:53.010 --> 02:25:55.996
I think sometimes observers
wonder what the board does.

02:25:55.996 --> 02:25:58.470
I mean, they do this,
they do a private board,

02:25:58.470 --> 02:26:02.315
but also, and I thank the
executives and the non-executives,

02:26:02.315 --> 02:26:04.770
you provide very significant input

02:26:04.770 --> 02:26:06.930
in a whole range of different committees

02:26:06.930 --> 02:26:11.727
overseeing and challenging
all aspects of NHS England.

02:26:11.727 --> 02:26:14.100
And so you see those with regard to

02:26:14.100 --> 02:26:15.900
the normal Audit and Risk Committee,

02:26:15.900 --> 02:26:17.550
there's a Cyber Committee,

02:26:17.550 --> 02:26:19.110
Data, Digital and Technology,

02:26:19.110 --> 02:26:20.460
there's a Quality Committee,

02:26:20.460 --> 02:26:23.310
which used to be called
Patient Safety Committee,

02:26:23.310 --> 02:26:24.960
now called Quality Committee,

02:26:24.960 --> 02:26:27.570
chaired by Munir that
meets five times a year.

02:26:27.570 --> 02:26:29.970
We've got the people in
the Remuneration Committee,

02:26:29.970 --> 02:26:30.870
which David chairs,

02:26:30.870 --> 02:26:32.250
and there's a Nominations Group

02:26:32.250 --> 02:26:34.890
which looks at succession planning.

02:26:34.890 --> 02:26:35.740
Very important.

02:26:35.740 --> 02:26:38.610
And then we've got two other committees.

02:26:38.610 --> 02:26:40.740
One is called Time Limited,

02:26:40.740 --> 02:26:42.840
which is a new NHS England committee,

02:26:42.840 --> 02:26:44.820
chaired, as we know, by Wol Kolade,

02:26:44.820 --> 02:26:48.000
which is overseeing the
merger of the entities

02:26:48.000 --> 02:26:52.560
and the 30 to 40% reduction in workforce

02:26:52.560 --> 02:26:55.650
and the redesigning of NHS England,

02:26:55.650 --> 02:26:57.780
maintaining its expertise,

02:26:57.780 --> 02:27:00.702
but essentially making
it somewhat smaller.

02:27:00.702 --> 02:27:03.570
And then also there's a
separate CFO advisory group

02:27:03.570 --> 02:27:06.480
which acts in support of Julian

02:27:06.480 --> 02:27:11.040
and all of the areas of
responsibility that he has.

02:27:11.040 --> 02:27:13.170
So that's the governance you can see

02:27:13.170 --> 02:27:15.630
set out in the appendices of the annexes.

02:27:15.630 --> 02:27:17.850
You can see these are
the broad areas we cover.

02:27:17.850 --> 02:27:20.400
And you can also see the
matters reserved to the board

02:27:20.400 --> 02:27:22.200
and actually what the methods of assurance

02:27:22.200 --> 02:27:23.580
to the broader board are.

02:27:23.580 --> 02:27:25.473
If it sounds a bit dry as a topic, it is,

02:27:25.473 --> 02:27:28.290
but it's also very
important that we understand

02:27:28.290 --> 02:27:30.120
exactly what our responsibilities are

02:27:30.120 --> 02:27:34.166
and that we are managing
those appropriately.

02:27:34.166 --> 02:27:39.113
When we come, as David
knows, to the merger with HEE

02:27:39.113 --> 02:27:42.060
at the end of March,

02:27:42.060 --> 02:27:44.760
very much the same model we intend to run

02:27:44.760 --> 02:27:45.840
as we've run with digital boards.

02:27:45.840 --> 02:27:48.210
There's HEE running a huge agenda-

02:27:48.210 --> 02:27:49.830
- Mm-hmm.
- As a separate board.

02:27:49.830 --> 02:27:52.020
It's going to get
absorbed into NHS England

02:27:52.020 --> 02:27:54.300
and we need to make sure
we keep good oversight

02:27:54.300 --> 02:27:56.580
and continuity of knowledge.

02:27:56.580 --> 02:27:59.220
So we are going to think
we've got the same idea of

02:27:59.220 --> 02:28:03.120
a separate subsidiary
committee of the board,

02:28:03.120 --> 02:28:05.670
which will manage and keep discipline

02:28:05.670 --> 02:28:07.890
and challenge over that workload.

02:28:07.890 --> 02:28:09.570
And again, David is talking to

02:28:09.570 --> 02:28:13.140
a number of his board
colleagues on that board

02:28:13.140 --> 02:28:15.960
to ask them if they'd also
serve as associate members

02:28:15.960 --> 02:28:17.340
on that subcommittee,

02:28:17.340 --> 02:28:19.350
which I think is a very
sensible way to think about

02:28:19.350 --> 02:28:21.330
absorbing and merging these entities

02:28:21.330 --> 02:28:22.650
and making sure that we can keep

02:28:22.650 --> 02:28:26.070
continuity of knowledge and oversight.

02:28:26.070 --> 02:28:27.660
So that's an update on governance

02:28:27.660 --> 02:28:29.010
as we see it at the moment.

02:28:30.284 --> 02:28:32.700
Happy to take any comments or questions,

02:28:32.700 --> 02:28:33.810
but straightforward.

02:28:33.810 --> 02:28:35.130
And thank you to colleagues, actually,

02:28:35.130 --> 02:28:38.520
for your help and guidance
in how we've structured it.

02:28:38.520 --> 02:28:40.950
So I think that's covered all the items.

02:28:40.950 --> 02:28:42.210
Let me just see if there's
any other business.

02:28:42.210 --> 02:28:45.270
Let's just look around the
table, any other business?

02:28:45.270 --> 02:28:46.110
And if not, Kathy,

02:28:46.110 --> 02:28:47.367
I forgot to say thank you very much

02:28:47.367 --> 02:28:48.900
for that very good paper,

02:28:48.900 --> 02:28:49.733
which it was.

02:28:49.733 --> 02:28:50.566
And Tim, thank you.

02:28:50.566 --> 02:28:51.540
That was excellent.

02:28:51.540 --> 02:28:53.084
And thank you to colleagues.

02:28:53.084 --> 02:28:55.006
I'll call the board to close.

02:28:55.006 --> 02:28:55.863
Thank you.

